      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <p>AN OVER PRODUCTION OF POISON.</p>
        <p>Some people produce uric-acid twice as fast as others, and an overload of this poison is a serious thing for anyone. ,It comes in different ways, but the most productive causes are over- exertion and eating too mUeli, partifularly of meat. Some allowance should be made to those suffering from Uric-acid complaints, for they can't help being nervous, morose, cross, sus- picious, headachy, dizzy, at times; and racked with all sorts of queer pains. The rheumatic sufferer knows whal real pain</p>
        <p>_ is like, and it is no wonder he grows old too fast—and sooner or later develops heart trouble, sciatica, gravel, dropsy or hardening of the arteries.</p>
        <p>The sensible thing to do is to take warning at the first sign that uric-acid is developing—eat less meat, and not too much of any food. Drink plenty of both milk and water. Take regular exercise, Eind get eight hours sleep seven nights a week. Use Doan's Backache Kidney Pills to repair the weakened kidneys and help them filter uric- acid from the blood. You can't be really well while an excess of this poison remains in the system. , There is scarcely a man or woman in the town who has not a good word for Doan's Backache Kidney Pills, the special kidney medicine that does not claim to cure every- thing. Of all dealers, or 2/9 a box, from Foster- McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford-street, London, W.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>RECHABITES SAY</head>
        <p>RECHABITES SAY</p>
        <p>Nationalisation Of Drink Traffic Would Strengthen Enemy.</p>
        <p>The quarterly meeting of the Warrington District No. 4) of the above Order was held in the Lecture Hall of the Congregational Church, Ashton-in-Makerfield. There was a very repre- sentative gathering, presided over by Bro. J. Gaskell, District Chief Ruler. Reports were presented showing that, as all over the country, the Order is making very considerable progress. In the course of the report presented by the Secretary it was shown that over two hundred members of the district bad enlisted, many of them being on active service. Nine have been killed in action. Bro. S. Fernley, D.S., presented his report for the past quarter, which showed the numeri- cal strength of the District, Order and State to be .1,244 members—a good increase in both Order and State Sections. _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>Bro. Briscoe, D,S.J.T"., presented his report of the work done in the Juvenile Tents, which was of a most satisfactory character, and was pleased to state that quite a number of the juvenile meMbers had been succeessful in ob- taining prizes in the Order Temperance Knew- ledge Competition.</p>
        <p>Warrington Next. " Charity " Tent, Warrington, was selected as the next place for the District Meeting. _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>At the Close of the business</p>
        <p>Campbell, High Secretary of the Order; replying to a member who asked what attitude the Order, as represented by its leaders, would take in respect to the nationalisation of the drink traffic, stated that in his opinjon from every point of view the Order ought to resist any such legislation. An enemy which we al- ready found to be of almost supreme strength and force would be very materially strength- ened by any such change, as all those persons, in whatever capacity engaged, would., to some degree, regard themselves and be regarded as engaged in Civil Service. This would result in the establishment of our common enemy into a very much strengthened position. A.t the conclusion of business the company did full justice to a first-class tea, which had been prepared by Mrs. Corless, Mrs. Winstan- ley and the Miss,es Winstanley, who were warmly thanked for their services in a resolu- tion proposed by Bro. W. Lowe, of Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>Presentation to Successful Secretary.</head>
        <p>Presentation to Successful Secretary.</p>
        <p>To make a recognition of the services ren- dered to the district by Bro. T. Winstaaley, the representatives and friends reassembled ill the Lecture Hall, when the District Chief Ruler was supported on the platform by Bros. A. Tomlinson, M. 8., D.S., and R. Campbell, H.S. Bro. Gaskell explained that they were met to do honour to one who had rendered signal service to the cause of Rechabitism in the. area of the Warrington District. Great success had attended the work during the sixteen years in which Bro. Winstanley had been the guiding and directing personality in the district.. Bro. Winstanley was a man of great powers and great resource, a man who could organise; a man of initiative and enthusiasm, and it was a matter of great regret that being in failing health and over-worked, both in his business and by the extension of the Order, he was unable to cope,-with the work. He had plea- sure, as Chief Ruler of the district, in present- ing to Bro. Winstanley a handsome geld watch, bearing the insCription : "Warrington No. ,4 District, 1.0. R. Presented to Bro. Thomas Winstanley by the members of the above as a mark of great respect, and in recognition of his sixteen years' service as District Secretary, and the invaluable work which he has done for the cause of Rechabitism. October 30th, 1915." Be also asked Mrs. Winstanley to accept at the hands of the district a diamond and gold brooch in recognition of the services that she herself had rendered, and, what was of more import- ance, had made it possible for her husband to render. Whilst the men were able to do much useful and valuable work in Rechabite and kindred causes, no small share of the success was due to the self-sacrifice and co-operation -of the wives. - -</p>
        <p>Bro. James Shaw, District,Trustee, the oldest member on he executive, in a felicitous and reminiscent manner, supported Bro. Gaskell. • Bro. Edmund Walkden spoke of Bro. Win.: stanley's abilities and the permanent value of the work which he had clone for the Order. During the period Bro. Winstonley had served the district the membership had been raised from 710 to almost 2,000. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Bro. Campbell, High Secretary, paid high compliments to Bro. Winstanley for the faith- ful, painstaking, prompt and invariably accu- rate manner in which be had performed the duties devolving upon his office. Having ex- perience, personal and otherwise, with all the District Secretaries in the Order, he had no hesitation in placing Bro. Winstanley in the first rank, and would always remember with pleasure the pleasant dealings they had had together.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>Difftculties To Overcome.</head>
        <p>Difftculties To Overcome.</p>
        <p>Bro. Winstanley, on rising to respond, was received in a manner which showed the mea- sure of esteem in which he is held. He thanked them for the gifts, the warm expressions which accompanied them, and the appreciation of the cervices that he had been privileged to render. He recognised that not one word had been said in exaggeration of the part which his wife had played in his work, as she was heart and ,soul in Rechabite and Temperance work. He desired to take this opportunity of thanking all his colleagues for their loyal slipport and help during the years he had been their servant, and he asked that the same spirit and support might be shown to his successor. "We have had difficulties," he added; "we have difficulties. We have got over them in the past, and I believe we shall get over them in the. future; but let us .face them In the right spirit with a determination that our Order shall succeed and extend in a greater measure in the future .than ever it has done in the uast. Whatever, services I can. render to the cause of the district and the Order, and parti- cularly in the Temperance side of our work, will always be willingly given. Whatever. I can do for Warrington District I will gladly do."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
