      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, S ATURDA 1, NOVEMBER 13, 1915.</p>
        <p>jioroughly equipped and developed in his mental qualities. The proposition was then.put to the meeting, and the Council signified their unanimous approval in no unmistakable manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>THRICE MAYOR OF WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>THRICE MAYOR OF WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Alderman Pet.,:c. Peacock's iheanit.lk to 6,vic Chair.</p>
        <p>,,...•,:,, f:?!.;,.; -,- e,., I do it iosli</p>
        <p>FELICITATIONS AND HOPES.</p>
        <p>For the third year in succession Alderman Peter Peacock has been elected Mayor of the ancient and historic Borough of Warrington. His reelection took .-plaee at a special meeting of the Town Council, held in the Town Hall on. Tuesday afternoon, and was characterised byi ,complete unanimity—a graceful tribute to, a man • whose worth and work have won the; respect of all parties in the Council Chamber.' • In addition to Alderman • Peacock himself, the following members of the 'Council were: present :—The Deputy-Mayor (Dr. Joseph), Aldermen Bolton,. Jan Evans, -Tinnion; James' Evans, Pierpoint,•Smethorst and Bennett; and Councillors Broadhurst, Jenkins, Wilson,i Gough, . Craik, Skelland, - Halliwell, Barton,. Firth Lindley,. Pemberton, Chrimes, Chantler and Archer; together with the following: Mr.; I. Lyon Whittle (town clerk), Mr. A. M. Ker (borough surveyor),'. Mr.; J. Moore Murray (director. of ediwatitm), Dr. G. W. N. Joseph (medical officer. of health), Mr. M, A. Piereey ' (deputy borough sonveyor), Mr: Rani Fairhurst (borough treasurer), and Mr. 'Martin NiCholls (chief constable). The ceremony of re-election was presided over by the Deputy-Mayor, and the speeches dental thereto were listened to by the Mayoress. (Mrs. P. Peacock), Mrs. Mee, and other ladies,: who were accommodated with seats beside the Mayoral dais.</p>
        <p>those duties that were necessary for safeguard- ing the health of the town, the comfort of the inhabitants, the education of the children, and so on. Every othr matter during the nett twelve months would 'only be of secondary im7, portance. The work of the Mayor during the .Past year had been of a difficult and most im- portant:character, and he (the speaker) won- dered whether the Council realised the very great ,importance that would be attached .by the military authorities, during the _coming</p>
        <p>months, to what the Mayor and the Corpora- tion could ,do. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>REWARD FOR EXCEPTIONAL SERVICES.</head>
        <p>REWARD FOR EXCEPTIONAL SERVICES.</p>
        <p>The re-election of Alderman Peter Peacoc'k. was proposed by Alderman 13sNNErr, Who said: "When a Mayor in Warrington is asked to stand for a third year, it is either as a reward for exceptional service or because the time is one of exceptional iriterest, 'importance, .or responsibility. Each of tlfese conditions has been fulfilled on the present occasion. lam old enough to recollect a long seiies occuparits:of the civic chair, but I cannot think of one who has worked harder or more efficiently than Alderman Peacock. Happily for us,• circum- stances have made him master iff'his own time, and he has given it unStinteilly for the public good, and left us such a tale , of strenuous achievement as few can hope to equal: and I doubt if any can surpass. (ApPlause.) 'More- over, he has brought to his high office not</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>ONLY OBJECT</head>
        <p>THE ONLY OBJECT</p>
        <p>The only object that every true, loyal Brit- isher had at heart was to end the preseat terrible war. (Hear, hear.) Warrington had been very well pleased, he was going to say blessed, with a very efficient, Mayor, and that was something to be very thankful for in times like the present. (Hear, ,hear.) The town had also been exceedingly fortunate in the class of public officials which he had;' indeed, he ventured to think that 'there was no town in England where the AiffiCials had more fully grasped the importance of their duties, and discharged such duties so well. In other places there were very great -complaints about the allowances to the .dependents of soldiers. We had nothing of that kind in Warrington; there was a man at the head of that ,department who had done his, .duty well. (Hear, hear.) In the Borough Treasurer they also had a man who had iisen'to the' occasion with regard to the various war 'funds, while the Town Clerk and the Deputy 'Town Clerk had done their duty exceedingly well. (Hear, hear.) ".Done exceedingly well."--Ahat was Warr,ington's character, and 'it was to be lived up to for another twelve months by Alderman Peter. Peacock, who, as -Mayor of the 'borough, had given . his time ungradgingly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>SACRIFICES FOR 'ALL.</head>
        <p>SACRIFICES FOR 'ALL.</p>
        <p>Some people were louChed more than others by the war, but it was up to them all to make whatever sacrifiCe night be demanded of them, and to make it ungrudgingly. "He never felt his duty as a member Of the Council more than he did at the present time; he never felt more called upon to do all he could; yea to try to do more than he could, to assist his</p>
        <p>.. - --,, --~ ~ . --------- . • , - .1 , a,.. , • ,- -- ......--..', ...: . .:- , 1.'... .:,; . , . .., , , , •,.. , , , .. • . .. .. ..,. ,•.. . . . . . .. : .. , . ~... .... ~ , ~ ... i ..,...:•:;:."::.::.•.,;.,....7i.i.::::•:.:....:i......:..-:...:::::::.,.... .. ~ . ' . • . -.•••:-."•:::::::•-•,-,i'',:,?,:i:,:'--;•••::::::.:'',...:-::,•::.-.......:.,i;,i'::::::.i:,,iliiii::iiiigt,:,:i!!iii:.:ii:.:•::•..........:i:......,.:::::.::::•••':::'....iii:. . ..,,.• • c............ • -.... • ••••••• ' ' ..X. :...., .., •'. • :•:..x..........:.,,.....- .; . •-. ~: :. :....,..: .- . ... ...... , . • .. •.. .• • .• • ,• - ... . ... .. .......... , . . . --,............................,,, .- • .. •• .• .• .., ... .. .............. • • ............ .............,................... ......... ... . • -.•...., ~., ..............,..... .. , • . . -...., .. -..... . • ........... , .............w.,... ... . •. • % • ' •' "' • • '-' - • ..-.-....................... ~.. . ......,. ...... ....::::::::.,.:,.::::...,:::ii*i.....,.:.„ , , - • ' - .. .::::::;..ii:.:.::::;.:,,X:i.,...:•....•:i...•i:.••::.:.'.::::.....„4,.::,'..-.'.:;-i..:...,,-.....::.-...,.•....:,......:•,..1 ' •:•.:::...........:::::.!'.:"''' .•:;::-.,:•:.i.i.:.j.Qiii::.::•:::. . .• ~.. . . •-....:,,,,,,,:::.:,,,„„.„:„.„.„,..„,...::.::::•:,,,„„,,,,..,::,,:::.::„:.:.-•,:„•:.:::::..-.:•-::..„:::.::.:, 1 - ....,....-.„.:..4.:,,...:::::::::„.,-. --. . .. . ~„:„:„.„.........,,,..„.--•.:::::::.:::...„.„:„„„.:,•:•:.,-..,,,,,,,,,,:,....„:„:.--:::::-....:•:,-,:•:„, 1::-...•..... -.- -.....----.::::.:,:::,....,..-•.„,'...:::;:i;,.---..:.:. . -..... ~.. -- ~.,: . , .--:::::::::..„,,:::::,•:::::::-,::,:,1,i.,:,,,ii,.....---1,..i.N..,:„ii:.:„..-t„,-.-.......•.:....::::::,....,-.„.,.....,: ~ ... . &gt;:::..:-....:„.„..-:-:•:.::.,„..-:::,,,w,„ . —. , . .. . ..' ...:.:::;:,,,,,,,,..„::.--.-...-..-.......- .:.::::-..........:,:.,,,,,,, , •,.. . ......,,,„....,„:::.„,„„.,:.::::::::„:„.,::::„. , . ..'. ...............:.,„.„,„„.„....,,,,,,-. ,--. ::-::,:„.-:,,.:.:::,,::::.„,:: ~, . . .:.,..,...„-....::„.:„.„:„„,„,„:.....:.,::„..... , .:. .:..::.::.:,•:::::::::,.:::.......„.,:„,..„.:,„,,..:...... , ..............„.„,„•„„,. .. ....................•:„:„........„.„....„. . ' . ..::::........„..,::,...,.,,„4„„„„:„..:-..::,:,:.:,.:,....:......... . ...... - : ... .:. ..,..:........:„..,...:.....,..„ ........ , . ...„„,„::::,:::,...:),..,:.„„,„.:„:„....„:„,.:::..::.::,::.,:...:.::„..„,..„. ....:.. ...... :::...„. :,..y....-•:.. •• .....:::::„,,,„..,,„„.„.....„„„,,•:„::::.::.::::„..::.:,:::„.,.,,.:..„.:.......:.:.....:,,,:„,„„„,::: • . ~. ....,„:.:„.,,.....:„:„..„....,,.,,.:•..„.„:„...•.....,,...:.::,:::•:...............,.........„•„::::::,„..:„.:. ........... ..............,......: . . - ..... .• „. .. 1:::::.t.:;,:.!-....-.,;-:.:'::::!.......::::,:::,...;,.,i--,;::.......::..1.:-.....i.:1,::•ii,:..--..,--.-:::..„--:::,..,:.0;-:.. :-...,.E.1 , .. .....:,....'............-...•:„:„......„:„,-.::::::::::::,,,,..„.,,,,,,..,-i.,,,,,g,:,,.. - ...::.,..,...:::;.:.:-.....:-.-• • :............:::,„iiii,,,,,,-goil,-,!7,„:.,t,..,f:,,,...*:::-..-:„...-:„.: . : ~...........,...•.:::::„:„.„,,,,,,.:.„.„..„,::::,„:::::„„ : . ~.„,...:.,..„....- ...,,,,::,„,:-...:. ... . .. ...:.:::,.:::„:„.....,:::„:„::,.........,,,.:.,; -..--.....„..., . ...............;„„:„.••••••„:„:.,.• . „„......., ....,-„„,„„,.„,::.::,..,.:::.:...::.:...:.„,„„›,,,:. .......„,:..;,..,....„.„ .. .... .. .........•....:•....::„.„.....::::-._ :::. -7;... . !......:•••:•:::::::,:::::•'!''''",-;•,-,... '.:::::::N, •,• :::... :::;.::::::;:: • , ••• • . .--.'s•-•::::.::::,•::-:::. ,''''. ....::-.-i:',,,1::::.::.'.:••••1'.... : :Ii::::i.:: . ..................,... ....... . . .•... •••••• • • ..• • .... • .... . • . .... ........ ....., .... . . . • : • . . . . .: . . 'X.... . . • • . ,___,- • . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>MAYOR AND MAYORESS FOR A THIRD YEAR.</head>
        <p>MAYOR AND MAYORESS FOR A THIRD YEAR.</p>
        <p>merely that personal urbanity which is one of his most striking characteristics, but a broad common-sense and a disinclination to be bound by mere tradition which cannot be too highly praised.</p>
        <p>couptry to bring an end to the present terrilile war. Whilst the soldier lads were doing their duty so nobly at the Front; he hoped that, led so well by their Mayor, they at homoir Warrington would also do their duty, for ultimate success depended upon every man doing his duty well and truly in whatever sphere of life ho.. might ,move—whether it was, his lot to fight at the Front, or Whether it was his lot to reriniin at home and help to .carry on the business pf the country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>SERIOUS EVENTS AND GREAT RESPONSIBILITIES.</head>
        <p>SERIOUS EVENTS AND GREAT RESPONSIBILITIES.</p>
        <p>• "And, if Alderman, Peacock's services have been of an exceptional character, surely never Mayor in all the history of the town has had to face events so serious or responsibilities so grent. Happily the ,Bridge has been completed during his term of office, and the broadening of Butterniarket-street has reached its latest phase in the removal of the Old Fox Inn. These alone are events which would suffice to make his Mayoralty memorable. But all our usual municipal activities have been overshadowed by this earth-shaking war; our dreams of municipal improvement are in abeyance, and our one palpable and imperative duty is to see that Warrington plays its' part in this great fight for England and for freedom. The Mayor has fanned the flame of local patriotism, and, in a hundred ways, contributed to the spirit which is essential to success. (Applause,) Some day, in the not too distant future, we shall, I hope, be able to resume our normal activities, and much, indeed, remains to be done. In the meantime, we must stand as one man behind his Worship, the voice of Strife must be com- pletely hushed, our little quarrels must be all forgotten, and, led by the Mayor; I hope that Warrington will play a worthy part in .this great crisis to the Very end. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>HIS WORSHIP'S SOLDIER SON.</head>
        <p>HIS WORSHIP'S SOLDIER SON.</p>
        <p>"May strength be given him for his high task, and, before his year of office closes, may the soldier son whom he has given to his country come back safe and sound, ancl a last- ing' peace reward the unexampled sufferings of the gallant lads who are dyinc, for us and for England -even while I speak. When the Mayor lays down his chain of office, and the war is ended, may he and all of us be able to echo Nelson's dying words, 'Thank God, I have done my duty!' Of the Mayoress it is almost superfluous to speak. I need only say that she has won not merely our respect and our regard, but our affection; and that, in a time of great public stress and domestic anxiety, she has shown unfailing tact and kindness, and played her part</p>
        <p>Like perfect music matched to noble words,</p>
        <p>Meanwhile, Our place is much: as far as in us lies We too will serve them both in servinwher." (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>BLESSED WITH EFFICIENT MAYOR.</head>
        <p>BLESSED WITH EFFICIENT MAYOR.</p>
        <p>Alderman TINNION, -in seconding the resolu- tion, said that he had had an important part in the election of Alderman Peacock as Mayor on the last two occasions, and to-day he played a part in his re-election for .the third time with more genuine pleasure, if possible. than he felt on the two previous occasions. He felt that at the preSent time the duties of the Mayor and the functions of the Town Council had alto- gether changed, and during the next twelve months they would be occupied in a sort of routine work—carrying on the various duties that fell to the lot of the various committees;</p>
        <p>[. op, 4.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>MUST BE NO SHIRKERS.</head>
        <p>MUST BE NO SHIRKERS.</p>
        <p>There must be no shirkers; every man must be true to his country, and then the war would be brought to a•luccessful end, and they. could enjoy again the -blessings of peace and a life worth living, knowing that they had fought for freedom and had achieved their end. "I am convinced that in Alderman Peacock we have got the one 7nan in Warrington., who can fill the position of Mayor, not only . with honour to himself, but with satisfaction to the town." (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>MAYOR'S SACRIFICE.</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR'S SACRIFICE.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON, in supporting the re- election of Alderman Peacock to the Mayor- alty, said that in his experience of the Council, which extended over a great many years, he did not think any gentleman had :filled the, position better than had: the Alderman whom they were about, to re-elect. to the civic chair. (Hear, hear.) Certainly he had. not heard of anybody who had occupied that chair, who had ever made the personal sacrifices that Alderman Peacock had made. "The Circum- stances in which he was made Mayor," the Councillor went on, "are ever before me. Very few men would have accepted the position under such circumstances. He was practically ,n untried man: Many , people thought ,that perhaps he had not had sufficient experience of public life, but he has justified his friends up to the hilt. (Hear, hear.) This Council is not' always an easy one to manage—(laughter) but Alderman Peacock seems to have the happy knack, which is a gift, of getting that obedi- ence from the members that other mayors have perhaps lacked." Alderman Peacock, 'the Councillor continued, was thoroughly honest and earnest and he wished to be fair to every party in the Council Chamber. Moreover, he was tactful, and, under his adroit leadership,, public business had proceeded with a degree of smoothness that used to be foreign to the work of the Council. 1 TOWN GREATLY BENEFITED BY ALDERMAN PEACOCK'S SERVICES. I</p>
        <p>The Mayor was, happily, able to give a great deal of time to the work of the borough, and there could be no doubt that Warrington had benefited greatly by the time that Alderman Peacock had been able to give to its business. (Hear, hear.) Councillor Pemberton went on to say that the people who, perforce, had to remain at home in these stirring days should strain every nerve in the doing of everything that was possible to help the country and the Men ,at the 'Front. The Mayoress, said" the speaker, by way of conclusion, had ably sup- ported her husband in his wOrk, displaying kindness, energy and unselfishness, and giving, without grudge, of her time, with the result that she had gained a place. in • the regard of the people of Warrington, which it would be difficult for anybody else to fill. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>BOTH THE TIME AND THE ABILITY.</head>
        <p>BOTH THE TIME AND THE ABILITY.</p>
        <p>The DEPUTY-MAYOR, endorsing all that had been said about the. Mayor, remarked that the fact that his. Worship could devote' all his time to the duties Peculiarly adapted- him for the position of the civic head ,of the borough at the present grave juncture in national history. "But I will go further," added Dr. Joseph, " and say that it is ,not every, man possessing the time; who has also the ability to discharge the duties so_ efficiently as Alderman. Peacock. The MAYores§ has ably assisted,. him in •every way, and. I know, from mY own experience, the value of having a popular and efficient Mayor- ess." Dr. Joseph also went on to speak of the advantage, all things being equal, of having a comparatively young man as Mayor. Although Alderman Peacock was past military age, he was in the zenith of his bodily powers, and j</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>HIS WORSHIP'S THANKS.</head>
        <p>HIS WORSHIP'S THANKS.</p>
        <p>A 'Call To The Unresponsive " Eligibles."</p>
        <p>The XiAro% who had been absent from the Council Chamber during the speechmaking, was then informed of his re-election, and entered the room preceded by the sword and mace bearers Having made the formal declaration, he said :</p>
        <p>For the honour you have again conferred upon me I thank you; it. has its peculiar plea- sures,. it .has its peculiar responsibilities, and it has only been an extremely strong feeling that those responsibilities at the present time should not be thrown upon a Mayor in his first year that has induced me to accept the office for a third period. Before coming to the main subject which is before us, I should like to thank the people of Warrington and district for the generous spirit in which every worthy appeal to their- charity has been met. The total sum collected for charities with *Moll I have been connected as Mayor amount S to no less than .29,368. (Applause.) I should also like to thank the Press for the splendid ser- vice they, too, have given, not only to Charities, but to every worthy object during the year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>LOCAL INTERESTS OVERSHADOWED BY EMPIRE'S CALL.</head>
        <p>LOCAL INTERESTS OVERSHADOWED BY EMPIRE'S CALL.</p>
        <p>"In previous years it has been the custom, a wise custom, for the Mayor to indicate upon what lines the policy of the town will in all probability be developed during the coming year. To-cloy I merely content myself by say- ing that the civic progress of the town will, as far as possible, go on in the usual way, and that the welfare and interests of our town and townspeople will have, as they always have had, our earnest consideration. But now we are gathered together not merely as townsmen, but as Britons—(applause)—and we have the interests not only of ourselves before us, but the interests of that great Empire which stands before the world to-day as the great champion of honour, liberty, justice, and all those prin- ciples which we as dwellers in this historic ,county of the Red Rose .hold so dear. (Ap-, plause.) , Throughout our vast Empire to-day there is but one stirring call, and that is for all who are eligible for military, service to rally round the standard and at once offer to serve their' King and country. Our men, we are told, have done splendidly, and though I be- lieve to-day that the Empire of Germany is tottering to its fall, the price of victory is heavyj. and only •by our young men coming ,forward, and at once, so as to be ready to .reinforce those who have gone earlier into the fighting line, can victory be assured.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>HAVE THEY/DIED IN VAIN?</head>
        <p>HAVE THEY/DIED IN VAIN?</p>
        <p>Many Warringtonians have gone, and up- wards of 150 of them will return to the good old town no more. Have they died in vain? Are there not other Warringtonians to fill the places- they have left? I speak here to-day in the presence of you gentlemen and as your :representative; nay, more. I speak as the re- presentative, in a humble degree, of our gracious King: but in neither of these capaci- ties. do' I appeal to the young men of War- rington. I appeal to them as a man—a fe,ther, and, like many other fathers, with a eon at this very moment out on those battlefields—and as such I put it to them not as a duty, but as a privilege that they have the proud privilege of defending their hearths, their homes, their King, their• countryr-,a privilege which they cannot lightly disregard.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>VOLUNTARY SYSTEM ON ITS TRIAL.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTARY SYSTEM ON ITS TRIAL.</p>
        <p>"At the present time we have a voluntary system, and of that system we are extremely proud, but already the shadow of some form of compulsion hangs over the land. The voluntary system is on its trial, and by, our efforts during the next few weeks it will stand or fall. I appeal with all the earnestness of which I am capable, and with a sincere belief in the justice of the cause for which we are fighting, to our young men to come forward and show the world that England is undivided in her intention to win this war and will not hesitate, whatever be the price of success. There are great numbers of eligible men in Warrington who have not yet responded to the Call. I cannot, and will not believe, that they are devoid of all patriotic instincts. I urge them to come forward at once and help their comrades who have given up everything for them. How often do we hear the excuse: 'I would go, but I can't be spared at my work,' or I would go, but my father Or mother would rather I did not'? Well, none of us is indispensable, and even Mayors can be re- placed, and to that young man I would point out that if he -died to-night the world in all probability wonld go on just as usual to-mor- row. Let him come forward, and a competent authority will say whether he is indispensable or not.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>CONSCRIPTS MAY BE DIFFERENTLY TREATED.</head>
        <p>CONSCRIPTS MAY BE DIFFERENTLY TREATED.</p>
        <p>"To the fathers and mothers I would also appeal, and ask them to remember that there are other fathers and mothers in our land who have given of 'their dearest and best. Will they lag behind the other fathers and mothers when England calls? Generous terms have been given by most employers, and in parti- ,cular by this Corporation—who have sent a large proportion of their employes—but the time is drawing near when those terms may not be forthcoming. I cannot speak for the Council, but for myself I do say that in the event of a system of compulsion being found necessary, there will not be the same generous treatment for the man who simply does his duty because he is forced, as for the man who freely comes forward as a volunteer. I do not know the number of single men that have gone from the Corporation, but at the present time the country's needs 'demand that no single eligible man should be found either in the service of the Corporation or of any other firm in Warrington, that is, without an adequate reason. (Hear, hear.) lam sorry that I can- not put the appeal more strongly. I am but a plain, blunt man, but, as our great dramatist says: An honest tale loses none the less for being plainly told.'</p>
        <p>THE ONE TASK OF' THE COMING YEAR. " Poi the coming year we have but one task— the safety. of our Empire—and let us unite and work together wholeheartedly for that purpose, and that purpose alone. (Applause.) Let there. be a greater spirit of give-and-take, less criticism, and bitter debate on matters -which, however deai• they may be to us as individuals, fade into insignificance before our Empire's need. Let us be a little less critical of those in high- places to whom Our interests are com- mitted, and give them credit for doing their best to win through a difficult task. It is only by co-operation that we can win through, and I ask you to give me your support in a greater measure if possible than before, and particu- larly in the great task' of enlisting every avail- able man for the service of his King aid country. 'England, my England, what can I. do for thee?' should be the text and watch- word of each one of us, for it is only self- sacrifice, coupled with a humble confidence in the all-seeing goodness of a Higher. Power, that will again bring our country safely through this great crisis, and give again the blessing of a righteous and lasting peace to lit and ours for all time to come." (Loud applause.)</p>
        <p>DEPUTY-MAYOR REAPPOINTED. The newly re-elected Mayor then appointed Dr. Joseph to again act as his deputy for. the twelve months. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>HIS WORSHIP'S CHAPLAIN.</head>
        <p>HIS WORSHIP'S CHAPLAIN.</p>
        <p>Next the Mayor intimated that the Rev. Canon Willis, Rector of Warrington, had again kindly consented to act as his chaplain. He also invited the members of the Council and the officials to accompany him to church to-morrow. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>LOCAL INSURANCE COMMITTEE'S FELICITATIONS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL INSURANCE COMMITTEE'S FELICITATIONS.</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of the Warrington Insurance Committee, held on Thursday even- ing, Mr. Peter, Delaney, on behalf of the com- mittee, congratulated She Mayor (Alderman PAer Peacock) upon his re-electiim as civic head Of the borough for another twelve months, and trusted that his further period of office would be a successful one. It began amid the clashing of arms; might it end on the note of peace. Mr. Delaney also paid a high tribute to the services of the Mayoress.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>COUNCIL BOMBSHELL,</head>
        <p>COUNCIL BOMBSHELL,</p>
        <p>Mr. E. Ons.rne Resigns His</p>
        <p>Seat.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>OUTC'POfecEN CRITICISM.</head>
        <p>OUTC'POfecEN CRITICISM.</p>
        <p>Mayor Speaks Of "Prussianised</p>
        <p>Machine."</p>
        <p>A letter of resignation from Councillor Edmund Osborne who for three years has sat as one of the representatives of the St. Austin's Ward—was read at the Mayor's Day meeting of the Warrington Town Council, and gave rise to considerable discussion and to the expression of some opinions that might be described as of the outspoken and " nothing- wrapped-up" variety. The rumour had got abroad that the popular Councilor's resignation was impending, but, nevertheless, to• many in the Council Chamber the following letter came as something of a surprise :- 22, Bold-street, Warrington, 29th October, 1915.</p>
        <p>Dear Mr. Mayor,—As I cannot now devote any time to the work of the Town Council, I am reluctantly compelled to resign my seat, and beg to enclose the fiat, =cording to the bylaw of the Council.</p>
        <p>During my membership my . relations with you, sir, and with the other aldermen and councillors have been most friendly, and I have received unvarying kindness from the offi- cials. I cannot take my leave without saying how grateful I am for the kindly c¢nsideration which has always been shown me. Hoping that your extended term of office may be as fruitful in good results for the town as previously, I beg to remain, Very truly yours, EDMUND OSBORNE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>MAYOR'S OUTSPOKEN COMMENTS.</head>
        <p>MAYOR'S OUTSPOKEN COMMENTS.</p>
        <p>The letter was read by the MAYOR, who went on to say : I am sorry that Mr. Osborne has been com- pelled to send in his _resignation. The loss 'of his services at any time would be keenly felt, but, coming as it does, under such unusual and trying ,circumstances, makes it doubly so. • I have a high personal regard for him, a regard which, I -am sure, is shared by every member of the Council. He has been a member only three years, but during -that period he has devoted himself to the duties in no half-hearted manner. (Hear,. hear.) He has served upon important committees, was chairman of one and deputy-chairman of another, and his services have been highly appreciated. His -business acumen and his knowledge of finance, which have been shown in this chamber more than</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>EX-COUNCILLOR E. OSB</head>
        <p>EX-COUNCILLOR E. OSB</p>
        <p>once, and his desire to serve the best interests of the town, are well known. He has always maintained a spirit of independence, and that has prompted his every action. (Hear, hear.) His keen public efforts in providing organised sports in Victoria Park and elsewhere are well known to you, and his knowledge in that direc- tion will be a distinct loss to us, and, whilst some may not attrp3ute much importance to it, yet it is a work which will have to be taken up more and more systematically in the future. (Hear, hear.) In all those matters I have re- ferred to we shall miss his services, and, what is of equal, if not of outstanding importance, we shall miss his personality. (Hear, hear.) • Should be Publicly Denounced.</p>
        <p>I had hopes that the wishes of Parliament. would be respected, and that there would be no elections save under circumstances over which we have no control, but I am informed that Mr. Osborne is carrying out the wishes of the gentle- men who control the institution where he is engaged. If that is so, and I have been credibly informed that it is, then I think it is high time that such action should be publicly denounced. (Applause.) Are public usefulness and service to be esti- mated at such a low value? Are the rights, of citizenship to be so easily' flouted? Is there no liberty of the subject, or are we to be parts of a machine Prussianised up to the hilt, and con- trolled by levers in the hands-of those who, while not refusing honours themselves, deny them to their capable, efficient and public- spirited servants? I feel strongly upon such an un-Christian attitude. Where is the boast of freedom when such actions dominate our public institutions? Is it honest? Is it fair? Is it just that such behaviour should go unchal- lenged? With full knowledge of my position, and advisedly, I say it should not; it cannot, and everyone with any degree of fairness will agree. (Applause.) I cannot imagine for a single moment how any self-respecting com- mittee, members of which themselves hold public offices, can 'deny to a. member of their ,staff equal rights and privileges of holding similar offices, if he is capable of doing so, espe- cially as in the case of Mr. Osborne, who never attended the Council and committee meetings except in his own leisure time.</p>
        <p>"Some Mistake in Their Methods of Reasoning." Surely, there must be some mistake in their methods of reasoning. I am sorry, for obvious reasons, that it should fall to my lot to have to denounce publicly such an insult upon the ser- vices to the town, but I feel that I ought to protest against it. Mr. Osborne has my sincere sympathy, and if it were possible—knowing full well his sterling qualities—l should refuse to .accept his resignation pending further considera- tion, so that the public of Warrington would then have an opportunity of knowing exactly on whom to fix the blame, and could assess them at their proper public value. (Applause.) It will be extremely difficult to replace Mr. Osborne with anyone of equal capabilities, and one who could command the confidence of the electors—especially at a time like the Present; when everyone is working at full pressure and eligible gentlemen cannot spare the time. I hope at the proper time we may be permitted to re-elect Mr. Osborne—(hear, hear, and ap- plause)—and leave with them the responsibility of saying what they should or Should not do. . (Applause.) The Mayor ended by moving the following resolution: "That this Council receives with regret the resignation of Edmund Osborne, Esq., of. the offiee of councillor, and desires to place on record the public services rendered by him dur- ing his Connection with the Town Council for a ,period of three years."</p>
        <p>, ,„1ff,01.7it1ti1fi1151141.1:.,... , ~i,,1tif.,,.: ..-;...•:, . '''7'il`''', 't,::11:;;4;li.44 Ali,s.',.::''' ~ ~. e. 0.4., 1,,,-,...v.v% • Mit4,,,A3it'',,, ..... 4•4, ',f'.l4;:. '.''',:.,...:: . v .t,v4it.tl•; :-.., ,:.,‘„, ... :, • . ,ttifotc-ge.,.:.- ••••:.t.,,, ~•;:. .',.,:*,:.:..,.'',.:;'.::4:-.......kik4..':;514j4k 4 #.4l;takiii-... ....','..i.t.;3'.::.:•.. ' .*,,.• , ' ...: ...,..Y.Atik}4.l glitl: V..'.4!?0,- ',i'.....-'lll,';',''' kiltlit* --. ~.:0.4-,,,,,t3.,,,. ,i,.. iAtt1ftke.94,..,:... :g*...*.iititg •,,k4..,:....4.'...,04:.,.'.„.4:',..... ... . Nit.1.4,...,:::::.',.. ‘ ' --,.f4,..., ~.. , v ~, &gt; .. • ~,,,,,,,,,,.. , ~... ......,,,,,,t ~:z... - -.....5...,,,t.?,.t* . —.. ~.z.F.J.,.......f.1t,,,\,„..,,...,,...":,.. lip . . v ...,..,..., ~,.. ....„.„..= 1.9,:ii.:*.,....:•....., ~..'.':;::'.4.:y:;.. ' ...... :.-.' , ~.„:....... . - .. :.-...,*•.,..,,, . ;OHNE. ____—</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>DEPUTY MAYOR'S PERSONAL EXPLANATION.</head>
        <p>DEPUTY MAYOR'S PERSONAL EXPLANATION.</p>
        <p>The DEPUTY MAYOR (Councillor Joseph) seconded the resolution,- and said that he thor- oughly endorsed all the Mayor's remarks with regard to the value of Councillor Osborne as a member of the municipal body. He also ,shared in the regret that was felt at the fact that they were losing his services: • (Hear, hear.) His (the speaker's) main reason in rising was to utter a few words of personal explanation. He happened to be a trustee of the institution to which Mr.. Osborne belonged, and he must say that the Mayor's denunciation of it ought to make him, (the Doctor) feel very much ashamed;</p>
        <p>but, as -A matter of fact, it did not, b.</p>
        <p>he</p>
        <p>was not responsible for it, and knew nothing about it. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Strong Opponent of Prussianised lMethods</p>
        <p>Personally, he was one of the strongest oppo- nents of such ,Prussianised methods; he was very strongly against that sort of thing. He did not know what could be done in the matter now, and this was the first he had' beard of it officially. He thought it was only fair to him- self that he should say those few words pub- licly, in order to clear his character, at all events, from taking part in the arbitrary pro- ceedings to which reference had been made. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>"A DASTARDLY PIECE OF WGRK."</head>
        <p>"A DASTARDLY PIECE OF WGRK."</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON supported the resolu- tion, which, he said, he was pleased the Deputy Mayor had seconded. His blood boiled when he heard the Mayor's speech; indeed, he (Coun- cillor Pemberton) did not know what he should have said if he had had to speak before him. He had gone' through some bitter moments in his own life, but never did he know such a dastardly piece of work as that, the details of which had just been disclosed to the Council. "You would not," continued the Councillor, "think that some of the men in the town were Englishmen; you would think that they were Germans, or worse. Did His Duty Without Fear or Favour.</p>
        <p>"I have heard of pressure being brought to bear directly or indirectly, on men, but never have I known such a base attempf as this. Here is a man who is ,obviously to the whole of the town thoroughly independent and does' net care a hang for anyone—a man who does his duty without fear or favour. He does not trim his sails to any wind." . Many a time, Councillor Pemberton continued, he had crossed swords with Councillor Osborne, but he had always respected that gentleman's opinions; as, Weed, did all the members of the Council—they all respected what Councillor Osborne said, because they knew that it came from the bottom of his heart. They knew that he had no motive behind what he said. He had no shares in companies, or anything else; no relations to back up or get into favour. Councillor Osborne was not a man who sought his own aggrandisement; he was a man who got down and did the donkey work, and had been doing it for years. He neither feared whether he was going to vex people or to please people; he was content that he did his duty to his fellow man. He did that duty un- flinchingly; without fear and favour, and for the benefit of the public of the town. "Most Abominable Thing." -</p>
        <p>• "If," concluded Councillor Pemberton, ." the Council and the public of Warrington sit down and allow a man like Councillor Osborne, with his -ability, principles, and honesty of purpose, to be sacrificed, well, all I can say is that they are not worth fighting for. (Hear, hear, and 'applause.) But- I will not believe it for one moment. I believe there will be a great cry of indignation from the whole population of the town. This is the most abominable thing I ever heard of in the whole course of my public career. It shows you the depths to which some people will stoop."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>COUNCILLORS NO PARTY TO IT.</head>
        <p>COUNCILLORS NO PARTY TO IT.</p>
        <p>•The MAYOR said he would like to make it clear that the members of the Council had been no party to what had occurred. He had . made careful inquiry into the matter, and had 'found that none of the members of the Council had any hand in it. Councillor BROADHURST : IS it through a differ- ence in political principles? The MAYOR: Excuse me, he was the only man in the Council who did not have any politics. Councillor BROADHURST went on to say that if was with extreme regret he had heard what had been said in reference to the cause of Councillor Osborne's resignation. Although he was opposed to him politically, he and the Councillor had been the best of friends. With reference to the pressure which it was said had been brought to bear upon Councillor Osborne, he supposed the Mayor was in possession of 'absolute facts. The MAYOR: Absolute facts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>WAS PUBLIC DISCUSSION A MISTAKE?</head>
        <p>WAS PUBLIC DISCUSSION A MISTAKE?</p>
        <p>Councillor BROADHURST : Unfortunately I have none of the facts, and there are very few mem- bers of the Council who have facts upon which they can speak with the emphasis that Council- lor Peniberton has spoken this Morning. :Still; he added; at the sathe time he knew that if ever there was a man in the town who would reason a matter out before he spoke upon it that man was the 'Mayor. He believed that every mem- ber of the Council would rise to the occasion if what they had been led to believe was the truth, but until they got the truth, and the whole truth, lie thought it was a great mistake to discuss the matter publicly. Until they were in possession of full knowledge of the whole of the transaction he thought they should refrain from taking sides one way or the other. _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor WILSON Said he thought it was a most dastardly act for any pressure, to have been brought to .bear to cause Councillor Osborne to resign from public service, more especially seeing that he was giving that service in his own time. "I would," the Councillor went on, "take. very •brusqiie steps with' these men. I say this, quite candidly and independ- ently, that I should have these men taken out publicly and thrashed in the streets. That's what 1 should do !" (Laughter.) The .Ithvoit, (smilingly) : But we can't do it, unfortunately. (Laughter.) COUNCILLOR WILSON'S HOPE.</p>
        <p>Councillor WILSON: I should never have be- lieved that these men—l know one Or two of them, but I shall never acknowledge them as long as my name is Wilson—could have done such a dirty trick to such an honourable man. I hope that in the near future these men will be paid back in their own coin, and that every member of the Council will join in .making a stand against them and say "No; we are going to have nothing to do with you if you treat honest and faithful servants as you have treated this man." (Rear, hear, and applause.) Councillor GOUGH said he was sorry that Coun- cillor Osborne was compelled to resign his seat. He had been told—he took it for what- it was worth and gave it forth for what credit the public would attach to it—that the directors of 'the public institution -concerned were put there by their friends'</p>
        <p>The 3iAYOR, ringing his bell, called the speaker to order.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>"GREAT TECHNICAL FINANCIAL ABILITY."</head>
        <p>"GREAT TECHNICAL FINANCIAL ABILITY."</p>
        <p>Councillor GOUGH went on to say that one reason why he was sorry that Councillor Osborne should have been forced to retire from the Council was because there could be no ques- tion about it, he was a man of great technical, financial ability—one who was a most useful asset to any public institution, and especially to a. Town Council. They all knew what he had done for the finances of the Gas Committee, and also for the water undertaking. The Mayor had said that no members of the Council had been the cause of Councillor Osborne's retire- ment,. Well, if they had not been so directly, he believed that they had been indirectly, and— The Mayor again vigorously rung his bell for order, but Councillor Gough went on speaking. For a few seconds his remarks were inaudible, but as he sat down he was heard to say, 'lt's a most despicable, Germanlike action."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>"IT IS A PITY TO THINK THAT -"</head>
        <p>"IT IS A PITY TO THINK THAT -"</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON said it was a pity to think-that they were living in an age when any .man who gave value for money in his employ- ment, could be told by his employers what he should or -should ndt do with his own private time. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>Alderman S3IETHURBT : The Council has done that. It has objected to any. person in his private time doing private business. . The 3.1.4Y0n.: This, is public business; this is a public . duty. . Copncillor WILKINSON went on to say that he deplored the fact that it should ever occur in the town of Warrington that any young man who was willing to give his abilities and a por- tion of his spare time to the public work of the town, either as a member Of the Council, or in any other capacity, should be compelled to relin- quish such works at the dictation of his em- ployers. 'lre regretted that employers should be in a position to say, "You shall do this and you shall not do that!" In one sense, and in one sense only, was he rather pleased that this busi- ness .had occurred, because it would probably be the means of bringing more democratic feel- ing out of the breasts of the people Of War- rington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>"LANGUAGE THAT CANNOT HELP MR. OSBORNE."</head>
        <p>"LANGUAGE THAT CANNOT HELP MR. OSBORNE."</p>
        <p>Alderman TINNION observed that a good many things had been said that some people might be sorry for. Language had been used and a posi-</p>
        <p>tion faken up that couldrnot possibly help Coun- cillor Osborne, and his view was that when_ Councillor Osborne got to know what had been: said his comment would probably be "For heaven's sake, deliver me from my would-be friends." "I am only- sorry," Alderman Tin- nion went on, " that Councillor Henry Roberts is not here this morning. If there is one man. who has taken the matter up and done all he possibly could for Councillor Osborne it is .he,. and if he had been here this morning I think he would have directed the debate into a some-- what different form. We all appreciate the. work of Councillor Osborne, and learn with regret the circumstances surrounding his ietire- ment. I remember when the matter was raised some considerable time ago, Councillor Roberts took it up and defended him before the com- mittee. The committee Were insisting upon his retiring at that time, and he got this conces- sion—that, at any rate, he should sit for the. three years for which he was elected. CouncilL- lor Osborne has told me on more than one occa- sion that if there is one friend who has stuck to him in this matter, it is Councillor Roberts. I believe in doing justice to everyone. Council:- lor Roberts has been Councillor Osborne's friend* in the matter, and Councillor Osborne has been- from beginning to end willing and ready to, acknowledge it." The resolution accepting Councillor Osborne's resignation with regret was then carried unani- mously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>CORPORATION COMMITTEES.</head>
        <p>CORPORATION COMMITTEES.</p>
        <p>Unchallenged Appointments At Mayor's-Day Council Meeting.</p>
        <p>At last year's Mayor's Day meeting of, the Warrington Town. Council, it will he recalled. the filling up of the necessary committees gave rise to prolonged discussion and not a little acrimony. But when the Council, on Tuesday morning, came to discharge the same task- all was peace.</p>
        <p>A printed list of the committeemen suggested, together with details of the duties of each com+ mittee, was in the possession of every member of the Council. According to this list, which was approved without comment, the following will constitute the various committees during the ensuing twelvemonth : Watch: The Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock), the Deputy Mayor (Councillor Joseph), Alder- men Bolton, • James Evans and Tinnion, Councillors Chrimes, Dickenson, Halliwell, Henshall, Lindley, Pemberton and Wilkinson. Finance and Estate: The Deputy Mayor„ Aldermen Bennett John Evans, Pierpoint and Smethurst, Councillors Archer', Barton, ChrimeS, Benshall, Jenkins, Lindley, Locker, Taylor and Wilkinson.</p>
        <p>Street Improvement: The Deputy Mayor, Aldermen Bennett, James Evans, Peacock and Tinnion, Councillors Barton, Chantler. Cyaik. Chrimes, Gough, Halliwell, Lashford, Pember- ton and Wilkinson.</p>
        <p>Health: The. Deputy Mayor, Aldermen Bur- ton, James Evans and Tinnion, Councillors Archer, Broadhurst, Dale, Dickenson, Fox, Lashford, MeKee, Shaw, Wilkinson and Wilson. Sanitary Works: Aldermen James Evans, John Evans, Smethurst and Tinnion, Coun- cillors Archer, Barton, Broadhurst, Chrimes. Lashford, McKee, Shaw, _Taylor and Wilson. Paving and Sewerage: Aldermen James Evans, Peacock, Smethurst and Tinnion, Coun- cillors Archer, . Barton, Broadhurst, Cunning- ton, Fox, Gough, Pemberton, Taylor, Wilkin- son and Wilson.</p>
        <p>Museum: The Deputy Mayor, Aldermen Ben- nett, Peacock and Pierpoint, Councillors Fox, Halliwell, Henshall, Jenkins, Lindley, Locker. Roberts and Skelland, and the following non- members of the Council: Messrs. Robert Pier- point, T. J. Ridgway, J. F. Dutton, Thos. May, L. Greening, A. J. Jolley, John -Upson and Thos. Leslie.</p>
        <p>Cemetery: Councillors Archer, Barton. Chantler, Cnnnington, Gough; Halliwell; Hen- shall, Lindley and Skelland. Town Hall, Parks and Baths: Aldermen Bolton, Burton, Pierpoint and Smethurst, Councillors Charmer, ChrinieS, Ctiriningtbn„ Dickenson, Gough, Halliwell, Henshall and. Wilkinson,</p>
        <p>Markets: The Mayor, Alderman James Evans, Councillors Archer, Barton, Changer,: Chrimes, Cunnington, Dickenson, Gough, Jen- kins, McKee and Skelland. Gas.—The Deputy Mayor, Aldermen Bolton, John Evans, Pierpoint and Tinnion, Councillors Broadhurst, Craik, Cufinington, Gough, 'Lind- ley,- Locker, McKee, Taylor and Wilkinson. Rivers: Aldermen Bennett, John Evans, Pie"" point and Tinninn, Councillors Chantler, Gough, Halliwell, Henshall and Shaw. Fire Engine: The Mayor, Councillors Barton, Broadhurst, Chrimes, Cunnington, Dickenson, Lashford, Lindley, Pemberton, .Skel- land and Wilson.</p>
        <p>Water: The Deputy Mayor, Aldermen Bolton. James Evans, John Evans and Pierpoint, Coun- cillors Archer, Barton. Cunnington, Jenkins. Lindley, Locker, McKee,, Pemberton and Taylor.</p>
        <p>Gymnasium: Aldermen John Evans and Tinnion. Councillors Barton. Dickenson, Shaw, Skelland and Wilson, and the following non- members of the Council: Messrs. Thomas Locker and John Upson:</p>
        <p>Electricity and Tramways: The Mayor, the Deputy-Mayor. Alderman .Tolin Evans, Council- lors Archer, Broadhurst, Chrimes, Dale, Halli- well, Lashford, Lindley, Roberts, Taylor and Wilkinson.</p>
        <p>Small Holdings and Allotments: The Deputy- Mayor. Aldermen John Evans and Smethurst, Cotincillors Dickenson, Henshall, Jenkins, Lind- ley, Pemberton and Wilkinson.</p>
        <p>Towns: The Mayor, Aldermen Bennett and James Evans, Councillors Chantler, Cunning- ton, Dickenson, Henshall, Lashford, Locker and Pemberton.</p>
        <p>It was also decided that the Bridges and General Purposes Committees should consist of all the members of the Council. The MAYOR observed that there were one or. two members who would resign off some of the committees, but the vacancies would be filled. up by the next meeting. In the meantime the; list stood.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>AFTER THE WAR.</head>
        <p>AFTER THE WAR.</p>
        <p>Something Of Great Importance</p>
        <p>To Workers.</p>
        <p>Mr. -E. Plinston, the newly-elected chairman. of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council, returned his thanks for his elevation to his present position at the monthly meeting of the delegates to that body held at the Socialist Hall. on Tuesday evening. He very highly allpre-, dated, he said, the honour which had been conferred upon him. The coming twelve-. months, he felt, would be full of very impor- tant work for the Trades Council and he relied • upon the delegates to give their very best at- tention to the business that would come before them. It was rather regrettable that, seeing that .they had some seventy-fire delegates, the. average attendance should he 60 small, for, it occurred to him, that if the real interest waS shown in the bdsiness there would be very much larger attendances. He wanted to appeal to branches to see to it that during his year of office they insisted Upon their delegates attend- ing as regularly and punctually as possible. He was convinced that during the coming year it would be the duty of the Council to look more closely after many branches who •were, pro- bably, apt to regard the present terrible ordeal of war as being the only thing fo trouble .about. ,He- was second to none in his Opinion that the war had to be fought to a successful issue, but he was afraid that too many people were losing sight of the fact that there would- be something else after the war which would be of very great importance to the workers; •viz., the question of cohesion, which was essen- tial to their own well-being. They must band themselves, as workers, more strongly together in the future than ever they did in the past, • and strive by every means to uphold the tradk. tions of trade unionism..</p>
        <p>The more you do for some people the angrier they get with you for not doing more.</p>
        <p>The Wrong Ticket. _ Ticket Collector: We don't stop here, sir. Montague Swank (who has just given 1111 at ticket): Stop where? . Ticket collector: At the pawnbroker's.</p>
      </div>
