      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>Tetters to the (Ehitar</head>
        <p>Tetters to the (Ehitar</p>
        <p>SUNDAY SCHOOL PROSPECTS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The Rev. EL B. Firth is gravely con- cerned about .Sunday school prospects. I have been very much Concerned myself for some •</p>
        <p>If there is not a greater interest taken in the children our Sunday schools will go down. There should be more earnest teachers who will visit their absent scholars to know the :reason why they are not attending school. The :parents will, perhaps, be encouraged when they find their children are being cared for by the teachers. There ought to be a children's, ser- vice in every school on Sunday evenings to bencourage them to ,recite psalms, paragraphs of Scripture, sing solos, &amp;c. We must find them -something to do to keep them out of the streets, and train them in the way they should go, that when they grow up they will not depart from :it. Bands of Hope are also being neglected. I would be very pleased if some ladies or gentle- men would come to, the school to play the piano ,or harmonium and help in this work. ' - J. SKELLA.ND,, Superintendent Ragged Sunday School. • 63a, Bewsey-street, Nov. 11th, 1915.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>Dlt INK RESTRICTIONS.</head>
        <p>Dlt INK RESTRICTIONS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—lt is really rather amusing to. find a correspondent who himself gives no other name and address than "Citizen" taking me to task for signing my letter with my own name and that of the town from which I write. It is a case of "people in glass houses should .not throw stones"; and I trust Mr. "Citizen" .feels the foolishness of his action now that his con- servatory is shattered by the return of his own brickbat. Mr. " Citizen ". says that the "logic of facts' is dead against me, and that drunkenness statistics are "eloquent proof of the axiom; -reduce the tcitie Oi rinking., and you lrontei.ness " An a data is a sit- evident truth, requiring no proof, and Mr. " Citizen's" sweeping assertion is most cer- tainly not one. It is just this idea that reduced drinking facilities mean reduced drunkenness that I am fighting, This stupid fallacy is at the bottom of the teetotalist policy of coercion and restriction, so-called ." temper- mince" reform; .and until it' is dropped, I hold no progress can be made towards the promotion of national sobriety. Far from reducing drunkenness, undue restriction only. aggravates the evil, and we find that the neart=r we get to complete prohibition the more objectionable mind deadly become the forms which excessive drinking takes up. In Petrograd to-day the use of methylated spirits as a substitute for the prohibited vodka is very genera), and as a con- sequence the number of deaths from delirium tremens is constantly increasing. - Were Mr., " Citizen's " precious "axiom " correct we -should naturally expect complete prohibition to produce a complete absence- of .drunkenness. What do we find? Portland, the chief city in the State of Maine. Which has enjoyed over sixty-nine years of prohibition, has en average of arrests for drunkenness ranging from 57 per 1,000 inhabitants to 70 per 1.000. Mr. Arthur Sherwell, M.P., the well-known teetotalist, in giving the above figures, states that be can testify from personal experience that the Portland police are slow to.artest for drunkenness alone! I could multiply -instances indefinitely to show that reduced facilities for drinking almost invariably lead to an increase of insobriety. Just the opposite of our friend's "axiom." Space, however, forbids; end t will conclude by giving Mr. " Citizen " a little hint. The most potent sources of drunkenness are rapid drinking and Secret drinking; reduced hours aggravate both these evils. Hence the problem. J. E..SIMPSON. Liverpool, November Bth, 1915.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
