      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>NEW STANDING ORDER.</head>
        <p>NEW STANDING ORDER.</p>
        <p>Alderman TINNION successfully proposed a new Standing Order at Tuesday's meeting of the Town Council, the innovation being de- signed to meet the special, war-time conditions prevailing. The new Order. reads :—" That the Mayor be empowered, after A conference with the chairmen of each of the several committees of the Council, to postpone any meeting of a committee to a date that may be agreed upon." ONLY A TEMPORARY ARRANGEMENT.</p>
        <p>In submitting his proposal to the Council, the Alderman said that he thought it was as well, when they were making any change, that the Council should be governed by a Standing Order. The Standing Order he was proposing was only intended as a .temporary one;: indeed it was not a Standing Order he would like to see adopted in ordinary times. But the present were not ordinary times, and for the moment the duties of the Corporation were largely of a routine character, and the work which, in the immediate future, would fall on the various committees would not be half so exacting as it had been in the past, when various schemes of improvement had to come before the Council— all that sort of thing being tiqnporarily hung up. Probably many committee meetings would be arranged, as usual, and at several of them it would be found that the only business to be transacted was the formal passing of accounts., It did seem a pity that members of the com- mittees should be called together so frequently as in the past, only to find when they turned up at the meetings that there was hardly any business to transact and, so often, none of any importance. The proposed new Standing Order would give power to the Mayor, after consulta- tion with the chairman of the committee con- cerned, to say whether or not it was desirable, or necessary, to call the members of the com- mittee together. Tie was sure that the Council would have sufficient confidence in the Mayor and the chairmen of the various committees to leave it in their hands as to whether they thought it necessary that the meetings should be called. Alderman BENNETT seconded.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR PEMBERTON'S SUGGESTION Councillor PEMBERTON was Of opinion that it would be more convenient ,if, at their own meetings, each Committee fixed the date of its next meeting, subject to the Mayor having 'power to call the members together should necessity arise.</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON ,stid that the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee had adopted that principle for some time. They had passed a resolution to have a meeting once a month only. A DANGEROUS PRINCIPLE?</p>
        <p>Councillor BROADIII3B3T argued that if Alder- man Tinnion's motion was carried it would mean instituting one of the most dangerous principles that had ever been introduced into the Council. The whole of the responsibilities of the committees would be left with the chair- Men of such committees and the Mayor. Whilst he believed that Alderman Tinnion put forward his proposal in the best interests of the towil, in his opinion the passing of such a Standing Order as that proposed would constitute one of the greatest mistakes the Council ever made, for it would be a mistake for the whole of the Council to leave the business of any committee resting on the , shoulders Of its chairman, for the simple reason that the chairmen of com- mittees had not always given. that satisfaction which some members of the committees had desired: He thought the best thing they could do was to go on as they were going at present. A SUFFICIENT SAFEGUARD.</p>
        <p>The DEPUTY MAYOR (Dr. Joseph) strongly sup- ported Alderman Tinnion's proposal "for the ensuing year, or during the period of the war." There would be a sufficient safeguard if the matter was left to the chairman of a com- mittee and the Mayor. However, he could , not help thinking that the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee illustration was rather an un- fortunate one (Laughter.) Alderman BENsErr pointed out that the pro- posed measure was a peculiarly temporary one, dictated by the extraordinary circumstances of the present time. Such a Standing Order might be considered extremely dangerous in normal times, but the present times were not normal. Councillor WILKINSON . gave the proposal his support on the understanding that the Stand- ing Order was a purely temporary one. The MAYOR said that the principle underlying the proposed new Standing Order had been adopted in other towns, owing to business men who sat on the public bodies being so actively engaged that they could not afford, nor did they desire, to waste time. The chairman of each committee would be guided, in the matter of the application of the new Standing Order, by his own committee's feelings. Alderman TINNioN said he did not believe in placing unlimited power in the hands of the Mayor, or any chairman of committee, and he thought that if any postponement of meetings were to take plaee it should be done in con- formity with Standing Orders. Hence, his pro- posal. Councillor GOUGH considered that if the motion was carried it would put too much responsi- bility on the Mayor and the chairman of each committee.</p>
        <p>The motion was carried, 14 voting for it and only four against.</p>
        <p>AN ABORTIVE AMENDMENT.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON then moved as an amend- ment that each committee shouki have the power of settling the date of its next meeting, remarking that he .did • not see ,why the Standing Orders should have been brought into the ques- tion at ~all, as there had been no trouble in the past. and when the officials had intimated that there was no business for the. • next meeting everybody concerned had agreed on the course of action to be followed. The proposal put for- ward by Alderman Tinnion would just suit some members of some of the committees, .for their attendances Were shocking. They scarcely at- tended any meetings at all, and he believed that, if it were not for the fact that the list of attendances was occasionally published, , some of the members would never turn up at all. (Laughter.) Personally, he was one of those, who attended to their public duties. , He contended that members of the Council were very much in the position of managing direc- tors, and what firm would be .stupid enough to propose that there should not be directors' meet- ings as usual? Of course, whilst the war Was on the committees would not have much new Work to do, but the ordinary business of the Council had to go on. The Towrr CLERK (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle) inter- polated the •explanation that he .was entirely responsible for the suggestion that the War- rington Council's committees should g,,t into line with the Council committees of ether towns.</p>
        <p>- Councillor PEMBERTON I am verk sorry to hear it!</p>
        <p>WHY THE SUGGESTION WAS MADE</p>
        <p>The Toww CLERK added that he made the suggestion because the heads of the various Cor- poration departments had intim:lLA to him that 'there was not much work' to be done nowadays.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON: Yes, but I object to the motion being made a Sta,ntling 4 irder. My slot :•,iment is that the ut.eti nr,•:4 shall be arianged by the committees themselves, and that they shall have power to adjourn or cut out any meeting. The TowN CLERK: You can do that now! Councillor BROADHURST seconded Councillor</p>
        <p>Continued in Next Column.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAYS NOVEMBER 13, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAYS NOVEMBER 13, 1915.</p>
        <p>By permission of the proprietors o</p>
        <p>Punch</p>
        <p>tion in London caused him to retire into the country, and on this day he Set out fo his father-in-law's house at Smallwood. In 1644 he entered Brasennose College, Oxford, and pursued his study of astronomy, natural his- tory and the mathematics, but owing to "the daemon Civil War he quitted the rational and inoffensive circle of the sciences, and received from Charles I. the office of one of the gentle- men of the ordnance of the garrison" on the 9th May, 1645, and that of a Commissioner for the Excise at Worcester on the 27th Decemtler. In the year following-1646—he was made Receiver and Registrar, also a Cap- tain in Lord Ashley's Regiment. On July 24th " Worcester was surrendered, and thence I rid out of town according to the articles and went to illy father Mainwaring in Cheshire." ,This proved .to be the memorable visit from a Mason's point of view, the following extract being the earliest known record of an initia- tion into the craft in England:</p>
        <p>Sentry: "Halt! Who goes there ?"—Rnstie : " Priend."—Sentry : "Pass friend. " Thankee, sir. I'm sorry to 'ave woke 'eo, Sir!"</p>
