      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>FREEMASONRY IN WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>FREEMASONRY IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>"LODGE OF LIGHTS."</p>
        <p>The 150th Anniversary Of</p>
        <p>Constitution.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>ELIAS ASHIVIOLE.</head>
        <p>ELIAS ASHIVIOLE.</p>
        <p>The following article, written by Mr. Chas. Lambert, appeared in the " Manchester Courier" on Monday this week. Mr. Lambert is a well-known Freemason in the Provinces of East and West Lanca- shire) and in London, where he is a founder and P.M. of the " Authors' " Lodge, No. 3456. Those who are acquainted with the magnifi- cent Ashmolean Museum in Oxford will be interested to know that its founder, Elias Ashmole, was associated with Warrington. He married the daughter of Mr. Peter Main- viaring in 1638, and in 1646 was made a Freemason at Warrington in a lodge that preceded the " Lodge of Lights," along with Col. Henry Mainwaring, and in the presence of several gentlemen bearing well- known local names.</p>
        <p>To-day the "Lodge of Lights," No. 148 Warrington, attains the one hundred and fiftieth anniversary of its constitution. Elias Ashmole was admitted into the Craft in War- rington on the 16th October, 1646, but unfor- tunately the records of the name and number of his lodge are lost to us. The principal 'authority for the life of Ash- mole is his own diary (first published in 1717, and a later sedition in 1774), which is a record of matters of great importance. His father followed the trade of a saddler, as also his grandfather, but he would appear to be of good family, and the latter was Chief Bailiff of Lichfield on' two occasions. His mother, whose maiden name was Bowyer, was closely related to "James Pagit, Esq., some time Puisne Baron of the Exchequer," who married her sister Bridget as .his second wife. Elias Ashmole was born at Lichfield on the 23rd May, 1617, where he received his early educa- tion, his abilities as a singer obtaining for him the position of one of the choristers at the Cathedral. On the 2nd of July, 1633, lie left Lichfield for London, arriving at the latter city about eleven o'clock before noon on the sth, and shortly after received news of the death of his father. Whilst a Chorister at the Cathedral Ashmole studied Latin, also the virginals and organ, and he had so far benefited by his education that, in .1638, he: became a solicitor in Chancery, and had , indifferent 'good practice." " About the beginning of July, Peter Venables, Baron of Kinderton, wrote to me, to take upon me the management of his law business." .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>HIS CAREER.</head>
        <p>HIS CAREER.</p>
        <p>On the 27th March of this year he was married to Miss Eleanor Mainwaring, eldest daughter of Mr. Peter Mainwaring, of Small- wood, in Cheshire, uncle to the Coll: Henry Mainwaring referred to. This lady died sud- denly on the sth December, 1641. In the early part of, the same year he "was sworn 'attorney in the Court of Common Pleas." On the 29th August, 1642, the political situa-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>SAVING TIME--continued.</head>
        <p>SAVING TIME--continued.</p>
        <p>Pemberton's amendment, remarking that it might be that other towns had adopted the pro- posal put forward, but that fact did not make it a right proposal. He thought the resolution had been rushed on the Council, and that they should have had some notice of it. He noticed that almost every chairman of committee voted for the proposal, and that was a significant fact; for, in his opinion, it was not right for any chairman of any committee to vote on any mat- ter that gave the power into his own hands. The MAYOR: It is quite in order! Councillor BOADRURSTI It may be right -legally, but is it right morally? I have never voted myself into any position—with one excep- tion. (Loud laughter.)</p>
        <p>The DEPUTY MAYOR said the whole matter seemed to him to be something like a row in a teapot; it was a difference between written and unwritten law.</p>
        <p>ALDERMAN TINNION'S REPUDIATION. Alderman TINNION repudiated "in the strong- est possible terms the absolutely rotten in- sinuation" which had been made as to the reason why he brought forward the proposed new Standing Order. If he were not more honest in his intentions than "some of them" he would never show his face in the Council Chamber. He brought forward his proposal be- cause he was requested to do so by 'the- Town Clerk, and because he could see that it would I give official and legal sanction to what, hither- to, had been done by the Town Clerk and chair- men of committees. He repudiated altogether any suggestion that he was actuated by any other motives than those of the best interests of the borough. Councillor BROADHURST : I said so.</p>
        <p>"NOT THE ONLY PEBBLE ON THE BEACH."</p>
        <p>Alderman TINSION : You are "not the only pebble on the beach "; you are not the only man who has been speaking to-day. There are other members who have endeavoured to put words into my mouth almost, and to ascribe to me actions and intentions of which I am altogether innocent. S,</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT said that of course it was just possible that the proposed new Order might be abused, but he had sufficient faith in tho Mayor and in the chairmen of the various committees to knev that it would not be. If there was the slightest sus- picion of abuse or suggestion, the whole thing was in the hands of the Council, and the Standing Order could be revoked at any time. That fact, he considered, was an ample safe- guard. The amendment was defeated, only three members voting for it, and the Standing Order, with Alderman Tinnion!s additi9A ly.ere then passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>1646.</head>
        <p>1646.</p>
        <p>A FREEMASON.</p>
        <p>Oct: 4 II 30' P.M. I was made a Free Mason at 16 Warrington in Lancashire, • with Coil: Henry Mainwaring of Karincham in Cheshire. The names of those that were then of the Lodge; Mr. Rich Penket Warden, Mr. James Collier, Mr. Rich: Sankey, Henry Littler, John Ellam, Rich: . Ellam &amp; Hugh Brewer There is no• further reference to, Free- masonry in the Diary until March, 1682. -</p>
        <p>10 About 5 II:\ P.M. I recd. ft:Summons to appe at a Lodge, to be, held the next day, at Masons Hall London' 11 Accordingly I went &amp; about Noone were admitted into the Fellowship of Free Masons, Sr. William Wilson Knight, Cau.pt. Rich: Borthwick, Mr. Will: Woodman', Mr. Win. Grey, Mr. Samuell Taylour, Mr. .William Wise. I was the Senior Fellow among them (it being 35 yeares since I was admitted). There were present beside my selfe the Fellowes after named: Itlr. The: Wise, Mr. of the Masons Company of. this pre- sent yeare, Mr. Thomas Shorthouse, Mr. Thomas- Shadbolt Waindsford, Esqr., Mr. Nich : Yining, Mr. John Shorthose, Mr. William Hamon;_lir. John Thomp- son, and Mr. Will: Stanton. •Wee all dyned at the halfe Moone Taverne in Cheapeside, at a Noble Dinner prepaired at the charge of. the New- accepted Masons.'</p>
        <p>The point frequently raised as to whether the ritual of these lodges was operative or speculative has been so ably dealt with in the first case by Bro. W. H. Itylands, and as regards the London meeting by Bro. E. Con- der, that it is unnecessary to deal with it in the present article. Each of these brethren has satisfactorily Proved that the gentlemen whose names were, mentioned as members or initiates were far above the operative class, and that speculative Masonry had been Prac- tised,. in. England many years before the ad- mission of Elias A.sliinole in 1646. . The following is one of the more trivial entries in the diary: April .20th 1657. for post mend. I bruised my great tee with the fall of a great form.</p>
        <p>On May. 7th, 1658, Ashmole "first went to the Record Office in the Tower, to collect materials for my work of the Garter."</p>
        <p>On the 12th December in the following year Mr. Tredescant and his wife told him "they had been long considering upon whom to bestow their closet of curiosities when they died and at last had resolved to give it unto me."</p>
        <p>On 16th June, 1660, he "first kissed the King's hand, being introduced by Mr. Thomas Chiffinch," and the entry for the 18th " Flor ante mend, was the second time I had the honour to discourse with the King, and then he gave me the place of 'Windsor Herald."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>ANTIQUARY AND PHILOSOPHER.</head>
        <p>ANTIQUARY AND PHILOSOPHER.</p>
        <p>Elias Ashmole was married in November; 1649, to the Lady Mainwaring, widow of a wealthy city knight. An entry in his diary prior to this states that "it pleased God to put me in mind, that I was now placed in the condition I always desired, which was, that I might be. enabled to live to myself and studies; without- being forced to take pains for a livelihood in the World." An interest- ing and useful career followed. He .became intimate with three noted astrologers, Lilly, Moore, and Booker, and in 1651 with Mr. William Backhouse, a Rosicrucian. In Sep- tember, 1652, he "took a journey into the Peak in search of plants and other curiosi- ties." In 1668, having lost his wife in April, he married again in November.• His work on the Garter was widely commended, and in 1673 he was awarded four hundred pounds out of 'the custom of paper." In 1683 he presented his collection of rarities to the museum at Oxford. He died on the 18th May, 1692, and was buried in the Church Of South Lambeth.</p>
        <p>It has been suggested whether Ashmole, being a Mystic as well as a Freemason, formed a link between the Rosicrucians and the Free- masons; but this point must rest in abeyance until further proofs are forthcoming His greatest 'work was " The Institution, Laws, and Ceremonies of the most noble Order of the Garter," which brought him many other tokens of honour. At his sdeath his library and manuscripts were forwarded to the Uni- versity of Oxford (Ashmolean Museum), these, with his other collections, being placed in the hands of the Curator, Dr. Plot, who is referred to in the final entries in the diary. Charles Lambert.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>MIDDLE CLASSES. AND IN( AtEASEL</head>
        <p>MIDDLE CLASSES. AND IN( AtEASEL</p>
        <p>RENTS.</p>
        <p>The Middle-class Protection Association have forwarded to the Government a resolution de- claring that the alarming tendency of a section of landlords to increase rents- (thus Casting their own share of the national burden upon their tenants, who are already severely hit-calls for immediate action by the Government to prevenb any such increases 'during war-time, and the Association pledges itself to support the. Gover- nment in any legislation they may introduce hav- ing tbis object in view.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>WARRINGTON GAS,</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON GAS,</p>
        <p>Corporation Ask For New Lighting Standard.</p>
        <p>MORE WAR-TIME SAVING.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. W. Brightmore, an inspector of the Local Government Board, conducted an inquiry on Wednesday morning at the Town Hall, War- rington, into an application made by the War- rington Corporation for a Provisional Order enabling them to make certain alterations to the Warrington Gas Act, 1877 The Corpyrs- tion sought powers (1) To provide for the abolition of the standard of illuminant-4 per of gas supplied by the Corporation i‘ ad to sub- stitute a calorific standard; (2) To ihereaie the pressure at which gas is required by the local Act to be supplied by the Corporation. At the inquiry Mr. A. T: liallaway, the deputy town clerk, gave the Inspector certain -:tati3ti :s, showing, among other things, that titi popula- tion of Warrington was at-present estimated to be 76,000. The rates were now 9s. \kr. in the .t, but this figure was greatly in excess of the usual rate because of the war. Last year Ale rate was Bs.</p>
        <p>, Ali'. W. S. Iladdock, the manager of the i.zaS works, said that the total amount of the loan sanctioned On behalf of the 'gas undertaking was £255,948; the total amount borroTed was £252,743, the anionnt repaid was £111,981, lad .the amount outstanding was £137,71:2</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>FALSE IMPRESSION CORRECTED.</head>
        <p>FALSE IMPRESSION CORRECTED.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hallaway said that with regard to the second clause of the application, the Corpora- tion did, not intend to proceed with it at that inquiry. It was not coverefl by the resolution which was passed by the Corporation, and it was inserted in the draft Provisional Order under a misapprehension. It had given rise, so he was informed, amongst people whom the Cor- poration supplied with gas, to the idea that the Corporation intended to force the gas through the meter much quicker and consequently they would have to pay more. He regretted that an impression like that should have got abroad. It was entirely erroneous. With regard to ilu- minating power at the present time the standard was 16 candles. Since the Act was passed in 1877, there had been a great change in the use of gas. At that time gas. was largely, if not wholly, used for illuminating purposes. The use of gas for commercial -purposes had now largely increased, in fact, the amount used for commercial purposes equalled, if it 'did not ex- ceed, the amount used for. illuminating pur- poses, particularly in a town where they had so many industries as they had in Warrington. In 1877 illumination was practically confined to " flat-flame " burners.</p>
        <p>Incandescent: Lights Displace " Flat-flames." To-day, except in.isolated cases, these were a thing of the past. They had given place to the incandescent mantles. As the Inspector was aware, for the production of light by incan- descent mantles, it was absolutely unnecessary that there should be any illuminating power in the gas at all. ' The light was produced by the heating power of . the gas on- the mantle. That produced the ineandesence, and if the Corpo'ration had to enrich the gas in order to give.a specified illuminating power of 16 candles, it was absolutely thrown away, so far as the incandescent lights were concerned. Gas to-day was required for heating purposes. It was im- material to the commercial user whether the gas was 20 candle, or 10 candle, or even 5 candle power. In 1914 'this principle was generally recognised by Parliament. It was fought out in the House of Commons at great,length, and Parliament decided, for once and for all, that the old-fashioned candle-pOwer was obsolete, and that modern requirements 'placed too great a burden noon the manufacturers of gas. So the Corporation was askirig for nothing new.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>Saving On Enriching Materials.</head>
        <p>A Saving On Enriching Materials.</p>
        <p>The Corporation had considered the matter for some time, and had come to the conclusion that owing to the increased price of materials used for the enriching of gas, that it was money unnecessarily spent. The use of cannel and benzol for enriching the gas meant a consider- able amount of money. The suggested change would he of benefit to the gas nianufacthrer, and, incidently, for the benefit of the consumer. Although the Corporation was not a philan- thropic institution, they found it was to their interest to reduce tlw price of gas as much as possible. An increased demand iiivariably followed a reduction in the price of gas. he price of gas in 1877 was 45., it had been reduced to 2s. id. net up to the:beginning pf the present year, when on account of the increased price of materials and the' decreased sale of the residuals, it was found necessary to increase the price. But that increase was only a tem- porary one. Re understood that there was no opposition to the course proposed. The .clause that he asked to be inserted in the Act provided that the gas shoukl be tested once a day, and that it should be of a calorific standard of 500 units.</p>
        <p>MINIMUM NOT TO BE THE MAXIMUM.</p>
        <p>Mr. Haddock said that the enriching materials had .become , so scarce that it would be impos- sible .to procure them shortly., The Proposals were. in the interests of the consumer. Five hundred units were a reasonable test, but in normal times the gas would be above that standard. The minimum would not be the maximum.</p>
        <p>Alderman Evans, ex-chairman of the Gas Committee, pointed out that the Gas'Committce had discussed this question very carefully be- fore they decided to make the application. In reply to the Inspector, Mr. Haddock said that there would be a saving of the price of cannel and also of benzol. The saving, calcu- lated on last year's figures, would be between C 2,000 and £3,000.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>PERTINENT QUERY.</head>
        <p>A PERTINENT QUERY.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Mclntyre asked Mr. Rallaway if, Owing to the saving in the cost.of manufacture, he would be, able to give an undertaking that the cost of gas to the consumer would be reduced?</p>
        <p>Mr. Hallaway: In the words of an extremely able statesman. I would ask Mr. Mclntyre to " wait and see."</p>
        <p>Mr. Haddock observed that the extra cost ti labour would absorb the saving in the cost o materials. '</p>
        <p>The inquiry Nyas then closed.</p>
        <p>A man tries to make a woman love him: a tonian trics to wake a man make her love him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>DANGER AND DELAYS</p>
        <p>There is no danger of the suppl% of lime being cut off owing to the war, but thera is a danger that owing to scarcity of labour and .lifficulties of transpoct supplies may not easily be cbtained when they are wanted, especially if farmers do not order early and leave„as, large a margin of time as possible for obtaining delivery. In many districts, of the country lime, whether in the form of carbonate or of burnt lime, is far too little used. In order to increase our crops</p>
        <p>during this time of., stringency and high prices, greatly increased amounts of carbonate of lime' and of burnt lime should be used over large parts of the country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>HUGE CANADIAN CROPS.</head>
        <p>HUGE CANADIAN CROPS.</p>
        <p>The final figures as to the Western crops, issued last week by the Canadian Pacific Rail- way, conSi.derably exceed even the most enthusi- astic predictions. The figures show: Wheat, 336,258,000 bushels; oats, 481,035,000; barley, 50,868,000; rye, 2,478,000; 'flax-seed 12,694,700; mixed grains, 17,128,500; corn for husking, 14,594,000; buckwheat, 8,101,000; peas, 3,240,000. And the quality is set down as first-class. It is estimated that when all Canadian needs have been supplied there will be 264,000,000 bushels of export wheat, which would require a fleet of over 1,000 vessels to convey it across the Atlantic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>OMNIVEROUS COW.</head>
        <p>THE OMNIVEROUS COW.</p>
        <p>A Missouri farmer returned home from town rather late, and, in the rush of work, he left a three-pound package of plug tobacco on the waggon seat. When he went after it early the next morning he found one of his .best much cows standing by the waggon, diligently chew- ing. The cow had eaten two and a half pounds of the tobacco. A veterinarian was summoned, but the cow died several hours afterwards. When the editor was a boy on the dairy farm near Bozeman one of the cows became suddenly ill. Everything possible was ,done for her, but she gradually declined and died. We had no veterinarian near in those days, but were fairly good at judging ordinary illnesses. This one, however, baffled us all. After' her death it was decided to hold a post-mortem. The county didn't employ a coroner in those days, so we were not even under the necessity of con- sulting him.. We found nothing .wrong inside until we opened the cow's stomach. In it were fifteen or twenty pounds of soap. Not long prior to the cow's becoming ill we had pur- chased a box of washing soap. For convenience it had been placed on a shelf on the rear wall of the buggy shed. The cow, had wormed her way in there and helped herself. Most animals are very careful about what they eat. Very seldom indeed will a horse -eat or drink that which will cause it harm. Not so with a cow steer. I have known them to drink cyanide solution when it was so strong they dropped dead without moving out of their tracks. A horse or a dog wouldn't take a drink of water oontaining even a bare .trace of cyanide. It is well to know these -things, about a cow, so that she may have an abundance of food without opportunity to szet that which is not good for her.--a North-Western Stockman and Farmer."</p>
        <p>AUTUMN DRESSINGS FOR WHEAT. The Board of Agriculture and Fisheries wish to impress upon farmers the desirability of top- dressing • wheat on soils of poor or medium quality with from to lewt. of sulphate of ammonia per acre during November or Decem- ber. A leaflet explaining the reasons for this recommendation, containing suggestions for the manuring of different soils will be sent post free on application to the Secretary, Board of Agriculture and Fisheries,. 4, Whitehall-place, London, S.W. In order to encourage the use of top-dressings the President of the Board of Agriculture and Fisheries .with the-concurrence of the Secretary for Scotland, has come to an agreement with manufacturers of sulphate of ammonia in Great Britain who have agreed 4-o reserve a definite proportion of their make in November and December for farmers' use.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>IMPORTANCE OF PRODUCING MORE</head>
        <p>THE IMPORTANCE OF PRODUCING MORE</p>
        <p>CHEESE.</p>
        <p>A special leaflet issued by the Board of Agri- culture deals with this question. It says that an increase in the production of cheese is recommended both because of the present high prices and also as a contribution to the -national food supply. Cheese is the form in which milk can best be preserved as a food; it is particu- larly suitable in• a diet on which prolonged physical work has to be performed, and it is a very good substitute for meat. Most of the present methods of using milk give rise to avoidable waste. - . -</p>
        <p>f. Milk Selling.—As a result of imperfect or- ganisation among farmers milk which' is in excess of what is required to fulfil the farmer's sale contract is of te. n put to .a comparatively unprofitable use. Even if the individual farmer did not himself, undertake "cliees-making, co- operative depots might enable large -quantities of surplus milk to be converted into cheese dn- ing the •"" flush " season (May and tune), and thus add to the food supply. 2. Butter-making.—Under present conditions the use of large quantities of milk for the pro- duction of butter i 3 wasteful, since wholesome and economical substitutes for butter are available, and butter contains less than half the food in the milk required for its manufac ture. Further, the replacement of butter by cheese would be profitable, af a consideration the following fairly typical example will show : 24 gall. milk produce 211 b. cheese at VW. 2 1 11b. butter at is. 2d. 1 2</p>
        <p>Profit in favour of cheese over butter Less the greater value of the by-products . - _</p>
        <p>from butter over cheese manufacture ... 0 3</p>
        <p>Net profit or, say 3d. per gallon of milk. _</p>
        <p>3. Calf Rearing.—The use of whole milk for calf rearing is to a large extent wasteful, since experiments have shown that early in the calf's life (say, from about the third week) whole milk may be gradually replaced by substitutes. In- formation as to the use of milk substitutes in calf rearing is given in leaflet No. 142.</p>
        <p>ou, the Keep, _ r Purse, shouk sake the acquaintance of th, sionest good value of 'Benefit Winter Footwear NOW! Fi. cast a critical eye over our Win dow-show, then step in an, enlist the aid of "Benefit,- the Public's staunch Ally in thr Thrift Campaign. Benefit</p>
        <p>stake in the country," not necessarily on any particular plan, but in a general sense. There seems to be a feeling current that nothing would •be so well calculated to continue the good and sympathetic understanding that has arisen between the different classes of the com- munity since and through the war as the re- settlement of the land on some well-conceived scheme that would satisfy the settlers on the one hand and call for no great outlay from the State on the other hand. The agricultural in- dustry at the end of the war will probably be one of the least affected by the temporary dis- location of industry, both at home and abroad, that is fairly certain to occur; and a careful scheme for the absorption of a large amount of labour in that industry would most likely result in the prevention of a good deal of distress, both among discharged soldiers and the civilian population who have to earn their living. it would be idle to pretend that it would be uneconomic; the nation that has hundreds of thousands of acres of poor grass and ether un- productive land and a large amount of avail- able labour, both of which conditions will pre- vail at the end of the war, would be committing an egregious folly if it did not develop• its re- sources by mean S of surplus labour. There is no reasoh why this plan should be uneconomic. I hope the deliberations of the coinmitree in question will not be unduly prolonged, as even when they have submitted their report to the Government, and supposing it to be accepted and adopted in its original or in a modified form, a good many details would have to be examined and arranged. Moreover, I hope the proposals which the committee will make will be both, bold and constructive, and such as will un- doubtedly lead to the national development of otfr agricultural resources.</p>
