      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>LAND AND RETURNED SOLDIERS.</head>
        <p>LAND AND RETURNED SOLDIERS.</p>
        <p>The committee that was appointed to mu- sider the settlenAnt on the land of returned soldiers and sailors has held several meetings and taken a good deal of evidence on the sub- ject. There is, says "3. L. G." in the "Standard," an ardent desire to give some practical expression to the well-worn phase "a</p>
        <p>An Invaluable Remedy for</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>Gee all vacant ground dug up and left in a rough state so that frost can pulverise and sweeten it. Go over plants of pentstemons, calceolarias, snap-dragons, &amp;c., before discard- ing to the rubbish heap, and fill up spare frames with cuttings. Insert in light soil and cover glass with sacks. or a simple plan, scatter soil over glass for a few days till they riso up erect. Gather all leaves and carefully store away, scattering salt over them to ensure rotting. In- sert cuttings of privet in sandy soil and press firmly, these will be nicely rooted for spring and can be moved if neeessary. Dress the tops of rose beds with-well decayed manure. This must only be done to established roses, straw litter will do for newly-planted beds. Lift gladiolas with soil adhering, name and tie up and store away in .a cool, dry place till "spring. Montbretias can be lifted and the largest bulbs planted 6in, apart, finer blooms being obtained in this, way than constantly ,grown in clhmps, the smaller bulbs filling in odd corners to grow on to larger size. Cut down early flowering 'chrysanthemums within 6in. off the ground and prepare frame to receive cuttings of the choicer. kinds in December and January. This snap of cold weather has started the sparrow to bite the centres out of carnations, therefore set traps for them or put stretches of black cotton around and over them. , A good plan, after the war is over, would be to kill off this troublesome pest, then better results in gar- dens can be expected. top-dress strawberry beds and rhubarb w;th well-rotted manure, and people intending to force rhubarb should adopt the ft lowing method: Dig up as many roots as required, large plump roots, leave on ground a few Jays to get frosted, then put on a hot bed of manure, cfrier- ing with a layer of soil and keeping perfectly dark and warm, and.a few sticks will be thrown up in a very short time. The idea is to keep the plants in darkness and suceesq is bound to result.</p>
        <p>CEVO.</p>
        <p>A Lecture. "I can't praise temperance more effectively," says Booker T. Washington, "than in the words of old Uncle Calhoun Webster, of Nola Chuckey. " ' When I sees a man,' said Uncle Calhoun— 'when I sees.a man a-goin' home wid a gallon o' whisky an' a half pound o' meat, dat's tem- perance lecture enough for me--an' I sees it ebery day. An', I knows dat eberyt'ing in dat man's house am on de same scale—a gallon a' misery to ebery half pound o' comfort.' "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
