      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>COUNCILLOR OSBORNE'S "RESIGNATION."</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR OSBORNE'S "RESIGNATION."</p>
        <p>• We do not propose to follow the example of the Warrington Town Council and, in discuss- ing the resignation of Councillor Osborne, in- dulge in strong language. With Alderman Tinnion, we believe the case has not reached the stage at which it is wise to use strong lan- guage. It may be that "the institution" which employs Mr. Osborne May have a defence, and it is always unwise, to say noth- ing of unsafe, to dispose of any dispute when only one set of arguments are available. Still, on the face. of it, it appears that, in the cir- cumstances, discretion would have been better served had the parties concerned held their hand till a less distracted, and therefore more reasonable, time. To ask a man to give up his public duties when the country is literally panting for the services of its sons, seems to suggest, as the Mayor said, that the methods of reasoning in this ease have gone somewhat awiy. It was stated—and we believe it to be absolutely true—that all the time Councillor Osborne ha i devoted to his public duties has been his own leisure time. If that is so, where does the right of anyone to interfere arise? -We hope Conncillor Osborne and the gentlemen who manage the institution of which he has been a faithful servant for a long number of years, will be able to adjust matters without friction and in a fashion be- fitting these awkward times. It would be a profitless task to attempt to paint the lily, but in the short time Councillor Osborne has been one of ov local governors he has proved himself to be in'many respects the best coun- cillor we have. Independent and fearless, and especially efficient in the domain of figures, he has been the one solitary critic of our trading Concerns, the one man who has given the town information on subjects that few are able, if willing, -to give. The, present is a time when the public service cannot afford to shed even its mediocrities, much less can it afford to lose those of real ability. It is for reasons such as these that we hope an accommodation—we might even say a patriotic accommodation—may be reached, and that the further stage hinted at by the Mayor may be avoided. If the Council re-elect Mr. Osborne, and throw the onus of his withdrawal from public life directly upon his employers, a very regrettable stage indeed will--have been 'reached.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 13, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 13, 1915.</p>
        <p>Wm. Chadwick, and Mr. Wm. Rustan'e, which won the Easter Saturday race on the Mersey about seven years ago. When on furlough he was a member of the• crew of the lona, another Mersey yacht which he assisted to win several races. At Gibraltar he was a leader or captain in all branches of sport, his experience rang- ing from being in the boxing finals on "The Rock" to having a leg broken in a football match there.</p>
        <p>Bombardier Chadwick holds Army Certificates as a first-class gunlayer, marksman, telegra- phist, signaller and swimmer, and second-class education certificate.</p>
        <p>He was educated at Sankey day school, and attended Wesleyan Sunday School and Chapel, 'Sankey Bridges. He is a member of • the R.A.0.8. "Tom ' Sangster" Lodge. His widowed mother, prouder than ever of him now, continues to reside at 160, Liverpool-road.</p>
        <p>THE BOMBARDIER'S STORY. COULD NOT BE PROUDER IF I WERE THE KING.</p>
        <p>In a letter to his mother, dated November 6th, Bombardier Chadwick says how he received the French honour, though he does not say how he won it. It ma kbe presumed, however, that he has done some speoial service for our gallant Allies; it may also be presumed that some British recognition awaits him. "Last night," says the letter, "the Major sent for me and told me that, tI had been awarded the French Medaille Militaire, and gave me instructions to get new clothes and boots, to get spick and span for the great event. Well, at 9.30 this morning our Colonel came for me iq his motor ear and we went off miles away forlthe presentation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>CANVASSERS!</head>
        <p>CANVASSERS!</p>
        <p>Huge Task Still Remains</p>
        <p>Undone.</p>
        <p>WILL YOU VOLUNTEER?</p>
        <p>Interviewed as to the progress which is being made locally under Lord Derby's scheme, the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacook)—whose ser- vices in the cause of recruiting have been un- remitting, and such as will earn him a lasting niche in local history,-as affected by the war, when it comes to be written s—informed an "Examiner" representative that he was far from satisfied, in the present serious crisis, that so few people had thought it worth their while, in their country's interest,' to come and give a hand in the task of seeking out the eligible men—men who were well able to go and fight for their country and who ought to be going to do so. "Some of the members of the Council, at Tuesday's meeting," added his Worship, "gave the eligible men of the town a splendid lead, and I now hope that it will not be necessary, on November 30th, for War- rington to say that, by reason of the slackness of the canvassers—or, rather, of those who are well able to come and canvass, if they would— and the apathy of the young men, the old town was unable to give Lord Derby's scheme a proper trial. Does Warrington want Con- scription? If not, are the eligible men of the town going to come forward voluntarily and play their part in the preservation of the Voluntary system? And are more canvassers coming forward to help to bring home to the eligibles their duty at the present moment?" A TASK FOR THE INELIGIBLES.</p>
        <p>The Mayor thinks that everybody who is ineligible for service ought to devote some time to this work of canvassing the eligibles. It is a big task, and, although a large part of it has been got through, there's still plenty to do for many more workers than are available. At present it is being left to a comparatively few people to do more than their fair share towards "finding the men who will stand up for their country in her hour of trial." "It is," said his Worship, "hardly fair tg, leave it to some fifty people to make this attempt to .grapple with the big task which has been set the local committee; in fact, it is less than hardly fair, for it is distinctly unfair, seeing that there are so many who could lend a hand, if they would."</p>
        <p>His Worship added that there were some- thing like 5,500 men to be seen in the town and district when; Lord Derby's scheme was set in operation, and that, up to yesterday • morning, the local authorities had.only received reports concerning about 250 of the eligibles. It would be seen how much remains to be done, when it is stated that the campaign had been going on for about a fortnight and that only three weeks remain in which to accomplish the work still outstanding. When that fact is borne in mind the seriousness of the situation at once becomes apparent, and it is pretty obvious that it is up to such over-military-age Warrington- ians as have not already done so to hasten forward and come and do their "bit" in the task of filling up the gaps in the ranks, and swelling the grand total of those whom posterity will regard as the heroes who ven- tured all to save the prestige of the greatest Empire the world has ever known.</p>
        <p>CANVASSING NEITHER DIFFICULT NOR UNPLEASANT.</p>
        <p>The canvassing is not difficult work, .nor is it unpleasant work; for the canvassers report that they have uniformly—save in one solitary instance—been courteously received. There has been found a not unnatural disposition oh the part of the married men to suggest that it is a case of "single men first." "Starred" men have not been canvassed, nor will they be; the only men upon whom the canvassers will call being those who so far as can be judged—by reason of age, &amp;c.—come within the scope of Lord Derby's scheme. Of course, age is not an infallible indication as to a man's fitness and ability to serve, and men within the prescribed age limit may well have good and sufficient reasons why they have not offered. Such men would save the local officials a deal of time and trouble if they would call at the office, 3, Horsemarket-street—it cannot be , missed !—and give their reasons why they 'cannot "join um" By so doing they would greatly facilitate the completion of the canvass.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>NOT A MATTER FOR THE EMPLOYER.</head>
        <p>NOT A MATTER FOR THE EMPLOYER.</p>
        <p>It was mentioned to our representative by one of the officials at the office that' cases had been heard of in which eligible men said that they were forbidden by their employers to offer their services. Such men are reminded that the matter is not one for the employers; it being for the local Tribunal to decide, when the canvassers' report has been made, as to whether the men • can be spared from their work now, or should be put into a later class. Many of the men who have been canvassed, of course, have replied that they are working on munitions, and, for the moment, of course, these men, though otherwise eligible, constitute something. of a problem. Reverting to the matter of the married men who think it should be a case of "after the single chaps," it is worth while pointing out that they have the Prime Minister's pledge that they will not be called upon to serve until the young unmarried men have first been rallied to the Colours. Consequently, married men, of the requisite ages, should have no hesitation in enlisting in their respective grades, resting assured that they will not be called upon until all the single men have been accounted for.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>RECRUITING QUESTIONS.</head>
        <p>RECRUITING QUESTIONS.</p>
        <p>Composition Of Local Judiciary Tribunal.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Town Council held on Tuesday morning formally gave its approval to the appointment of the 'Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock), the Deputy-Mayor (Councillor Joseph), Alderman Tinnion, and Messrs. F. A. Frost and J. J. Whitley to con- stitute the local tribunal "to consider and adjudicate upon questions that may arise from time to time in connection with recruiting for His Majesty's Forces, in accordance with rules and regulations which may be framed from time to time by His Majesty's Government."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>TRADES COUNCIL'S ATTITUDE.</head>
        <p>TRADES COUNCIL'S ATTITUDE.</p>
        <p>Recruiting A Matter For The</p>
        <p>Individual.</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of the Warrington Trades and -Labour Council,, held on 'Tuesday evening, sUpport was given toi•a resolution of the Executive Committee to the effect that the Council reaffirmed its previous policy in remaining -.neutral with regard to recruiting, such policy, it being considered, left, the dele- gates, and the Unions they represented, open to take any course they might individually choose. -The Executive Committee' arrived at their decision after receiving a. letter from the local Recruiting Committee .asking the Coun- cil to assist in canvassing the " unstarred " men and after the matter, it was reported,' had</p>
        <p>been discussed for an hour and three- -quaiters</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>SOUTH AFRICANS FOR FRANCE</head>
        <p>SOUTH AFRICANS FOR FRANCE</p>
        <p>Contingent In England.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel-Maitland, replying, to Sir E. C;•rn- wall, in the House of Commons on Wednesday r.i6ht, said' :the Government of the Union if South Africa had sent an official contingent for service in Europe, and Iloy wore now trvining in England. (Cheers.) VICAR OF BURTONWOOD'S SON.</p>
        <p>The eldest son of the .a,3v. A. M. Mitchell, vicar of Burtonwood, has just reac.lo.l England, along with others of- the south African con- tingent, including the eldest son of Captain Tilbrook, of Vryburg, a youth of eighteen, who served through the recent German South-West African campaign as a signaller.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>MAYORAL WHIST DRIVE.</head>
        <p>MAYORAL WHIST DRIVE.</p>
        <p>A Mayoral whist drive and dance wi?l be held at the Parr Hall on December 16th. The proceeds will be devoted to a fund to provide tobacco for local soldiers at the Front, and also for the Working .Men's Infirmary Committee. A simi- lar whist drive and dance was held last winter, and proved to be a great success.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>All The Generals Were There!</head>
        <p>All The Generals Were There!</p>
        <p>"All the high Generals, English and French were there. I never saw so many Generals in my life. It made me nervous, and I was wish- ing it was over. Well, the Commander in Chief of the First Army, General Sir Douglas Haig, made the presentation. He made ce Speech saying how proud he was to shake my hand. Then hp pinned the medal on xny left breast.'Al) the officers came and shook my, hand and con- gratulated me. Then the Colonel took me home in the car, and I am the hero of the hour:. no matter where I go. Of course, as I write the chaps are coming np a few at a time to see the medal. I have it in its case now, but I will let them all see it before I send it home to you.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>" For Valour "—Like Our V.C.</head>
        <p>" For Valour "—Like Our V.C.</p>
        <p>"It 'is the most beautiful medal I have ever seen, all gold and silver, with silk orange and green ribbon. On the back is a French inscrip- tion "Valour and Discipline." It is the greatest honour the French can bestow on a non-commissioned officer. It is their V.C. No Garrison Artilleryman has had the luck to win it up to now, and if I were the King of Eng- land I couldn't; be any prouder than I am now. I am the hero with both French and English people. "I am coming home on leave in a week or two, and when I do it will be a proud day for me and for odr family. Fancy the greatest honour the French can give and a Chad get- ting it! It does not seem possible, does it? But it is true, although I have to keep looking at my medal to make sure it's right.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>" That's AU!"</head>
        <p>" That's AU!"</p>
        <p>"Well you will want to know why the medal was awarded to me. It is for bravery and devotion to duty at Ypres and Loos. That's all; nothing much, is it? I have worked hard and tried for honours by risking my life lots of times. If anything had to be done through fire I have chanced it. My life has been in the balance dozens of times—through shot and shell. I have never told you this for fear of upsetting you. God has been good; He has pulled me through these dangers. I have prayed for these times and' He has answered my prayers. Where I may have been killed a hundred times over, He has pulled me through. I will send home the medal when all the battery has seen it, for fear it gets lost. It must cost 'at least £lO. It is all gold and silver, and the design is great. You can ask, Bill if he can get any ribbon like it or a bar for me to wear. They never gave me a ribbon because the French always wear the medal."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>"OUR DAY." Over £350 Realised By Local Efforts.</head>
        <p>"OUR DAY." Over £350 Realised By Local Efforts.</p>
        <p>"Our Day" in Warrington and district was a decided financial success, no less a sum than £357 15s. sd. being raised by the various efforts put forth, and forwarded to headquarters in London to help to swell the joint fund of the British Red Cross Society and the St. John Ambulance Brigade, who have joined forces in order that they may the better carry on their noble work of succouring the gallant soldiers and sailors wounded in fighting their country's battles. Warrington and the township of the district are grouped together to form what is known as the Daresbury Division, and it is to that division that the total quoted above is credited. The sums raised in the different. dis- tricts comprising the division were:—</p>
        <p>2 s. d. Stretton and Appleton Daresbury Latchford Whitley Grappenhall • Stockton. Heath Warrington</p>
        <p>Total</p>
        <p>The usual monthly intercession service was held in the Bold-street Wesleyan Church on Thursday evening, when there was a fair attendance. An address was delivered, by the Rev. H. Ogden (secretary to the Council). Mr. J. C. Holland presided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Mortality Statistics.</head>
        <p>Mortality Statistics.</p>
        <p>During the week -ended November 6th 31 births were registered in the borough, and 22 deaths. The figures in the corresponding week of last year were 35 and 26 respectively. Seven- teen of the 22 deaths were those of persons bi- tween the ages of 25 and 65.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>New Insurance Committeemen.</head>
        <p>New Insurance Committeemen.</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of the Warrington Insurance Committee, held at the Town Hall on Thursday evening, Dr. Murray was appointed a member, in place of Dr. Sutherland, resigned; and Mr. E. Burke (135, Sankey-street) a mem- ber in room of Mr. T. H. Ash Croft, also resigned. Death of Mrs. Sarah J. Knox.</p>
        <p>We have to recbrd the death of Mrs. Sarah Jane Knox—widow of the late Mr. Thomas Knox and sister of the late Mr. Joseph Whit- field, of Lower Walton—who passed away at her home, 26, Wilderspool-causeway, on Wed- nesday. She was in her 78th year. The funeral takes place to-day at the Warrington Cemetery.</p>
        <p>Next Year's Town Council Meetings. At the meeting of the Warrington Town Coun- cil, held on Tuesday, it was decided to hold the quarterly meetings of the Council during the next twelve months on the first Tuesday in February, 1916, the first Tuesday in May, and the first Tuesday in August. The monthly meet- ings were fixed as follows: December 7, 1915; January 4, 1916; March ‘7, April 4, June 6, July 4," September 5, October 3 and 17.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>To Represent The Council.</head>
        <p>To Represent The Council.</p>
        <p>The followink were appointed representatives of the Council on the various bodies named, at Tuesday's meeting of the Town Council :—Lan- cashire Asylums Board, Alderman James Evans; Lancashire Inebriates' Acts Board, Alderman James Smethurst; County Rate Joint Com- mittee, Alderman Bennett ; and Lancashire and Western Sea Fisheries Board, Alderman Pier- point.</p>
        <p>, Photographic Society. An exhibition of colour-slides made by mem- bers of the Warrington Photographic Society was held in the Old Academy on Tuesday, a splendid collection being got together, the prin- cipal exhibitors being Messrs. J. Harding, sea., Bosco°, Frost and Rowlands. Lieutenant H. Holloway, who was for some years secretary to . .</p>
        <p>the society, was present at the exhibition and was accorded a hearty welcome by the members.</p>
        <p>New Education Committeemen.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Town Council, held on Tuesday morning, it was decided, on the recommendation of the Association of Roman Catholic Elementary Schools within the borough, that the Rev. T. A. Hind be appointed a co-optative member on the Education Com- mittee in place of the Rev. Father Neville Wilson, resigned; also that Mr. C. H. Tinsworth be reappointed a co-optative member.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Ward Returning Officers.</head>
        <p>Ward Returning Officers.</p>
        <p>The following Aldermen were, at Tuesday's meeting of the Town Council, appointed to act as returning officers for the different wards : Town Hall, Alderman J. Smethurst; Whitecross, Alderman W. Pierpoint ; Bewsey, Alderman T. Burton; Orford, Alderman James Evans; St. John's, Alderman D. Tinnion ; . Fairfield, Alder- man P. Peacock,; Howley,- Alderman John Evans; St. Austin's, Alderman A. Bennett; and Latchford, Alderman W. Bolton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>Lady Peter Walker's Thanks.</head>
        <p>Lady Peter Walker's Thanks.</p>
        <p>At • Tuesday's meeting of the Town Council, the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) announced that a letter had been received from Lady Walker, thanking the Council for their vote of condolence with her and her family in the recent loss they have sustained by the death of Sir Peter Walker, the head of the big local brewery firm which • bears his name. The letter further requested his Worship to inform the Council that • her ladyship was very grateful for their condolences.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Wednesday's Market Prices.</head>
        <p>Wednesday's Market Prices.</p>
        <p>Wheat was .firmer on Wednesday's Warring- ton Market, prices ruling from 7s. 9d. to 7s. 10d. per 751 b. Oath were better sold and well looked after, 4s. 6d. to 4s. Bd. per 451 b. being realised. Straw has had a big lift. Quantities are being used for cattle fodder owing to the high price of hay. Good oat bales fetched £3 per ton. Wheat straw bales realised 555. per ton. Flour remained firm at late rates, viz., 425. per 2801 b. for XX grade. Bran and other dials remained very firm. llay fetched £6 15s. per ton. Eggs !itold at three and four for Is.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Brick-street Mission Choir 'Sermons.</head>
        <p>Brick-street Mission Choir 'Sermons.</p>
        <p>The first annual choir sermons were preached at the Brick-street MissiOn on Sunday naorning and evening, by Mr. I3oyd, of St. Helens. In the afternoon the choir, under the leadership of Mr. A. Morris, rendered the sacred cantata, "The King of Glory," having the assistance cf a small orchestra composed of members of the Mission and friends. At the .evening service the choir gave the anthems "I will extol Thee" and "Praise the Lord." Satisfactory collec- tions were taken on behalf of the choir funds</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Death of Mr. Thomas Rowse.</head>
        <p>Death of Mr. Thomas Rowse.</p>
        <p>A highly-respected personality in the busi- ness life of the district has been removed by the death, at Llandudno, of Mr. Thomas Rowse, managing director of the Warrington and. Latchfoid Steam Laundry Co., Ltd. Mr. Rowse, who leaves a widow and two children and resided at Silver Mead, York-drive, Grap- penhall, had been ailing for about two years and entirely away from business for three months. The funeral took place at Blackpool Cemetery on ThesdaY, the service being con- ducted at the Swedenborg Church by the Rev. W. T. Stonestreet. '</p>
        <p>The business of the company will be con- tinued as usual at the Stafford-road and Thelwall-lane Laundries.</p>
        <p>Do You Want To Change Your Panel</p>
        <p>Doctor ?</p>
        <p>The attention of insured persons is called to the notice in our advertisement columns re- specting change of doctor at the end of the year. Those who wish to make a change of doctor must give notice in writing to the Clerk to the Insurance Committee, and must also surrender their present medical card. In order to protect, as far as possible, the lists of those doctors who are on active service with His Majesty's Forces, anyone wishing to transfer from the list of such a doctor will require to furnish the Clerk with a full statement as to the reasons why they desire to sci change. The Medical Service Sub-committee will then decide as to whether the appliCation should be granted or not. The n'otices must be sent to the Clerk before the .Ist December. .In the absence of such notices insured persons will be deemed to have decided to remain on their present doc- tors' lists for another twelve months.</p>
        <p>Stock Medicines for Insured Persons. Al the monthly meeting of the Warrington Insurance Committee, held on Thursday even- ing, under the chairmanship of the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock), Mr. Peter Delaney, on reporting at considerable length on his and Mr. Tilling's visit as delegates to the annual conference of the National Association of Ilk surance Committees, held in London on Octo- ber 14th and 15th, said that among the ques- tions dealt with was that of stock mixtures. It had, he said, repeatedly .been suggested from many quarters that the introduction of a recognised national system of stock mixtures which could be prescribed by ,panel practi- tioners in all cases in which drugs, or com- bination of drugs, could be kept,' without deterioration or,detriment, in bulk, would not only mean a considerable reduction in the work or the chexnist, but would, in addition, provide a means of economising. Stock mixtures when made up by wholesale houses, in large quan- tities, it was further stated, were more effective and reliable than when the mixture was pre- pared in small quantities by local pharmacists. —Messrs. Delaney and Tilling were thanked for their services, on the proposition of .Mr. J. F. Foster, seconded by Mr. 3. 8. 11111, the Tatter remarking with regard to stock medicines there was always a danger that the disease might be made to fit the medicine rather 'than the medi- cine fitting the disease.</p>
        <p>" - •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>May Link Up!</head>
        <p>May Link Up!</p>
        <p>It was reported- by Mr. Charles Dukes, at the monthly meeting of the Trades and Labour Council, held on Tuesday evening, that he had addressed the members of the local branch of the Associated Society of Locomotive Engineers and Firemen and that he thought, as a con- sequence, they might in the near future ally themselves with the Council.</p>
        <p>Trades Council and "Co-op." Society.</p>
        <p>At their monthly meeting on Tuesday night, the delegates to the Warrington and District Trades Council decided to recommend Mr. F. Berry (of the Carpenters' and Joiners' Union) and Mr. G. Haley (of the Gas Workers' Uniod) for the support of Trades Unionists in their efforts to secure seats on the committee of the local Co-operative Society as representing the Trade Union movement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Re-" elected" Without Election.</head>
        <p>Re-" elected" Without Election.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Town Council, held on Tuesday morning, the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) reported that the term of office of eaoh member of the Council had, owing, to the war, been extended by one year without the necessity of the usual• elections. The Coun- cillors who would, in the ordinary course, have come out of office on November Ist, will there- fore hold their seats unchallenged for another twelve months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Trades Council's Finances.</head>
        <p>Trades Council's Finances.</p>
        <p>,The annual balance sheet was submitted at the monthly meeting of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council on Tuesday evening. It showed a total income of £l5B 17s. 71d., and balance in hand oV 221 2s. sd. One item of expenditure was that of £24 4s. 9d., representing the cost of the abortive election fights under- taken by the Council's candidates at the muni- cipal elections a twelvemonth ago, and, when reading out this item, Mr. Charles Dukes (the secretary to the Council) remarked "It's time you started laughing," and the delegates, good humouredly, did so.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Friendly Society's Man_ On The Educa-</head>
        <p>Friendly Society's Man_ On The Educa-</p>
        <p>tion - Committee.</p>
        <p>A meeting representative of the Friendly Societies of the town was held in the Town Hall on Monday, the purpose being the selec- tion of a gentleman to be recommended to the Education Committee as a co-optative member of that body. Bro. T. P. Bradshaw, P.P.G.M., was in the chair. It was unanimously decided to recommend Bro. W. E. Clegg, commercial clerk, of 3, Rolleston-street, for the appoint- ment. Bro. Clegg is a Past Officer and a very active member of the Ancient Order of Shep- herds, and is at present employed by Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons.</p>
        <p>For Consumptive Scholars. Giving his report to the Trades and Labour Council, as the representative of that body on the local Education Committee, Mr. E. Plin- stoni on Tuesday night, said that the Medical Inspection Sub-committee duly received the resolution from the Council with 'reference to tho provision of a building for the treatment of consumptive school children. Every member of the sub-committee present agreed with and endorsed the Trades CounciVs suggestion, and steps were taken with a view to finding a site. Ile supposed, however, that, at the present juncture, the question of expense would crop up and delay matters, but it was something to think that the committee considered such Provision necessary.</p>
        <p>To Represent Local Labourites. The Waralngtou Trades and Labour Council decided at their meeting on Tuesday night to send their secretary (Mr. Charles Dukes) as their delegate to the next annual conference of the Independent Labour Party, which is to be held at Bristol. The Council have never before sent a delegate to the eonference, because, as Mr. Dukes explained, they had not hitherto been in a position to afford to do so.—ln thank- ing the Council for appointing him, Mr. Dukes said that the policy of the Labour Party would be the policy round which every social conflict during the next decade would circle. There would be a real and effective Labour Party at the; end of the war, because then the workers would have something tangible, something that came home to them, to light for.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Night Out.</head>
        <p>A Night Out.</p>
        <p>It was reported to the Warrington Insurance Committee on Thursday night that the Sana- torium Benefit 'Sub-committee had had before them a letter from an approved society stating that one of the members who was receiving institutional treatment at Sankey Sanatorium had been seen hf Warrington at 10.15 p.m. on Saturday, the 4th September, and asking if permission had been given. The medical adviser stated that he had made inquiries into the case and found that the man had left the instittitiOn without his knowledge or that of the nursing staff. The patient in . question had returned to the sanatorium in the early hours of the following morning and managed to obtain admittance to the building without de- tection. It was resolved, "That. .the clerk communicate with the insured person in ques- tion warning him that, if he again broke the rules of the institution, the committee would be unable to give him any further eanatoriura benefit."</p>
        <p>Why He Did Not Resign. Reporting on Tuesday evening to the Trades and Labour Council, Mr. E. Plinston—that body's representative on the Warrington Edu- cation Committee—made reference to the recent controversy between the Health Committee of the Town Council and the Medical Inspection Sub-committee of the Education Com- mittee as to which body—Council or Committee?—should have the appointment of a dental officer for the schools. As a result of that 'controversy, said Mr. Plinston, there had been so many resignations from the sub-com- mittee that he believed .he was the only. co- opted memberikho still remained on it. He did not resign because he held the view that he was appointed by the Trades Council to sit on that committee with a definite purpose in view, and that was to do the best he could, in a physical sense, for the workers' children. He disagreed with the Health Committee—who claimed the right to make the appointment, although the Medical Inspection Sub-committee would be responsible for the 'work of the officer—but he did not. carry his disagreement to the length of retirement.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Brotherhood. There was a good rally at the Pleasant Sunday afternoon service held in connection with the Wycliffe Brotherhood at the Wycliffe Congregational Church. Mr. Ben Farrar pre- sided and in the course of a few introductory remarks welcomed Privates Bond and Spencer and expressed to them the good wishes of the members of the Brotherhood.—The Rev. C. P. Pitt gave the address on the subject of 7 Good Work," remarking that to do good was quite a separate thing from unreality—it was to confer some definite boon. The Pharisees showed how unreal they were, depending upon outward shows, forms and ceremonies: St. Paul called upon the Romans to act upon the Spirit of Brotherhood, telling them that they had been looking at, the wrong source for help, whereupon they realised their own helplessness. But St. Paul drew them out after Christ, so that their lives would become beautiful and helpful in good works, and thus help on the Brotherhood of Man. There were many things to-day which tried to draw the peopleaway from God,and they must always look to Him to help them to over- come every impediment, striving always for the Spirit and Mind of Christ.—Mr. C. Cotton read the lesson and Mr. Payton presided at the organ.—One new member received a hearty welcome.—A vote a 'sympathy was passed with Mrs. Kent, of 42, Hoyle-street, whose son, 'Tarry, has Jdied in France as the result of wounds. Sympathy was also expressed with Mrs. Y. Davies, of Padgate-lane, in the Toss she has sus- -tallied by the 'death, from canter, of her litts-- band.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Mrs. Osborne 111.</head>
        <p>Mrs. Osborne 111.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Osborne, the indefatigable hon. secretary of the Warrington Women's Liberal Associa- tion, is, we regret to say, confined to her room owing to illness, and is, consequently, unable to fulfil her usual engagements.</p>
        <p>Getting* Colder. The mean temperature during the week ended November 6th was 38 degrees, or 5 degrees colder than in the previous week. The warmest day recently was the 31st ult., when 53 degrees were registered. The minimum temperature was 24 degrees, recorded on November 2nd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Special Services At Christ Church.</head>
        <p>Special Services At Christ Church.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Canon Morley Stevenson will preach at Matins to-morrow at Chr:st Church, Latch- ford. The Vicar (the Rev. G. C. Briggs) will preach at evensong. The collections will be handed over to the Sunday school fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>Scarlet' Fever "Season."</head>
        <p>Scarlet' Fever "Season."</p>
        <p>The back-end of the year is the season when the baccilli of scarlet fever most " doth stalk abroad." The said baccilli is busy in War- rington at the present moment, and there are under treatment in the borough hospital 108 patients suffering from this disease.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>Late Mr. Hey's Successor.</head>
        <p>Late Mr. Hey's Successor.</p>
        <p>Mr. Arthur Duckworth, 56; Gorsey-lane, has been appointed manager of the continuous wire- drawing department at the works of Messrs. Rylands Bros., in charge of the late Mr. H. S. Hey. Mr. Duckworth has been engaged in the department for seven years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Emmanuel Church Sale of Work.</head>
        <p>Emmanuel Church Sale of Work.</p>
        <p>The annual sale of work in aid of the funds of Emmanuel Church will be held in the Church Institute, Knutsford=road, on Wednesday, Thursday and Friday next. On Wednesday the sale will be opened by Mr. Percy W. Thompson, on Thursday by Mr. Thos. Heyward, and on Friday by the Rev. W. T. Jaye.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>Bible Society's Meeting.</head>
        <p>Bible Society's Meeting.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Auxiliary of the British and Foreign Bible Society will be held on Thursday evening, November 25th, in the Co-operative Hall. Cairo-street. The Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) will preside, and addresses will be given by the Rev. H. C. Moore (vicar of Earles- town) and the Rev. Tom Dring, the newly- appointed district secretary.</p>
        <p>Bewsey-road Wesleyans' Missionary</p>
        <p>Anniversary.</p>
        <p>The missionary anniversary of the Bewsey- road Wesleyan Church will be held to-morrow. The Rev. W. T. Derry will preach in the morn- ing, and the Rev. 0. J. Griffin at night. Mr. Griffin will also give an address at a public meeting on Monday night.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>list Drive and Dance for Red Cross</head>
        <p>list Drive and Dance for Red Cross</p>
        <p>Funds.</p>
        <p>Following the patriotic example set by various organisations throughout the district, Mr. F. Wilson Wood, principal of Wood's College, has decided to hold a social re- union of students and their friends at the Cairo- street Schools, Warrington, on Thursday next, 18th inst., commencing at 7 o'clock. The whole of the Proceeds will be given to the local fund of the British Red Cross Society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>Mayor's Sunday Procession.</head>
        <p>Mayor's Sunday Procession.</p>
        <p>• To-morrow is Mayor's Sunday, and the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) will attend divine service at the Parish Church in the morning. Members of the Friendly Societies who intend accompanying him are requested to assemble on the lawn in front of the Town Hall not later than 9.45 a.m., and to proceed to the Parish Church. The marshals of each Society are• re- quested to be in attendance not later than 9.30. Regalia should be worn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>Musical Society To Perform " Merrie</head>
        <p>Musical Society To Perform " Merrie</p>
        <p>England."</p>
        <p>The Warrington Musical Society will hold a patriotic concert at the Parr Hall on Saturday. November 27th. Edward Germans' 'Merrie England" (concert version) has been selected for peqormance. The plan of the reserved seats will be opened to the public on and after Saturday, November 20th, at Mr. Pearson's. Bridge-street.. Tickets for unreserved seats may be had at Messrs. Dawson's, Sankey-street, Mr. Pearson's, or from any member of the Musical Society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>Gifts For Aged Women.</head>
        <p>Gifts For Aged Women.</p>
        <p>Carrying out the bequest of the late Mr. Thomas Morris, 62 blankets and 27 petticoats were on Tuesday distributed to respectable Old women of the borough, at the office of Mr. Arthur Browne (solicitor, Sankey-street), who is the corresponding trustee under the will of the deceased benefactor. The oldest recipient was in her 80th year.. Mr. Browne's co-trustees in the administration of this charity are the Rector of Warrington, the Rector of St. Mary's R.C. Church, and the Rev. T. T. James, of the Wycliffe church. Foregone Licence-Fee.</p>
        <p>When application was made at the Borough Police Court on Wednesday morning for a tem- porary licence to sell. intoxicatinv ILurs at the forthcoming sale aid of The Agricultural Relief of Allies Fund and the British Farmers' Red Crass Fund—which sale is to be held on the Frewery Field, Wilderspool, on November lsth —it was suggested to the magistrates by their clerk (Mr George Whito That seeing the objects it was sought to aid, they might forego the usual fee, and their Worships ulianimeintly acquiesced in the suggestion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Late Mrs. Peter Leigh.</head>
        <p>The Late Mrs. Peter Leigh.</p>
        <p>The funeral of the late Mrs. Peter Leigh, whose husband for many years carried on busi- ness at Market Gate as a grocer and wine and spirit dealer, . took place at the Cemetery on SaParday. The prigcipal mourners were : lfessrg. John , and Herbert Leigh (sons), Rowland and Roger WO (grandsons), and Mr. John Whitlow. Alderman Pitryo.iutz an old friend, was unable to attend owing to indisposition. The Rev. E. C. E. Carleton was the officiating minister. Wreaths were sent by the following :—" Her Loving Children, Thomas Whitlow (Carlisle), Mr. and Mrs. Wil- liam Pierpoint and family, Miss Elizabeth Pierpoint, Mr. and Mrs. Whitham and family (Chester), Mr. E. W. Pierpoint, "From Her Grandchildren" (Hanover-street), "From Her Grandchildren" (Woodlands, Latchford), Mr. George Kenyon and family (Manchester), Mr. and Mrs. Robinson, Mrs. Randall and "Marie," Fred Randall and "Ella," Mr. Richard Delaney, "Ada and Kitty Birtles," Mr. and Mrs. Mainwaring, Mrs. Ferguson and family, Mr. and Mrs. Spencer Wallhead, Mr. and Mrs. Hugh Greenough, Mr. and Mrs. J. E. Birtles, "Dora Foster-Jackson and Lily Brough," Mrs. Finney and family, Miss Burdett, "Mary and Cissy Burrows," Mr.. Hem n (Dundalk), Mr. and Mrs. Edward Burrqws, Mr. and Mrs. Shake- shaft, "Ada Philips," "Tom and Nell," Mr. and Miss Harding, Mr. and Mrs. J. Houghton, "Annie Ward," Mr. T. and the Misses Woods,: the Misses Pierpoint (Palmyra-square), Mr. and Mrs. W. Hoult, "Alice, Leonard and Murphy,".. "Powell and Annie," "Her Neighbours." The funeral arrangements were carried out by Mr. H. P. Greenough. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>COUNCILLOR OSBORNE'S RESIGNATION.</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR OSBORNE'S RESIGNATION.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>• Sir,—A wrong impression, which I feel coin. pelled to remove at once, has been created by the introduction of the name of Councillor Henry Roberts into the discussion of Tuesday last.</p>
        <p>I wish to state, emphatically, that Mr. Roberts was in no way a party to my resigna- tion front ,the. Council. Yours truly, EDMUND OSBORgE, 22;11'01dt-street, Warrington, 12th November, 1915.</p>
        <p>STCONDARY SCHOOL "'OLD BOITS.?'</p>
        <p>FinT4 ToVrOVide Those' At Front With Gifts.,</p>
        <p>The .Ivrincipal. of iii;TWarrington SeCondaff School will be •glad to receive suhscriptione: front past pupils and friends of the school ta' the fund, for providing a. sthall Christrrias gift' to eatli "'Old Boy serVing, with' nis Idajestfir Fames:</p>
        <p>'p</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>WHITEGROSS HOSPITAL,</head>
        <p>WHITEGROSS HOSPITAL,</p>
        <p>-The Mayor And Mayoress Pay Soldier-Patients A Visit.</p>
        <p>AN UNEXPECTED RE-UNION</p>
        <p>The Mayor of Warrington (Alderman Peter Peacock), accompanied by the Mayoress, paid a '•visit to the Whitecross Military Hospital on Tuesday evening. With his Worship also were the members of the Management Committee of the hospital, viz., Mr. A. J. Jolley (chairman); Mr. W. Furness (with Mrs. Furness), Mr. W. -V. Brooks (with Mrs. Brooks), and Mr. A. Bottomley (secretary), Dr. A. Hodgkinson (visiting medical officer), Dr. E. Jagger .(yesident medical officer), Miss Emerton (matron), Mr. W. Loxham (officer in -charge), and Mr. IL I. Partridge (assistant -secretary to the committee).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>SOLDIERS FROM ALL PARTS</head>
        <p>SOLDIERS FROM ALL PARTS</p>
        <p>The party were conducted through the twenty wards of the hospital by Dr. ilodgkinson. -Tlrtrse wards contain 220 beds, and on Tuesday 158 were occupied by wounded soldiers. At many of the beds the Mayor and Mayoress stayed a few minutes, chatting, with the patients. The inmates of the hospital are drawn from practically all quarters. One of -them is a French-Canadian, and previous to his arrival at Warrington he. could not speak a. word of English. But, thanks t 6 the patience of the nurses and the comradeship that exists amongst all soldiers, he is slowly mastering the difficulties of thp English language. A patient specially interesting to the inhabitants of War- rington is Lance-Corporal Knowles, a War- -rington Territorial, who was formerly a local school attendance officer.</p>
        <p>It is well-known that " Tommy " can do most things of a practical nature, such as mending his own clothes, &amp;c., but the ladies of the party were taken a little bit by surprise on Tuesday evening when they came across a sergeant of a Scotch regiment busily engaged on "fancy" work. Sergeant Cameron, for this is the ,gallant Scot's name, told our represen- tative that he started the fancy work on Sun- day, and was soon told by the nurses that he should not do such Work on such a day. "But," said Cameron, "I replied if it's right to kill Germans on a Sunday, surely it is right to do this." And he went with his work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>CIGARETTES FOR "THE BOYS."</head>
        <p>CIGARETTES FOR "THE BOYS."</p>
        <p>Going through the hospital, the Al ayoress left a couple of pacicets of cigarettes and a box of matches on each bed. The party were then conducted to the operating theatre, where the doctors' gruesome instruments were to be seen. A rather interesting story attaches to a soldier who had been under an operation on the day of the visit. 'His name is Private H. W. Smith, and he is of the 2nd East Surrey Regiment. He has been in the Warrington Hospital for six weeks. Some time ago his brother, Private R. W. Smith, of the Rifle Brigade, was reported as "killed and missing," and Private H. W. Smith had naturally reconciled himself to the loss of his brother. One can imagine his sur- prise when on Tuesday afternoon his "dead" brother walked into the hospital to pay him a visit! By. now the visiting brother will be on the ocean.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>SUPPER AND SPEECHES.</head>
        <p>SUPPER AND SPEECHES.</p>
        <p>The visit to the operating theatre at an end, the party were entertained to supper, at, the close of which several of them spoke of the favourable impression that they had formed of the hospital.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>PATIENTS' GOODWILL</head>
        <p>PATIENTS' GOODWILL</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. Jolley, in proposing the toast of the health of "The Mayor and Mayoress," said that the visit of the Mayor was a -unique, occasion in the history of the institution. That night the Mayor and his friends had 'had an opportunity of seeing an institution which, at any rate, the Guardians could say that they did their best to make a credit to the district by providing, in the best possible manner, for the comfort and well-being of all who came under their charge. During the present year the Guardians had had additional responsi- bilities placed upon them in providing accom- modation for a number of wounded soldiers. The result of their efforts the party, had. seen that evening, during their stroll through the; different wards. He could say, without boast- ing, that it would be difficult to find anywhere , -a, hospital where better provision, was made Or the wounded soldiers. On the part of ; the Guardians it was a work of love, and he ; ventured to say that to the staff who had been .engaged in the work of restoring the wounded soldiers to health and vigour, and to the many voluntary helpers who had given time and service, the Guardians were deeply indebted. From what he. had seen and heard from men I who had returned to duty, the services that had been rendered them had been very accept-' able and very welcome. The committee and staff felt that, in asmall way, they were doing something of a national duty in a time of stress and difficulty. They had had occasional bits of encouragement, and the one that he thought they appreciated most was the good- will of the patients who had passed under their care. It was a 'great ;pleasure to be able to receive the head of the municipality of War- rington, accompanied by the Mayoress. The Guardians had done their best to proviae for the soldiers who had been sent to them, but at forty-eight hours' notice they could take in as many more patients without difficulty.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>HIS WORSHIP PLEASED WITH ALL HE SAW.</head>
        <p>HIS WORSHIP PLEASED WITH ALL HE SAW.</p>
        <p>Replying to the toast, the Mayor pleaded' ignorance concerning the institution he had just inspected. He never knew before that at any time they had above 50 soldiers. It was partieularly gratifying that the committee and the staff should have spent their time in ac- companying him and to make the visit enjoy- able. His experience in visiting the institution was not very great. Two years ago, • when he made a visit, he was favourably impressed, and felt that if ever he was going to be ill he would like to be taken to the institution. That 'night the conditions ,had improved. Anything (connected. with the war must impress a public 'man, especially the Mayor of a town like 'Warrington. He considered that it must be 'very gratifying to the wounded soldiers that so many comforts were provided for them. ife -was very pleased with everything that he had seen in the hospital. The Mayor asked the company to drink the health of the Matron and -staff of the hospital, coupling with the toast 'the name of Dr. Jagger (the resident medical ,officer). Dr. Jagger expressed his delight with the visit of the Mayor and Mayoress. The Mayor referred to the excellent arrange- inents that had been made for the provision of ['comforts" for the wounded soldiers. He hoped that the people of Warrington would contribute more readily to the fund.</p>
        <p>MAYOR'S FIRST PUBLIC DUTY SINCE RE-ELECTION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Bottomley, on behalf of Miss Emerton, pointed out that this was the first official function performed by Alderman Peacock since he had been re-appointed Mayor. With regard to the "Comforts" Fund, so far, he had been able to provide tit:e patients- with a good supply of cigarette i and tobacco, and the postage of all the letters written by the soldiers had been paid for out of the-fund. They had had some good friends in Warrington, but for' some time past they had had large sums to pay in, postages, &amp;o. If contributors would send him money instead Of cigarettes he Was in a posi- tion to obtain goods at special prices. The subscriptions had come in very well indeed, but for a few weeks there had been a falling off, and this was just at the time when they had a larger number of patients than previously. Mr. Bottomley also paid a tribute to the Voluntary nurses, and to the Matron and officials of the hospital. Mr. W. Furness and Mr. W. C. Brooks also spoke a few words of welcome to the Mayor and Mayoress. Mrs. Furness expressed an appreciative thought of the selfLsacrifiee of the soldiers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>LATEST CASUALTI ES.</head>
        <p>LATEST CASUALTI ES.</p>
        <p>Expeditionary Force.—Reported from the Base 'under date November 3rd:—</p>
        <p>SOUTH LANCASHIRE REGIMENT. 2nd Battalion.</p>
        <p>Previously reported wimmded, now reported. died of wounds.—Lanee-Corportal W. Riggs, 1261. Previously missing, now reported died as prisoner.—Private H. Fielden, -6704.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 13, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 13, 1915.</p>
        <p>TERRITORIAL ROLL OF HONOUR</p>
        <p>Many Names to be Added to the List.</p>
        <p>OLD AND NEW CASUALTIES.</p>
        <p>This week a long list of Territorial casualties has been published, but the names were largely those of men who were,)reported upon by us so long ego as last June. Eleven new casualties, however, tend still further to swell the Bat- talion's roll of honour.</p>
        <p>We give the new names below as well as details in most cases. We have been unable to trace the relatiVes of Lance-Corporal J. J. Howe or Private J. Jolley. In the case of Private R. Timmins, 36, Aiken-street, we a're informed that it is not a case of wounding but sickness; and in the case of Sergeant W. Perry, lkonard-street, the casualty is so old that it has ceased to be of interest. Still, we include these names in order to complete the "roll." This week's recorded casualties, with this ex- planation, are as follows :</p>
        <p>MISSING. Private W. Coyne. Private .1. D. Renshaw.</p>
        <p>WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>Sergeant W. Perry. Lance-Corporal P. Smith. Lance-Corporal J. J. Howe, 2059. Private J. Jolley, 1969. Private S. Ratcliffe. Private, G. H. Lloyd. Private A. Sharp. Private J. Grundy. Private R. Timmins.</p>
        <p>TOTAL CASUALTIES TO DATE.</p>
        <p>Killed Wounded Missing Prisoners</p>
        <p>. 91 . 436 . 18 . 2</p>
        <p>Total .</p>
        <p>buried amongst their other comrades. You will understand that a big shell. plays havoc when it drops on a dug-out, as the explosion is terrific. One of the gunners -lound a little watch of his 'about a week later. It lay on a shelf in the billet and I have been keeping it until some one came on leave, If they don't come I will send it to you if possible. It is the only thing that I can send, as all his kit, &amp;c., Was lost. It cuts me up more than you think to have to write this. I wish to God I could tell you something different. Cheer up if you can. He was a good soldier, and I am sure he is in Heaven. I know it is hard, and it would grieve you to see all the different soldiers' graveyards out here. I sincerely hope you will bear up under so terrible a blow. We don't know whose turn it is going to be out here. I thank you very much for your kind wishes, and my greates•;; regret is that it has fallen to my lot to have to write this. I wonder how much longer it is going to last? I often get weary of it, bdt I expect we must do our best.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>MISSING.</head>
        <p>MISSING.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE WILLIAM COYNE.</p>
        <p>The Second Son Lost; Another One In Training.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. John Coyne, of 159, Longford- street, Warrington, have received official noti- fiCation -from the War Office that their son, Private William Coyne, a. bomb-thrower in the 4th -South Lancashire Regiment, is reported missing and ;his whereabouts not known. He was in the big advance on September 25th. Private Coyne was 23 years of age, and, as a youth, attended the St, Ann's School, worship- ping' at the Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church. He was employed, previous to joining N.M. Forces, at the commencement -of s hostilities, at the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron' Co.; and was well liked and respected by all who came in contact with him. The subject of our photo- graph was athletic, taking. particular interest in all branches of sport, especiallY football, having played for several local clubs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Brother-Like Chum.</head>
        <p>A Brother-Like Chum.</p>
        <p>The first notification of him being missing wa conveyed to his parents by the following letter written by a chum in the same regiment named Jack Hatton, dated September 30th : "I am very sorry to have to write this letter to you; in fact, I would ,rather be hit by a bullet than to tell you the news I now write. Your son is reported missing, and I am -hoping he is found if injured in hospital., I am quite at a loss wikat to say, as this is the worst task I have ever been put to do. I have lost .the dearest chum that walked the earth, as he was like a brother. The blow is great, but I hope you will keep ,your 'trust in the Lord and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>Territorials Inspected By The King.</head>
        <p>Territorials Inspected By The King.</p>
        <p>"I• had the pleasure of being inspected by King George yesterday, but perhaps Mrs. W. will tell you all about it. We are at present resting at a small village in France, but the weather is so wet and cold. I dread the winter coming. I think this is all this time. Please give my kind regards to your father and mother and sisters, and assure them of my deepest sympathy. Also. kind regards to your husband, and if at any time you would care to write your letter would always be appreciated. Hop- ing you are all well, I will close and remain, sincerely yours, E. J..WIDOCKS."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>C.L.B. Lad.</head>
        <p>A C.L.B. Lad.</p>
        <p>Private Renshaw was in the ,Machine Gun Section of the 4th South Lancashires, and was employed for some time by Messrs. H. Milling and Sons, but prior to enlisting was in the wheel department at the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd. He was a well-known member of the St. Paul's Church Lads' Bri- gade, Ambulance Section, and was also a mem- ber of the St. Paul's Sunday School and Church. Private Renshaw was 21 years of age and a member of the Ancient Order of Druids, "King William the Fourth" Lodge. He was educated at St. Ann's School.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>CORPORAL P. SMITIL</p>
        <p>A Rapid Recovery And Return To Duty.</p>
        <p>Lanee-Corporal Peter Smith, Of the Warring- ton Territorials and son of Mr. John Smith, 30, Naylor-street, received a slight wound to the muscle of his arm on October 18th, but has since fully recovered and has returned to the trenches.</p>
        <p>In conveying the news to Mrs. Smith, Private J. Tandy, of the Territorials, wrote "Just a few lines to let you know that Peter received a shrapnel-wound in the right arm on Monday night. I am glad to say, however, that it is not serious, and I am hoping to hear tell of him being in England- shortly; so you need not worry. He was very cheerful when I left him at the dressing station. He will be missed by all the 'boys, for he was thought a great deal of."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>White Star Footballer.</head>
        <p>A White Star Footballer.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal Smith, who is 22 years of age, was educated at St ..Maxy's School, Butter- market-street. He was formerly employed by the Whitecross Co., Ltd., and was at one time a member of the Warrington White Star Foot- ball Club. He joined the Territorials soon after the outbreak Ci f war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>PRIVATE J. GRUNDY.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE J. GRUNDY.</p>
        <p>Glad To Be In Old England Again.</p>
        <p>Private John Grundy, a married man, 72, Laira-street, is in the Edinburgh War Hospi- tal, suffering from shattered nerves. Writing to his wife from the latter hospital, he says that he is progressing favourably. "I am glad to get out of that hell," he adds, "and get to Old England again." He has been in five different hospitals. Private Grundy, who is 25 years of age, at- tended as a boy Hamilton-street School, and Silver-street Sunday School. He was formerly employed by Messrs. Joseph -Crosfield and Sons,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>PRIVATE G. 11. LLOYD.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE G. 11. LLOYD.</p>
        <p>An Old Volunteer Ilit In The Night.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Lloyd, of 16. Watkin-street, has. received an official notification from. the War Office to the effect that her husband, Private George Henry Lloyd, of the Warrington Territorials, has been wounded in action. The following letter, written by Corporal Smith, also of the. Territorials, under date October 20th, has come to hand :--</p>
        <p>"George got hit last night whilst out work- ing with me. Please do not get excited or worried, for I can safely assure you that it is not very serious, and by the time yeu receive this letter I expect he will be safely across the water. He was cheerful and comfortable when I left him, and he will be well looked after, I was very sorry that he got wounded, for he is a good soldier!' Private Lloyd has since written home from a hospital in Nottinghamshire, stating that he is wounded in the left thigh, but is progressing favourably. - Private Lloyd, who is 34 years of age, at- tended as a boy St. Benedict's B.C. School. He was formerly emnloyed by the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd:, for which team he formerly played football. He joined the Territorials in January, having previously served in the old Volunteers for eleven years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>ON HONOUR'S SCROLL.</head>
        <p>ON HONOUR'S SCROLL.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE F. DEAN.</p>
        <p>Killed.</p>
        <p>Private Cronin announced that Private Dean was buried on the evening of the day of his death, and added, "When killed, he suffered no pain whatever."</p>
        <p>His Company Officer's Tribute.</p>
        <p>Later, dated November 6th, Mrs. Dean.got the following letter from Captain R. Wolryd Gor- don, the officer commanding the Ist company of the 3rd Battalion of the Grenadier Guards, the company to which the deceased belonged: "In reply to your. inquiry . . I regret to have to inform you that your husband was killed by a shell on October 17th: It will be gollle con- solation to you to know that he suffered no pain, as he was killed instantly. He was buried by his comrades and a cross was erected on his grave. I can assure you that he wilt be very much missed, as he was always cheerful and willing, and very popular among his comrades, who all tender you their deepest sympathy in your loss."</p>
        <p>Church P.S.A., where a memorial service is being held to-morrow. Private H. Kent was widely known and re- spected by all, for his kindly demeanour and disposition earned him many friends. Two of the deceased's brothers have already been wounded in the present war. They are Private John Kent, of the Gordon Highlanders, and Private Ernest Kent, of the Warrington. Territorials. Botl are making satisfactory pro- gress. Another brother, Corporal J. H. Kent, of the "Terriers," is at present "somewhere in France."</p>
        <p>to Mr. and Mrs. Patrick Walder, of 18, Bank- street, Warrington, the news that their son, Private William Walder, died of wounds three weeks ago to-day. The dead soldier was only 19 years of age, an‘l he joined the Ist Lancashire Fusiliers but seven months ago. He was edu- cated at St. Alban's Roman Catholic School, being a member of the Romish community, and he worked as .a moulder at Messrs. Richmond's Works at Grappenhall. He had been in France a bare fortnight when he met his fate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>Orphaned as a Baby.</head>
        <p>Orphaned as a Baby.</p>
        <p>Private Dean was 33 years of age, and he re- ceived his education at Orford School. When two years old he was orphaned, and he was brought up by older brothers and sisters. For a time he served In the Liverpool Police Force, but subsequently came to work at the Orford Tannery. Leaving there he worked at the Clock Face Colliery, and was there employed when he was recalled to the tolours, for before he was a policeman he was a soldier, joining the Grena- dier Guards some eight years Ago. . He leaves, in addition to a widow, two Young children to mourn him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <p>PRIVATE WILLIAM COYNE.</p>
        <p>Missing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <p>bring news that will be of the best; but sur- prises are things that break the heart more than anything. I have sat alone often and thought of what has become of him, for if I knew it would ease h 4 parents' hearts. This war is a curse and one that will always be cursed by those who took part in it. I cannot write more, as I am on .the point of breaking down. He was loved by everyone, and all send their deepest sympathk." Further news is anxiously awaited by his parents, who have already lost one son, a sig- naller in the same regiment, in the big battle of June 16th last.</p>
        <p>Another son, Private Walter Coyne, is in training with the Territorials (the 2/ 4th) in the South of England,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>PRIVATE J. D. RENSHAW.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE J. D. RENSHAW.</p>
        <p>Buried By Shell Explosion In A Dull-Out.</p>
        <p>2</p>
        <p>Missing.</p>
