        <p>PRIVATE J. D. RENSHAW.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <p>PRIVATE G.. 11. LLOYD Wounded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>IN MEMORIAM.</head>
        <p>IN MEMORIAM.</p>
        <p>Working Men's Mission Mourn</p>
        <p>Three Members.</p>
        <p>"Responding to the call of duty—the sacred importance of which they had always been taught to recognise—they, went forth volun- tarily to do their utmost in the noble cause of thdir King and country." In 'these words did Councillor G. Dale pay a tribute to the memory of three fallen heroes—Privates John Brimelow, Elias Thomas and Norman Wilson—at a special memorial service, held at the Working Men's Mission Church, on 'Sunday evening. While all the three soldiers were members of the 1/4th South Lancashire (Territorial) Regiment, they did not fall in the same battle. Privates J. Brimelow and N. Wilson were reported wounded and missing., after the battle of Hooge, on June 16th, and, although not officially reported dead, their relatives have received from time to time information which seems to prove beyond doubt that the gallant lads are no more. It was in the great advance on.. September 30th that Private E. Thomas was fatally wounded by a piece bf shell whilst in the execution of his duty as a stretcher bearer.</p>
        <p>There was a crowded congregation, among</p>
        <p>those present being the members of the Men's Bible Class, to which the soldiers were formerly attached. Mr. Richard Lee, of Wigan, was the special preacher, and, taking a text from the Book of the Revelation, spoke of the bright side of death.</p>
        <p>The panegyric was delivered by Councillor George Dale, in the cows() of which he touched upon the high qualities of each soldier, and extended a note of sympathy to all those who were left to mourn their loss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>Have Done Their Duty.</head>
        <p>Have Done Their Duty.</p>
        <p>. In concluding, the speaker referred to the fact that the lads had joined the great cloud of witnesses, and said that . seeing we were en- compassed about with so great a cloud of wit- nesses, it waiS up to us to lay aside every weight, and in the present crisis ask ourselves, "What can Ido to help my country?" The young men who were especially in their thoughts at that time had done. their duty. It wasjor us to do ours. The man who was of military age and physically fit could have no excuse unless on munitions work, 'at a time of such gravity as i the present. He ,(the speaker) ventured to be still more emphatic and to say that the man who in these days of national and interna- tional strife refused to do whatever might be possible, was a Coward and a traitor to his race. He knew that the religious argument was some- times used against war, but while they were bound to respect a man when he assured them that he had a conscientious objection, yet at the.same time such ,an objection was difficult to understand when applied to the present war. Forced Into War By Highest Considerations. As his hearers knew, they as a country had beexi forced into the war by moral pressure, by the highest and holiest considerations, affecting the rights of the weak, and touching the very basis and core of Divine law. In fat, in 'a war of this kind, he contended that the religious argument, as applied by some people, appeared to have no justification at all. He hoped that the service would be something more than a memorial Service in the fact that it would stir them all. in resolution and determination not only to fight the good fight of faith, but also to' do whatever might be possible for England and 'humanity in the present terrible crisis. During the evening Mr. Pierce sang very effectively "Thou art passing hence," while Mr.. F. Atkinson rendered in a sympathetic manner the voluntary "0 rest in the Lord."</p>
        <p>• Esperantists and Interned Sailors. A number of the British sailors who have been interned in Holland for several months past, have occupied their leisure time in learn- ing Esperanto, ..and they have reached such a degree of proficiency that they are now able to correspond in the universal language. Several local Esperantists, write letters to them regu- larly and receive replies, the correspondence being conducted in the new language.</p>
        <p>" PLAYING THE GAME" is the title of the lovely coloured plate centrepiece in the "Liver- pool Weekly Post" Sheet Almanack for 1916-- the best issued, and given free on Saturday, November 20th. Also two fine stories begin— " The Dancing Girl" and "This Woman to this Man." Big demand certain. Ask your newsagent to-day to keep your copy for you.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
