        <p>PRIVATE HARRY BENT. Died of Wounds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>"The Pet of the Regiment."</head>
        <p>"The Pet of the Regiment."</p>
        <p>The letter from Sister E. D. Smaile, convey- ing the news of his death, was dated October 23rd, and ran as follows: "I am writing to tell you that your dear lad . . . was brought into our station yesterday, severely wounded in the abdomen, and though the doctors did every- thing for him that could be done, he went quietly home at 12 o'clock midnight. Rest assured that everything that human skill could do was done, but the Heavenly Father decreed that his life should not be spared, and took him to Himself. As your boy was so ill when he came to us, the doctor got the priest to come and see him. One of his chums—who came down with him, also wounded—said that your boy was the pet of the regiment, and kept them all so cheery and merry. He was buried to-day with full military honours, and lies in a quiet resting- place in this sad land of France. Please accept my heartfelt sympathy for you in your sad</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>Two Brothers Wounded.</head>
        <p>Two Brothers Wounded.</p>
        <p>Two of the dead Private's brothers are serv- ing in the Army, and both have been wounded. James. who is also in the Lancashire Fusiliers, was wounded in the head in the Dardanelles fighting on August 6th. That fact was officially conveyed by the War Office to the parents, but though they have anxiously waited for further tidings, not a line has come during the twelve weeks that have elapsed. The other soldier brother, Thomas, is with the Army Service Corps in France. and he has been in hospital about nine weeks, recovering from a bullet wound in the head. Formerly Thomas worked at Messrs. Richmond's as a moulder, and he at one time played for the Works Rugby thirteen in the Works Competition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>MISSING.</head>
        <p>MISSING.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE A. H. HITCHINSON BELIEVED TO BE WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>A Three Months' Silence.</p>
        <p>The mother of Private Arthur Hollins Hitchinson, 67, Priestley-street, Warrington, who belongs to the 6th South Lancashires, and was reported missing in Gallipoli three months ago, has received several letters which show that her son- was wounded and may be a prisoner in the hands of the Turks. She is in communi- cation with the authorities on the subject, but the methods of the Turks do not allow her to hope that she will get a very early reply. Private Hitchinson, who was a galvaniser at Messrs. Rylands Bros., was, says his Quarter,</p>
        <p>TERRITORIAL EQUIPMENT</p>
        <p>FUND.</p>
        <p>Winter Comforts Are Needed For Three Battaiions.</p>
        <p>TO the Editor of "The Warrington Examiner." The committee are anxious to provide an adequate supply of winter comforts for the three battalions of the 4th South Lancashire Regiment—our own Territorials. During the past fifteen months the following articles have been despatched, viz.:- 7,116 Pairs of Socks. 1,560 Shirts. 2,069 Glove Mits. 720 Helmets. 2,217 Body Belts. 2= other things.; also 2,000 boxes of cigarettes and a large quantity of writing paper. The ladies of the battalion—mothers, wires and sisters of the officers and N.C. officers—have been working continuously sincv the outbreak of the war in obtaining, making and delivering the goods before-mentioned, and it is their intention to carry on their important work until the termination of hostilities.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <p>Master-Sergeant, a splendid soldier and the life and soul of the whole regiment when on the march. This was said in a letter dated Sept. 25th, in which it was stated that a letter sent to Private Hitchinson was returned "as we have no idea where he is, and he might probably be in England." A letter from Private A. E. Davenport, R.A.M.C., acknowledging inquiries made by Mrs. Hitchinson, says—" So far he has not passed through the Field Ambulances or clear- ing hospitals. I am inclined to think he is out of touch with his regiment. As you will under- stand, when an advance is made and the men extend it is very easy to lose each other. . . . You must look on the bright side of the matter. We are making all inquiries, and hope soon to be able to let you know something definite."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
