      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>DRIVER ALBERT COX.</p>
        <p>" Fell A Stinging Pain In My Left - Leg." Mr. and Mrs. Benjamin Cox, 107, Oldham- street, Latchford, on Tuesday morning received a field card from their youngest son, Driver Albert Cox, of the R.F.A., intimating that he has. been wounded. On Thursday afternoon arrived the following letter from the Third , Scottish Hospitall--" There is no cause to worry about my wound, for lam getting on fine. I was going up to the guns with ammunition. We had just got off the horses while they were un- loaded, and immediately I felt a stinging pain in my left leg. I told my pal that I had been hit, so he helped me into a gun-pit, and there my wound was dressed. Operated On Board Ship. The wound caused me pain for ,the first two nights, because the bullet was still in my leg. They took the bullet out on the boat just as we reached Southampton. I have kept it as a souvenir. It is a shrapnel bullet. I expect you received my field card and a letter from my pal. I am in the pink, save for my wound." - "Joined Up" On Outbreak. . Driver Cox, who is 19 years,of age, attended, as a boy, the St. James' School. He was for- merly employed by Messrs. Richmond and Co. He enlisted in August of last year. A brother is serving with the same regiment in France.</p>
        <p>MENTIONED IN DISPATCHES.</p>
        <p>Honour For The Architect Of New St. Benedict's Church.</p>
        <p>Captain Mathew Ronan, of the 10th South Lancashire Regiment, a well-known Liverpool architect, and designer of the new St. Bene- dict's Roman Catholic Church, Orford-lane, has been mentioned in General Sir lan Hamilton's latest dispatch. for valuable services rendered on the Gallipoli Peninsula. Captain Honan, who was granted a commis- sion in December, was drafted out to the Dar- danelles in May, being attached to the Ist Battalion of the Lancashire Fusiliers. He is at present recovering from a shrapnel wound in the head, received during fierce fighting in July.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>"Something For Christmas."</head>
        <p>"Something For Christmas."</p>
        <p>The funds generously provided by the friends of the regiment are now nearly exhausted, and. es the battalions—especially the first battalion at the Front—will all require further supplies, and, as it is thought desirable to mark the advent of Christmas by a small and seasonable gift, at least to each of our men in the fighting- line, it is necessary to make a further public appeal for subscriptions. It is very desirable that the interest in the present effort- should be as widespread as pos- sible, and the committee will be grateful for all sums contributed, however small or large. Hardships of Winter.</p>
        <p>We know of the hardships so uncomplain- ingly endured by our heroes in the trenches during the frost and rains of last winter and spring; therefore let us see to it that every- thing humanly possible shall be done to lessen the trials and difficulties which the winter, now so Tepidly approaching, will bring in its train. Sums as small as a shilling will be gratefully received and big cheques will be equally wel- come.</p>
        <p>Subscriptions may be paid to the account of the Territorial Equipment Fund at the District Bank, Sankey-street; to Mr. Jas. H. Smethurst, Briery Croft, Warrington; or to any of the undersigned : KATE REID WALKER, President. ETHEL C. FAIRCLOUGFI, Vice-President. ADELA FAIRCLOUGH, Vice-President. HELEN FAIRCLOUGH, Hon. Secretary.</p>
        <p>Drill Hall, Warrington, November 11th, 1915.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>"A FRIEND IN NEED ! ,' Concert To Aid Police Court Missioner's Efforts.</head>
        <p>"A FRIEND IN NEED ! ,' Concert To Aid Police Court Missioner's Efforts.</p>
        <p>The wounded soldiers at the Raddon Court Hospital gave an entertainment at the Parish Room, Latchford', on Thursday afternoon. The programme was similar to that presented a fortnight ago by the same performers. The various items were given in the same swinging style as marked the previous performance. The artistes were: Sergeant-Major Northcote, Cor- poral Molyneux, Trooper Whorlow,4 Bugler Monrad, Private Sheehan, Private Johnson, Private King, Private Wild and Private Bram- well. The second half of the programme con- sisted of the military sketch "A Spy Some- where," which has been written by the wounded soldiers. Several new features had been intro- duced, which had the effect of making it more entertaining than ever. During the interval a short address on the work of the Police Court Missions was given by the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock). Mayoral Tribute To A Fine Cause.</p>
        <p>His Worship said that it gave him great plea- sure to help, by every means in his power, the good work done by Mr. William R. Laws, the local Police Court Missionary and Probation Officer. He (the speaker) had been to a great deal of trouble to ascertain the nature of that work. Recently he saw a number of male prisoners coming out of a gaol. They were taken in hand by Police Court Missioners and given a cup of coffee and something to eat. Shortly afterwards the women came out of the gaol and they were also taken in hand by the Missioners. Besides being given something to eat and drink, the prisoners were given a few words of encouragement. The Mayor went on to refer to the wok amongst girls. He said that Mr. Laws had set many a girl on a good career. A lot of the Warrington people did not know what good work the Police Court Missioner performed, and therefore Mr. Laws did not get the public support to which he was entitled. He was glad, however, that Miss Constance Broadbent had made the suggestion of holding that entertainment, with a view to swelling the funds of the Police Court Mission. Personally, he thought it was a splendid idea. It was pleasing to see the wounded soldiers helping others, who, in another sense, were also "down." (Applause.) He thanked them for the entertainment and he thanked Miss Constance Broadbent for making the arrange- ments. On behalf of Mr. Laws, he also thanked the Chief Constable and the police officials for their assistance. If anyone desired to take more interest in Police Court Missions he re- ferred them to Mr. Laws. He ,the speaker) had put two separation cases in the hands of Mr. Laws this very week. Mr. Laws had saved lots of homes. from being broken up, and the Police Court Missions were objects worthy .of all possible support. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>Proceeds.</head>
        <p>The Proceeds.</p>
        <p>The men who want to wind all the world's clocks seldom want to go to work by any of them.</p>
        <p>The Parish Room was "full up," and as a result of the concert over £2O vas realised.</p>
      </div>
