      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>z IcTRICT r.- tzt._Y • •• – : s' :</head>
        <p>z IcTRICT r.- tzt._Y • •• - : s' :</p>
        <p>I4YMM</p>
        <p>Lads' Club Temporarily Closed.—At a com- mittee meeting of the Lymm Lads' Club, held last week, it was decided, owing to the out- break of diphtheria in the village, to close the premises until November 22nd.</p>
        <p>Local Recruiting Committee.—As a result of the recent canvassing in connection with Lord Derby's scheme the local committee have been inundated by a large number of inquiries respecting the scheduling of married men who have been informed that they will be tabulated strictly in conformity with the wishes of the Government, and in accordance with the pub- lished statements.</p>
        <p>Foreign M issions.—A public meeting was held in the Wesleyan Church on Wednesday evening, when an address was delivered by the Rev. S. C. Hall, of Manchester, formerly.missionary on the West Coast of Africa. The Chairman was Mr. Wheatley, of Grappenhall. There was a large audience, who thoroughly appreciated the rever- end gentleman's lecture on life and conditions on the West Coast of Africa. The collections so far have been very satisfactory.</p>
        <p>Private H. Cadman.—Private IL Cadman, Lymm Territorial, belonging to the Mining Section of the Royal Engineers, has this week been over on a short furlough. He is looking in the best of health after his recent experiences in the • firing line. The popular Booth's Hill soldier is returning to France at the beginning of next Week, and states that he has quite re- covered from his severe illness of a short time ago. PreTious to joining the Colours, Private H. Cadman was a bricksetter in the employ of Mr. J. Holt, who lives on Booth's Hill, and at- tended the Congregational/Church and Sunday School. This week he has been the recipient of many messages of good fortune for his return to the firing line by a host of friends.</p>
        <p>Sapper Joseph Clare Arrives in England.— Sapper Joseph Clare, of Booth's Hill, Lymm, whose photograph we published last week, has written home to his mother in a postcard dated 11th inst.:—" Just arrived in England, and ex- pect eventually to be in hospital somewhere around Manchester. At present lam a patient at the Clearing Hospital, Eastleigh, Hants. I am feeling much better." Sapper Clare, it will be remembered, was hit by a piece of trench mortar, which went through his mouth and lodged in his cheek, and is attached to the 3rd Section Royal Engineers. He is 23 years of age, and prior to the outbreak of hostilities was four years with the "Terriers." He is a noted athlete, and was employed by the L. and N.-W. Railway Co.</p>
        <p>stocks at Lymm, that is if his carcass hadn't been too big. We have been on more than one occasion against the Prussian Guards, They are fine, big fellows, but are terribly afraid of the cold steel. They will face bullets all righ• but the bayonet is too much for them." Sergeant Moston joined his Majesty's For;:os as a Private, and was promoted to Corporal whilst in training, subsequently rea2bing his present rank in France. He is a irothcr cl Sergt. S. D. Moston, of the Lymin Platoon, , Cheshire Volunteers.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE ARTHUR THORNICROVI.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <p>Funeral of the Late Mr. F. Warburton.—The remains of the/late Mr. Frank Warburton, The Cross, were on Monday afternoon interred in the Parish Churchyard, in the presence of many sorrowing relations and friends. Practi- cally the whole of the inhabitants turned out to witness the last rites. The late Mr. War- burton had only been ill about a week, the news of his death creating considerable sur- prise and regret in the village. The chief mourners were Mr. Hugh Warburton, (uncle), Mr. Arthur Forrest (cousin), Miss A. Forrest (aunt), the Misses Elsie and Etta Hesketh (cousins), and Mr. A. J. Willett. The Trades. men's Association was represented by Messrs. H. S. Saville, F. E. Evans, E. B. Richardson, C. E. Ardern, H. E. Ogber, E. Brown- and F. W. Druce. Lieutenant Wilcox, Mr. T. Heaven ,and Mr. Butterfield were among others present. Mr. R. H. Gotobed had charge of the undertak- ing arrangements, and the Rector (the Rev. Granville B. Thurston) conducted the burial service. Many handsome floral tributes were received, including those from the following "Sister Annie," "Ada and family," Mr. Hugh Warburton (uncle), Miss A. Forrest (aunt), the Misses Elsie and Etta Hesketh (cousins), Mr. and Mrs. A. B. Richardson, Mr. and Mrs. Tomlinson, Mr. and Mrs. Thorley, Mr. and Mrs. Gotobed, Mr. and Mrs. Morris. the Tradesmen's Association, and Mr. A. J. Willett.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>CANVASSING FOR RECRUITS.</head>
        <p>CANVASSING FOR RECRUITS.</p>
        <p>Volunteers Wanted To Carry Out Lord . Derby's Scheme.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the local branch of the Altrinc- ham Division Parliamentary Recruiting Com- mittee was held in the Grammar School on Monday night, when a goodly number of help- ers responded to the patriotic call for volun- teers for the purpose of canvassing the district. The scheme was briefly outlined by the Chair- man (Mr. Alfred Watkin), who emphasised the need and urgency for everyone to put their heart and soul into the work, with a view to carrying out Lord Derby's scheme satisfac- torily. Vice-Chancellor Stewart Smith, K.C., and Mrs. Ridgway made suitable and appropriate speeches, which were the cause of a consider- able npmber being enrolled. The• committee desire to state that any further ladies and gentlemen willing to assist with the work can obtain particulars from the joint honorary secretaries, Mr. A. W. Callender and Mr. R. Barker.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>SERGEANT J. H. MOSTON.</head>
        <p>SERGEANT J. H. MOSTON.</p>
        <p>Says Prussian Guards Won't Face Steel.</p>
        <p>Sergeant .IL Moston, Newfields, who is with the Lymm Territorials, has this week been home on a short furlough from France. He returned yesterday to rejoin his battalion after visiting many friends and admirers, m-,ho wgsh him " God-speed "on his journey. Whilst in the village, it is interesting to record, another Lymm soldier (Private J. Booth),- who met Sergeant Moston in the firing line, has also this week been enjoying a well-earned leave. To our representatives the popular Sergeant imparted the following account of his experiences in the firing line "Whilst out at the Front I met an old Lymm comrade in the person of Private J. Booth, and it is a coincidence that the first to greet me at Lymm Station was my old friend. itgis good to see the old faces, especially those who have fought for their King and country. My last spell in the trenches was for a period of 34 days. Sometimes we were up to the ankles in mud, which makes you long for a good fire and a book out here in England. Ofttimes we have been billeted in churches partly 'destroyed by the German shells, but more often in the open, which tries one's stamina and patriotism. 1 must tell you of an amusing incident of a little while age," continued Sergeant Moston, with smile.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>German At The Listening Post.</head>
        <p>The German At The Listening Post.</p>
        <p>"I was in charge of a listening post, when all at once I espied a big burly -German of the type drawn by our caricaturists. I am sure he must have been fourteen stone in weight. He was in the act of flinging a, hand grenade at us, but before he.threw it one of the men in my section got there, first and over 'he toppled. We hauled him in front .of ad 'found -he had broken his leg azd arm. -Some of the boys wanted to tickle him up with the bayonet, hut they were stopped. We searched him with a view to obtaining information as to what regi- ment we were fighting against, but in vain. }le kept repeating 'Mercy! kind Englishmen, mercy.' lie would have looked well in the</p>
        <p>SEND nine penny stamps to Newball &amp; Mason, Nottingham, and they will send you a bottle of</p>
        <p>Mason's Ginger Wine Essence</p>
        <p>One Gallon Ginger Wine with the addition of lump sugar</p>
        <p>All who apply before December 31st will receive a Neat Money Box, which makes a Useful Gift for the</p>
        <p>, Children. A bottle of Ginper Wine Emmee loonht make an excellent gift for the men at the front.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>"An Efficient Soldier" Hit In Titv</head>
        <p>"An Efficient Soldier" Hit In Titv</p>
        <p>Forearm.</p>
        <p>News has been received by Mr. and Mrs. Edwin Thornicroft, of Clay-terrace, that their son, Private Arthur Thornicroft, 10th Battalion Cheshire Regiment, has been wounded in action. The first notification was received from Captain A. Howard Gamble, commanding the above regiment, wherein he stated in a letter dafed November 4th :</p>
        <p>"I very, much regret to report that your son, Private A. Thornicroft, of my company.. Was slightly wounded in the forearm this morning. He is now in the R.A.M.C. hands and will be well looked after. Should any serious com- plications arise, I will immediately advise you. I may say that I am personally very sorry to lose temporarily such an efficient soldier as your son has proved to be.'.!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>Said It "Was Nothing Much."</head>
        <p>Said It "Was Nothing Much."</p>
        <p>Further news has since been received from Corporal Moss, who was, prior to being trans- ferred to the Salvage Company, attached to the 25th Divisional Train, and was a comrade in the same regiment. In his letter dated the 6th inst. ho states:—</p>
        <p>I saw Arthur to-day about 2.30 and I am sorry to tell you he had got a slight wound. I was just coming out of an old house when I heard my name called, and, turning, there I saw him along with two Bed Cross men, with his arm in a sling. I saw in a second be had been hit, and I ran across to him and he assured me it was nothing much and .tliat be did not- think he would get to England with it. He asked me to write to you for him, as it is his right arm, so that means lie cannot write himself. It has got him a bit below the elbow, in the fleshy part, but I don't think it has caught the bone at all. I hope it has not, so don't, in any way worry over it. He told</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <p>PRIVATE A. THORNICROFT. Wounded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <p>me to be sure and tell you it was nothing much, and I said I would. He was looking very well indeed, and was smiling, as he always is, for he is one of the best lads we have. As you know, he was in my section for a long time, up to my leaving them a month ago, and I always found him a good chum.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>First of Lymm 10th Cheshires To Get Hit.</head>
        <p>First of Lymm 10th Cheshires To Get Hit.</p>
        <p>"I think he is the first of the Lymni 'boys' out of the 10th Cheshires to get hit. I hope and trust he will bp the last, for I can tell .you it hurts us to see our chums hit, for we have all got so fond of each other after being together so long All the boys' seethed quite happy when I last saw them, but they are in the trenches now. I don't know how long for. I myself never go into the trenches at all, so you see I am one of the lucky .beggars. I am in charge of what is called the Salvage Section. Our work is to go over the billets and headquarters of each bat- talion to pick up anything we can find that is useful, so that nothing is wasted, and it is surprising what we do get together.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>Comrade Writes " I Am Quite Safe."</head>
        <p>Comrade Writes " I Am Quite Safe."</p>
        <p>"So you see I have nothing to fear, only shell-fire—l am always under that. .or stray bullets—so I am what one may say 'quite safe' 1 only wish the others were with me. I was sorry in a way to leave the 'boys,' for I should have liked to have seen it through with them. . . . I have missed them all very much, but now I have got used to the chaps with me, so I am quite happy at the work Shall not expect an answer, but if I can do anything I shall be only too pleased. I don't think you need to worry in any .way.. They may send him down to the base, but he no doubt will get someone to drop you a line. If I hear anything more I will let you have it." Private A. Thornicroft is 23 years of age, and as a boy was educated at Lymm School. He worshipped at the Primitive Methodist Church and was eMployed prior to enlisting in Sep- tember, 1914, as gaidener to Mr. T. Dixon, " Clifflands," Lymni. He was drafted to Prawe in September last</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>PRIVATE JAMES BO( )fll.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE JAMES BO( )fll.</p>
        <p>Home On Furlough After Being Wounded.</p>
        <p>Private James Booth, who lives at Cross Brow, Statham, returned on Tuesday last on a ten days' furlough, after having been for some time a patient of .the Winsford _Red Cross Hospital. Private Booth, it will be remem- bered, was wounded in both legs in the great British advance of the 25th of September, and after being in several hospitals was eventually transferred to the •institution named above. Being an old soldier, he answered his country's call immediately on the outbreak of hostilities, rejoining his old regiment, the Ist Welsh, in which he had previously seen seventeen years' service. Private Booth went through the South African campaign and possesses the South African medal and eight bars (six Queen's and two King's). "Something I Shall Never Forget !"</p>
        <p>Interviewed by our representative, he stated that the treatment meted out to him had been of the best whilst in hospital, especially at Winsford, a testimonial which speaks volumes for that institution. Relating his experiences of the big charge, he said : "The fighting around strenuous and our regiment got mixed Up to- gether with other Infantry; in fact, we fought side by side with nearly all Infantry branches of the British .Army. It was a sight when we were told to advance. It is something I shall never forget." Previous to being wounded, whilst resting one day, he chanced to see a Cheshire soldier and thinking he-might gain some information as to the whereabouts of the Lymm "Terriers," he asked him where the Cheshires were stationed.</p>
        <p>Jolly Meeting With the Lymm Lads.</p>
        <p>- On vu the news that they were</p>
        <p>"I am writing this letter on a bully-beet tin in the firing line. In some places we are only forty or fifty Yards from the Turks' trenches. Shells and bullets are flying over our heads, but, thank God, I have escaped being hit sO far. A shell fell at my feet yesterday, but fortunately it did not catch me. "If you could only see this place you would wonder however the Allies over got a landing at all. What with the high cliffs and hills, it looks impossible. But they did get here, and now here they intend to stay. I am glad to say I am in the best of health. Good Food and Good Officers.</p>
        <p>"We are supplied with good food, and the officers are doing their best to make it as com- fortable as they possibly can for us. Of course we are kept very busy, but we don't mind that as long as we know that victory is ours in the end.</p>
        <p>"I got a letter some time ago from a scholar at Stockton Heath School. Mr. Rose gave her my address. ..I am sorry to say I have lost the letter and have forgotten the address. I in- tended to rep.ty to it, but my jacket, with the letter, was lost in a dug-out. I am the more sorry the lettbr got lost, as I very much appreciate the kindness of Mr. • Rose and of the writer in thinking of the old scholars who are now fighting for England, both in France and in. Turkey." Private Whitley goes on to ask for news from home. He writes: "I hear they are making great changes in Efigland." He concludes with the request "to save me a stick of celery please, as we might be home sooner than wc expect."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY NOVEMBER 13, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY NOVEMBER 13, 1915.</p>
        <p>about five miles off, he at once set out and found the Lymm "boys" peacefully reclining in an old shed. On opening the door he was at once recognised and the shout went up "Why, it's • Smack' ! How are you, old sport?" The following hour, he averred, was the happiest he had spent since he crossed the Channel. , The gallant soldier expects to be soon in the thing-line again. In civil life he was employed by the 'Manchester Ship Canal Company, and worshipped - at the Ly-mm Parish Church, amongst the congregation of which he is widely known and respected.</p>
        <p>through another gate. The defendant's brother went through the gate, but the defendant wrenched the gate off and banged it against a wall, doing damage to the extent of 13s. Mrs. Warburton asked, "What do you mean; is this one of your tricks?" and the defendant began to use fearful language. Piece of Wood as Weapon.</p>
        <p>When spoken to by Mr. Warburton, he took off his coat, turned up his sleeves, and took hold of a piece of wood about 4ft. Gin. long 'and struck Mr. Warburton over the eye. The de- fendant got Mr. Warburton on to the ground and kicked him several times. The defendant also struck Mrs. Warburton, who is 64 years of age. There was no doubt that this was done wilfully.</p>
        <p>Evidence bearing out this statement was given by Mr. Warburton.</p>
        <p>-Cross-exainined by Mr. Steel, the witness denied that he had any " feeling " against the defendant.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Warburton and John Herbert Clare, a teamsman in he employ of Mr. Warburton, also gave evidence for the prosecution.</p>
        <p>The Defendant's Version.</p>
        <p>For the defence Mr. Steel said that for some time there had been a certain amount of "feel- ing" between Mr. Warburton and Mr. Bradford, and because Mr. Warburton could not take it out of Mr. Bradford, he took it out of the de- fendant. On that Sunday afternoon Mr. War- burton was looking for trouble.</p>
        <p>In the witness-box the defendant denied doing any damage to the gate or striking either of the complainants. Evidence was given on his behalf by Frederick Shuffiebotham (his brother), Mrs. Shuffiebotham (his wife), Mr. John Bradford and P.C. Kelford. The latter said that he examined the gateway on October 25th, and did not notice any damage. The charge of improper conduct was dismissed On the assaUlt charges defendant was fined 10s., and he was also ordered to pay ss. towards the cost of repairs to the gate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>• Clerical Error on a Summons.—At the Dares- bury Petty Sessions at Stockton Heath on Tues- day, T,homas Mounfield, of Green Hill Common, was summoned for removing four pigs without a licence.—Mr. Mounfield pleaded that a licence had been made out, but the wrong address was inserted.—The summons was dismissed.</p>
        <p>- Sunday School Teachers' Half-yearly Meeting. ---,The half-yearly meeting of the Sunday school teachers in the Frodsham Rural Deanery was held in the Church Room, Stockton Heath, last Saturday afternoon. A good number of clergy and .over 200 teachers were present. The meet- ing was presided over by the Rev. and Hon. C. F. Cross (Vicar of Stretton and Rural Dean). The Rev. IT. A. Lester, formerly of St. Thomas' Church, Stockton Heath, addressed the teachers on the preparation of lessons and the conduct- ing of classes. The officers for the ensuing year were elected, after which tea was served in. St. Thomas' Schools by the local teachers. Social at Hill Cliffe.—The congregation of the Hill Cliffe Baptist Church held a social on Thursday evening. The schoolroom was. well filled,. and a most enjoyable evening was spent. The Rev. G. Curtis presided over a short enter- tainment, the greater part of which was sup- plied by a Glee Party from the Little Leigh Baptist Church. Other items of the programme consisted of two cornet solos by Mr. Smith, Miss Byfield being the accompanist; a recitation by Miss Lily Brocklehurst; solos by Miss Within- shaw and Mr. Pemberton, and the glee "Sweet and Low" by the Hill Cliffe Church choir. At a .suitable interval -refreshments were served. The social terminated with the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>TO GAS CONSUMERS.</head>
        <p>TO GAS CONSUMERS.</p>
        <p>\I) Lowering Of The Standard Of Light.</p>
        <p>Reference was made—at a Local Government Board Inquiry, held at Warrington on Wed- nesday, to the lighting question at Stockton Heacjk by Mr. A. T. Hallaway the deputy town clerk): Mr. Hallaway was making an application on behalf of the Warrington Cor- poration for power to alter the standard of the gas. Mr. Mclntyre was present on behalf of the Parish Council and the Runcorn Rural District Council.</p>
        <p>Referring to Mr. Mclntyre's presence, Mr. Holloway said that he did not expeq any opposition to the proposals. With regard to the street lighting of Stockton Heath, which was always a contentious matter in the dis- trict represented by Mr. Mclntyre, there would be no change whatever. The standard of lights in Stockton Heath would not be decreased. The change would make no difference in the stand- ard of hilts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>" BASTING " TURIZEY.</head>
        <p>" BASTING " TURIZEY.</p>
        <p>NN, arrior Who Hopes To Be Home In Celery Season !</p>
        <p>Private P. Whitley, of the sth Manchester Regiment, has written a letter to his parents and brothers at Stockton Heath cies.lribiztg his experiences on the Gallipoli Peninsula:, Private Whitley is the son of Mr. J. Whitley, of the Walton-roae Post Office He wntcs as follows:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>CIIE,SIIIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</head>
        <p>CIIE,SIIIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Detachment.</p>
        <p>orders for week commencing 15th November: Monday.—Drill, Headquarters, 7.45.</p>
        <p>Thursday.—Lecture, Ilfarketry, Church Room. 7.45. Saturday.—Trench-digging. Fall in 2.30, Headquarters. Extract From 'Battalion Orders. -1. The O.C. 22nd Regt. District, grants. per- mission to the Cheshire Volunteer Regt. to practise signalling, such signalling only to be, practised by men 'acting under proper control, and when engaged " on military duty, such military duti being indicated by the men wearing their War Office Brassard. 2. The efficiency badge: when worn on uni- form, will be worn on the right breast oit the same level as medals. By Order, F. WARBURTON, • .Officer Commanding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>RECRUITING CANVASS.</head>
        <p>RECRUITING CANVASS.</p>
        <p>Pro!gess Being Made AVith Lord</p>
        <p>Derby's Scheme.</p>
        <p>A meeting was held on Thursday evening in the Church Room to make the necessary arrange- ments for the canvass of the Stockton Heath and Walton Districts under Lord Derby's Re- cruiting Scheme. The meeting had been called by the local Conservative and Liberal secre- taries, under the direction of the Knutsford Division Joint Parliamentary Recruiting Com- mittee,</p>
        <p>There was a fair attendance •at the meeting, and Mr. J. 11. Clarke was voted to the chair. He explained the reason for the formation of the Joint Parliamentary Committee, and also how the question of holding a local meeting had arisen. He called on Mr. H. J. Westbrook to read a circular that had been received from headquarters requesting the holding of the meet- ing._</p>
        <p>After Mr. Westbrook had read the circular, every gentleman present—with the exception of one or two, and these.gave satisfactory reasons— offered their services. There are 726 " blue " cards for the parishes of Stockton Heath and Higher and Lower Walton. More Canvassers Wanted.</p>
        <p>It was felt that there was not a sufficient number of canvassers present,, and it was de- cided to ask that the names of other gentlemen who would be willing to canvass, should be 'sent in before Monday next to the _local secretaries, Messrs. It J. Westbrook and S. Albinson. A further.mecting of the canvassers will be held on Monday to distribute the remainder of the " blue " cards, but the cards of the single men are already in the hands of the canvassers, The Church Room, or one of its committee rooms, will be placed at the disposal of the honorary secretaries every evening for the next two or three weeks. The secretaries will be in attend- ance each evening to 'receive reports from can- vassers, and a 'daily, report will be sent- to the head dace.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>PAD GATE.</head>
        <p>PAD GATE.</p>
        <p>Wesley Guild's Roll-Call Secretary "Joins Up."—The monthly devotional meeting of the Wesley Guild was held on Monday last, when a paper on Bunyan's "Pilgrim's Progress" was given by Mr. 11. Monks. 'Mrs. Cottrell was the ,soloist, and the meeting was presided over by Mr. F. Wright. Special reference was made to Mr. W. Seed, the roll-call secretary, who has entered the Army, and left for Plymouth on Monday to join his regiment.</p>
        <p>Successful Choir Concert.—There was a good audience at a concert held in the Wesley Hall on Thursday, the proceeds being in aid of the choir funds. There was an attractive list of artistes, and the programme was a very in- teresting one. Miss Nora Lewins (soprano), of Manchester, gave an excellent rendering of Gounod's " Serenade " and of the ballad "May Day at, Islington," after which she sang "In my garden,' as/an encore. She was equally suc- cessful in Elgar's "Land of Hope and Glory," in which she was assisted by the choir. Mrs. E. A. Sanders, who is well known in Warrington musical circles, sang "There's a land" and " Mifanwy," afterwards contributing ." Two Eyes Of Grey" and "Good-night, but not good- bye," as encores. Mr. George Walker contri- buted "The Carnival" and "Mountain Lovers," and Mr. F. Moon " England " and "The Village Blacksmith," whilst together the two sang the duet, "The Battle Eve." Mr. A. Dutton created roars of laughter with his humorous character study entitled "The Caretaker," and was equally successful in "The Night," " Uriah Heap" (from Dickens), and "The Student." He was recalled each time, and as encores gave "The Hindoo's Paradise" and "The Sweep." Mrs. Haviour, a violinist of considerable repute, gave an exquisite rendering of " Plainte Armour," and also played a dainty gavotte and Berthold Tour's " Seconde Romance," receiving well-deserved encores for each item. The choir, under the conductoi'ship of Mr. W. J. Bate, sang with more than usual expression and vigour Elgar'Sietting of "The National Anthem," "0, My Love is like a Red; Red Rose" (Cornwall), "Night, Lovely Night" (Berger), "March of the Men of Harlech " (Barnby), and "You Stole My Love" (Macfarren), being requested to repeat the latter item. -A quartet, entitled " Howl Sweet the Calm," was else well rendered by members of the choir. The' accompanists were Mrs. Bate and Mr. W. H. Ward, and Mr. F. Hickman, of Warrington, occupied the chair. Votes of thanks to the chairman, artistes, &amp;c., were proposed by Mr. Duckworth and the Rev.. A. J. Morton, and carried with applause.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>PRIVATE E. STRINGER.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE E. STRINGER.</p>
        <p>Cheshire Lines Platelayer killed In France.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Joseph Stringer, of Railway Cottages, Padgate, have this week received news that their son, Private .Ernest Stringer, was killed in action on November 3rd. The first intimation of the sa4 news was conveyed to his</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Cyclist-entertainers At Wet'cyan., School.— On Tuesday evening the musical members of the Warrington Wesleyan Cycling Club gave their first concert in the Wesleyan Schoolroom, Penketh. The affair was a decided success, the programme, which was a long one, being greatly enjoyed by a large and appreciative audience. The artistes were: Miss Turner (pianoforfe solos), Miss Ward, Miss Kershaw, Miss Jones, Miss P. Jones and Mr. Turner (solos), Mr. Whitfield (recitations), Mr. C. Wilcockson (readings), and Mr. Lawton (one-stringed violin solos). The Rev. 11. T. Dixon was in the chair.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>TO STIMULATE RECRUITING.</head>
        <p>TO STIMULATE RECRUITING.</p>
        <p>Local Committee Appointed And Workers Wanted.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening a meeting was held in the Penketh Council School to elect a recruiting committee, and secure canvassers to call on the eligible men of the district. Mr. J. B. Aitken was voted to the chair. Mr. F. J. Downes, one of the joint recruiting secretaries of the Newton Division, addressed the meeting.</p>
        <p>Mr. Aitken was unanimously elected chairman of the Recruiting Committee, and Mr. D. R. Griffiths secretary. A proposal that the Parish Council should form the committee, with power to add to their numbers, met with some slight opposition, one gentleman remarking that as most of the Coun- cillors were absent from that meeting it did not look 'as if they took much interest in the pro- cedings.</p>
        <p>The motion was, however, Volunteer canvassers were asked for, but, as most of those present were of military age, only four could offer their services. More will be appointed later. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>ORFORD.</head>
        <p>ORFORD.</p>
        <p>WMTLEY.</p>
        <p>Licence That Came Too Late.—William Henry Sutton, a farmer, of Antrobus, was fined ss. at the Daresbury Petty Sessions, held at Stockton. Heath on Tuesday, for • failing to deliver up the licence after removing a .number of pigs.—He sent the licence a week too late. The Farmer and the Summons.—Hubert Acton, farmer, Whitley Inferior, pleaded "not guilty" at the Daresbury Petty Sessions on Tuesday to a summons for allowing two cows to stray on the highway.—The defendant caused much amusement in Court by pointing out a series of mistakes that had been made on the summons. His Christian name, his address and the description of the animals had been wrongly entered. He claimed that the animals that had strayed were heifers, not cows. They followed a donkey on to the road.—He was fined 4s. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>STRUCK BY FALLING SACK.</head>
        <p>STRUCK BY FALLING SACK.</p>
        <p>Fatal End To Accident To Corn</p>
        <p>Merchant's Employe.</p>
        <p>Mr. Coroner Brighouse held an inquest or Tuesday morning at the Borough Police Station, .Warrington, on the body of James Donaldson, aged 47 years, of 63, Marbury-street, Witrrington, who met with a fatal accident whilst in the employ of Messrs. Stanley Carter and Son, Ltd., corn merchants, Bridge-street, Warrington. Present at the inquiry were Mr. Brother,:4 Inspector under the Factory Acts), Mr. Cooper (of Messrs. Robert Davies and Co.), representing .the employers, and Mr. Sidney Wallhead, o behalf of the relatives.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Dona the widow, said, that her husband left home dfl the 25th of last month in good health. On the ktittic day she was told that he had met with aft ateideht, a -sack hav- ing fallen on him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>How the Accident Happetied.</head>
        <p>How the Accident Happetied.</p>
        <p>Richard Ward, 67, Ash-lane, Latchford, a foreman miller, employed by Messrs. Stanley Carter and Son, .Ld., said .that at 11 on the. 25th ult., he was employed loadifig a lorry with sacks at the firm's warehouse ifi Vernon-street. . He was at the .hoist, which was on the top storey. Donaldson was below receiving the 'sacks. One of the sacks slipped off the ring and struck Donaldson a glancing blow, causing him to collapse underneath it. He was taken in a taxi-cab \to the ..Infirmary. The sack weighed 1691 b., and the drop was about 20ft.</p>
        <p>Similar evidence was given by • Frederick Crompton, a carter, who was passing at the time of the accident.</p>
        <p>Following Custom</p>
        <p>Mr. Carter, a representative of the firm, stated that on the .day of the accident the men were loading the lorries in accordance with their usual method; which was the recognised method in the trade. •</p>
        <p>Addressing the jury, the Coroner said that the hoistman was carrying out his duty in the way that he had been taught. A verdict of "Accidental death" was re- turned.- -the jury exonerating the hoistraan from blame.</p>
        <p>At birth a child was rubbed over with salt. This was supposed to cleanse it, for the term of its natural life. After salting, washing was never considered necessary, and in the East at the present time there are many of the poorer classes who are never washed from the cradle to the grave. After being salted a child was swaddled, that is, wrapped Up in long strips of cloth. The "swaddling clothes" consisted of strips three yards long and six inches wide, and these were boUnd round the child so tightly that it could not move. Only once in seven days did it enjoy the luxury of kicking its legs when the clothes were removed and fresh salt applied. The swaddling period was forty days. Friends pay a visit to the home where a boy has appeared, and, like the Wise Men from the East, who visited Bethlehem„ they always bring a gift with them. Children are suckled longer in Eastern lands. They are kept, at the mother's breast for three or four years. When, therefore, we read in the Bible of "babes and sucklings" we must not think of tiny infants, but of young children. As soon as a child can sit upright it is placed on its mother's shoulders, and she usually car- ries it. in this fashion. It has the effect of mak- ing the children 'capable horsemen and women from their earliest years. After learning to balance- themselves and hold on by their knees, they never need to learn to ride a horse.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <p>PRIVATE E. STRINGER. Killed in Action.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <p>parents in a message from the deceased's chum, Private J. Ashton, who states in a letter dated November sth :—" Just a line to let you know that Ernest was killed at 3 o'clock on November 3rd. It has upset me very much; for he was my best pal, and in your sorrow all the boys and myself send you their deepest sympathy. He was well liked by all of us. We buried him half a mile behind the firing line to-day, the sth inst.</p>
        <p>Private E. Stringer was 23 years of age, and unmarried, ,and in his youth attended the Pad- gate Church Schools, worshipping at the same church. Although of a quiet disposition, he was nevertheless greatly esteemed and admired by all who came in contact with him. He was a member of the Primrose League, and was employed as a platelayer by the Cheshire Lines Committee.</p>
        <p>Private Stringer enlisted on September 2nd. 1914, in the 2nd South Lancs., and at the com- mencement was stationed at Orford Barracks, subsequently going into training at Crosby, Six months later he was drafted to France.</p>
        <p>He was at the Rouen base for a fortnight, eventually departing to the firing line "some- where in Belgium." Only three weeks ago he wrote home to say that he had been sett back to France:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE ArOLUNTEER REGIMENT</p>
        <p>Grappenhall Detachment. Orders for week commencing 14th November: Tuesday, 16th November.—Squad Drill. Fall in at Headquarters at 7.40 p.m.. sharp. Saturday, 20th November.—Parade (in ' uniform). . Fall in at Headquarters at 3.25 p.m. sharp.</p>
        <p>N.B.—Arrangements have now been com- pleted with regard to shooting, and a good muster is requested on Tuesday, so that the attendance at the range can be arranged.</p>
        <p>Orderlies.—Section-Commander C. G. Butch- ings and Privato J. Fairhurst. By Order, F. A; FROST, 0.C., Platoon Commander.</p>
        <p>Headquarters, .13th Noveniher, 1915.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>TEAMSTER WHO LOST MS TEMPER.</head>
        <p>TEAMSTER WHO LOST MS TEMPER.</p>
        <p>Fined For Assault And Ordered To Pay Damages.</p>
        <p>At the Daresbury Petty Sessions, held at Stockton Heath ,on 'Tuesday, before Councillor H. Roberts, Messrs. J. Hepherd, J. J. Whitley, W. Fletcher and J. A. Ransome, Harry Shuffle- botham, a teamster, in the employ of Mr. john Bradford, . Grappenhall, was summoned for assaulting Arthur Henry Warburton; with assaulting Mrs.. Sarah Warburton; with break- ing a gatepost; with doing damage to the value of • 135.; and also with improper conduct., The defendant was represented .by Mr. T. S. Steel, who pleaded not guilty. Mr. Arthur Browne prosecuted on behalf of the complainants. Took Gate Off Its Hinges.</p>
        <p>Opening the case, Mr. Browne said that Mrs. Sarah Warburton was the tenant,of the Manor -House Farm, Partridge-lane, Grappenhall, and her son assisted her in the management of the farm. The defendynt was a teamster in the em- ploy of Mr. John Bradford, who resided close to the complainants' house. Defendant had been employed by Mr. Bradford for some months and since he came there had been constant an- noyances to Mr. and Mrs. Warburton. On Sunday, October 17th, at 2.30, the defendant and another man—whom they had since learned was the defendant's brother—came up the public footpath thrcugh the complainants' farm. When they got into the yard the defendant immedi- ately behaved indecently before Mrs. Warbur- ton and a servant. .Mr. Warburton .IVils putting two calves in a shippon. The two men had to go</p>
        <p>Death From Anthrax.—The death occurred On Wednesday at the Warrington Infirmary of John Arthur Robson (52), who reSided at the Feld, Orford. He was admitted to the Infirm- ary on Saturday suffering from anthrax, and died at 9.20 p.m. on Wednesday. "Splendid Fellows."—" What splendid fellows you have sent out from your parish," writes a lady from "somewhere in England," to the Rev. G. Vale Owen, vicar of Orford, "and how good to be in such living touch with them." This comment has reference to the fact that the reverend gentleman receives and publishes in his Parish .Magazine many letters from Orford men at the Front and in training. "We have had Lancashire regiments quartered near us some months," she proceeds, "and have liked them particularly on account of their straight- forwardness and ready response to any kind- ness. My daughter got up socials for them and we have had them in for tea and have met with such a pleasant response.. I liked their hearty 'Good . night, mother!' when w stood to see them on their way to the Dardanelles."</p>
      </div>
