      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>Workers' Charges Denied At Munitions Tribunal.</head>
        <p>Workers' Charges Denied At Munitions Tribunal.</p>
        <p>MORE CERTIFICATES REFUSED.</p>
        <p>At a sitting of the Warrington Munitions Tribunal held on Monday a tool fitter at a large works in the town applied for his certi- ficate—enabling him to go to work elsewhere— and alleged that his employers had wrongfully withheld the same. In the course of a written statement, read by Mr. G. A. White, the Clerk to the Tribunal, the applicant set forth the fact that having previously made applica- tion for a certificate, the case came before that Tribunal at a previous sitting. He had to come to Warrington by train, and as he was unavoidably delayed, and unable to give evidence, the case was dismissed. Since then his working conditions had become worse, until at present they were intolerable, owing to persistent snubbing and boycotting. During the last two or three weeks the work of repairing and altering the lathes and other machines in his department had been taken from him and his mate, and, without a word of explanation, given to another man. This job had always been recognised as belonging to tool-fitters, and the case was so pronounced that it had caused the other men in the shop to ask them the reason why " so' and so" got all their work: Applicant had now to be satisfied with any odd job that came along. An Alleged False Charge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>SAID HIS WAGES WERE NOT ENOUGH.</head>
        <p>SAID HIS WAGES WERE NOT ENOUGH.</p>
        <p>An engine-cleaner employed by the London and North-Western Railway Company at Sutton Oak Station complained that he had been re- fused a certificate, though he desired to be transferred to a munitions works. His present wages were not enough to pay for his food, the standing rate being only 14s. a week. The railway company's representative said that the work applicant was doing was of great importance, as it was necessary for Gov- ernment transport. The company was short of mcn, and applicant could not be spared. The certificate was refused.</p>
        <p>sitting with the President, as assessors, were Mr. C. W. Emmett, of St. Helens (for the em- ployers) and Mr. George Benson, of Wigan (for the employes).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>DUTTON.</head>
        <p>DUTTON.</p>
        <p>Stole Special Constibleis tisili:=-At the Daresbury Petty Sessions, field itt Stocicton. lieettb on Tuesday, James Rini,' a farm labourer, pleaded guilty to a charge of steatinii two hens, valued at is., the property of George Kinsey, of Prospect Farm, Dutton.—Evidence was given by Mr. Samuel Kinsey, who said that the defendant was found with the two fowls in his possession.—A fine of 20s. was inflicted, the alternative being 14 days' imprisonment.—Mr. Kinsey, who is a special constable, was com- plimented by Mr. Henry Roberts, the chairman of tha Bench, upon his smart capture.</p>
