      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>CHILD LIFE IN THE HST.</head>
        <p>CHILD LIFE IN THE HST.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. Mounfield Tells Of Curious Customs.</p>
        <p>At the fifth of the series of lectures on "Eastern Life," given by Mr. A. Mounfield in the Friars' Green Schoolroom, there was again a very appreciative audience. The subject was "Child Life in the East." There was still, said the lecturer, as through all past ages, a marked difference between the reception of a boy and a girl. When a boy was born a messenger ran to the father with the cry "Good tidings, a son is born." The father would hasten to name the child, and afterwards would call himself by the child's name.- Thus if the 'child were named Isaac, the father would become Abou-Isaac, i.e., the father of Isaac. \then the birth of a girl had to be announced there were no words too gloomy. "God bless thee," the messenger would say, and then, looking forward to the lay when the girl would be old enough to be 116 Id as a bride, he might say, "Blessed be the bride." A girl was of no concern until she was old enough to be married, and was never counted in among the children Never Been Washed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>Flies Cause Blindness.</head>
        <p>Flies Cause Blindness.</p>
        <p>A great scourge of the children of the East has always been the plague of flies, which settle around the eyes and mouth. At first the chil- dren fight them, but after a time they come to take little notice, and may be seen playing con- tentedly, though' both eyeq..,and mouth 'are black with flies. This is the chief cause of the preva- lent blindness of the East:, The flies carry disease germs, and few children escape eye disease. About one in ten or twelve becomes blind. This will explain the frequent mention of blindness in the Bible. Great as is the evil, the parents, to this day, make no effort to drive off the flies; they are regarded as part of the fixed order of things. _</p>
        <p>_ A boy's earliest accomplishments are swear- ing and lying. As soon .as he has learned to say " father " and " mother "- he learns to swear, and his growing 'proficiency is the admiration of his father and , father's friend, who predict a great future for him. Lying comes next, and clever lying is always applauded. It is import- ant to remember in reading Bible stories which tell of deceit and fraud, that among the Orien- tals clever lying is regarded as a valuable accom- plishment. The Arabs have a proverb, "Lying is the salt of man," and they would approve and commend the frauds of acob and of his father-ill-law 'Laban.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>Female Burden.</head>
        <p>The Female Burden.</p>
        <p>Girls have always felt the burden of life very heavily in the East. They ?lever know playthings, and as soon as they can walk and run become the water carriers of the family. Woman has always been the beast of burden, and since the days when Rebecca carried the water, pitcher has clone the heavy tasks. To the present hour in the land where Jesus lived and taught, women ure regarded as goods and chat- tels, bought and paid for, and a man a1v47,5 refers to laio wifo us 'this thins." - Mr. Afou.nfieht dc,sombeu the streets and homes of an Eastern town, and the scenes.with which a child would become familiar—the market- place and gate of the City, and the games the children played there in imitation of their elders, until the day came when they were mar- ried and given in marriage, and began a new chapter in life.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>HIM Weddings Arc Arranged,</head>
        <p>HIM Weddings Arc Arranged,</p>
        <p>Martiagog itb:ayg arratod by pronts and not by the young people themselves. Thus Abraham sent a trusted servant to find a wife for Isaac, and Samson asked his father to secure him a bride. The „marriage .was a serious business proposition—much too serious to admit of any considerations of love entering into it. The fathers •of the prospective bride and bridegroom bargained over it and made a binding agreement as to .dowry and 'gifts. The young 'folks were married and the wedding feast begun in many cases before they even saw each :other.</p>
        <p>The story of Jacob and his marriages appears very unreal when read by Western eyes, . yet the same thing may occur to-day among the *Arabs. A father will try to firsf dispose of his less favoured daughters and will resort to any kind of strategy ill elfotts. to get them turned to lirofit, As the brae wears a thick and large blue éil dartng the cercniony, and as it would be conadered inunodest for her to speak, it is possible to hide the- wrong daughter under the veil and t.:,;'6.( he Married. To' guard against this fraud thes bfidegroonf minally' arranges that his mother shall See the placed and shall , keep close to the bride until the Ceremony is performed. Jacob had no Mother at hand to watch his interests, and found himself married to Leah instead of Rachel.</p>
        <p>. , . It is still a common thing, for n bridegrooni who cannot pay Cash down for .a bride to make weekly payments, and when. he bas n,o money to offer labour to the bride's father: in lieu of it. When Jacob agreed to serve seven years for liachel he was following a cowl:non- Eastern custom, and it will be noted that the service had to be rendered in full before the father handed over his daughter. , . The lecture next ,11ridny. Will deal, with the life of Eastern women and Eastern' hospitality.</p>
        <p>"This," the written statement went on, "is the work of the under-foreman, who, after never speaking during this time, put the climax on the matter by having me brought before the foreman on a false charge of creating discontent amongst the youths of the department. The foreman, in making the charge, also said a lot of other things, ending up with the statement that since my case had been up, there had been nothing but bother on my bench. This statement and the accusation are utterly absurd, as I had not even spoken to those concerned. A few hours after, I asked the foreman for my discharge, pointing out the falseness of the position, and also telling him that I would rather go if it would save any further ill-feeling." Answering' a representative of the firm, the applicant said that he had not complained to any one in a responsible position, because he had not deemed it necessary to do so. Be denied that there was work lying on his bench for a long time and which he had not com- pleted. The Chairman (Mr. T. J. Ridgway) : Why do you wish to leave this firm? Applicant; I want to go to another works where I can do more munitions work. r The: Chairman: More than you have been doing? Applicant; Yes, I want to go to place where I can be of better service to the country. NO CAUSE FOR REASONABLE COMPLAINT.</p>
        <p>The under-foreman of the firm said he failed to see how the applicant's position had, as he alleged, "become intolerable," for he, person- ally, had said nothing to the man about which he could reasonably complain, nor had he seen anything in the way of "snubbing and boy- cotting" going on in the shop. He denied that he was the cause of the charges being made against the applicant, to the effect that he sought to stir up trouble among the youths in the department. All he did in the matter was to do his duty. The certificate was refused.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>HAVE WE SAVED ?</head>
        <p>HAVE WE SAVED ?</p>
        <p>Actual Experiences In The Thrift Campaign.</p>
        <p>" lIOLSEKEEPER'S " IIL MOLE.</p>
        <p>We have received the following from "A Housekeeper " : I remember reading the following from a speech of Mr, .McKenna's, in which he said</p>
        <p>"In our present circumstances extravagance is a crime and economy the highest of national virtues. We must look to the individual acting alone in his home for the foundation of the national resources by the help of which alone we and our Allies can obtain the triumph we look forward to." _</p>
        <p>I determined that I would at once begin the practice of "the highest of national vittues," so I got into my home-trench, so to speak, and have ever since been up to the knees in the uncomfortable waters of economy. I became a diligent reader of the advice given in connec- tion • with saving, and did not find much that I could.apply. I read that "all treating should be given up until we could toast to the final and complete victory. No drinks until we have won." Most religiously have I followed this advice, but as I neither drank intoxicants nor treated others before the war, I cannot claim virtue for abstaining now. , _</p>
        <p>Again I read: "Nothing but the most spare expenditure on mourning or funerals should be indulged in!' This, too, I have observed, but as none of my near relatives have passed away, • and as I happen to be a Christian and therefore do not believe in doleful mourning, I can claim no credit under this bead. We were told to be economical in the use of gas; this, too, I have observed, but under com- pulsion, as I have not been able to get enough gas to read by, far'less.to waste, and the credit here must go to the Warrington Gas Commit- tee. I only wish that their passion for economy wire a little less erratic. Why, for example, should this economical fit last only until about seven every evening, and then why should they suddenly revert for a time to pre-war pressure, involving a resort to the complicated arrange- ments on our burners for regulating air and gas pressures? The turning of awkward little screws is interesting for a time, but you soon realise that it is impossible to keep pace with the changing moods of Warrington gas, and turn all full on and let economy go to the winds.</p>
        <p>We were told that we were to economise by buying bread which is at lea 4 twenty-four hours .01d. This rule Ihave followed for the last thirty years. Who wants to invite pains by eating new bread? We were told to be careful in the use of coals so that there would be more coal to make shells. I think I have scored in this if in nothing else. I turned out an old-fashioned, high-standing grate from the, parlour and put in a modern low-set one, and so did away with all cinders. Any cinders that fall into the shallow tray below the fire are by the heat— calcined, do you call it?—and conve,xted into small ash. I have been most careful in back- ing. up the fires with small coal, which I got for the purpose when an active fire was not required. By this means I have saved about twenty per cent. of the coal consumption in the parlour fire, and a good deal in the kitchen by more careful firing. I do not know that I can tell you much more what I have been able to effect, the other sav- ings have been in a number of small things which seem too trifling to mention in a letter to the paper, but which in the aggregate and over the war period have amounted to a fairly big sum on my small outgoings during the war. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>OCCASIONAL E X PEN DITUR E.</head>
        <p>OCCASIONAL E X PEN DITUR E.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of " The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—You ask for experiences in economy. Here are some from one habituated to sim- plicity before the war began. To a total abstainer, intoxicants offered no field for ex- periment, nor was I a consumer of mineral waters. A non-smoker cannot sacrifice tobacco, though a cigarette smoker can turn to the pipe for consolation and show a 'saving in the pro- cess. Tons of tobacco are thrown away in the fag- ends of cigarettes. _ _ • .</p>
        <p>But after all, each man has his own. pet vice. Some men steal home and quietly slip a new book on the bookshelf. Reading is one of my vices. So I turned to my newspaper account with the eye of an economist and plunged in the sacrificial knife. The first to be slain wtts "The Examiner." Then came a religious weekly, and finally the penny daily paper was given up in favour of a halfpenny journal. It may comfort you to know that though the newsagent no longer delivers "The Examiner," I have invariably felt impelled to buy it across the counter. I know this is economically wrong, but after enjoying the pleasures of sin for a season I shall go back repentant to the newsagent and place your paper once more on the list, so that you may know beforehand what your demands will be. After six weeks of the halfpenny Press, I reverted to my penny daily as a small contribu- tion to national sobriety of judgment. One has to pay for truth in perspective. The net effect of my attack on my newspaper bill is that I am risking my immortal soul (at the price of 4s. 4(1. per annum) by doing without a " religious " journal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>Neckties, Literature and Crockery.</head>
        <p>Neckties, Literature and Crockery.</p>
        <p>One can save more easily upon occasional ex- penditures than upon those that occur day by day. For example I refused to go to the wed- ding of a near relative in the Midlands. Then I took an unusually short holiday in the summer and went to the Wirral Peninsula instead of farther afield, thus saving on railway fares for the family. I frequently resist the fascinations of .neckties, books and china. Household re- newals are also debarred as much as possible. It may be said that trade is thus injured. But the reply is that just now the nation is bound to spend on so huge a scale that every citizen who curbs private expenditure is doing a national service. It must also be remembered that when munition work ceases the trade of the country will be very grateful for any demand for goods which has been deferred until that time.</p>
        <p>The Butcher's Bill.</p>
        <p>But the most useful day to day economy in</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>How to Reduce Your Meat Bill!</head>
        <p>How to Reduce Your Meat Bill!</p>
        <p>Make delicious and nutritious puddings. with apples, figs, dates, golden syrup, jam, Sze., and Shredded ATORA Beef. Suet. Always ready for use and saves all the trouble of chopping. 10Id per 11b. carton whether block or shredded, and VA. per lib. carton. Ask your grocer for it; rtiuse substitutes.</p>
        <p>"The Cook's Best Friend." , ; BORWICK'S 1 .0, ~, BAKING POWDER.</p>
        <p>my household is the reduction of the butcher's bill. This again is a national necessity owing to the shortage of supplies. Fish at present for the same reason is not an economical substi- tute. Twice a week my mid-day meal is of a vegetarian character,. and I wish every restaurant would at least offer alternatives to meat dishes. Well-to-do people can set a use- ful-example by adopting a non-flesh diet regu- larly, say on Tuesdays and Fridays. Poorer people won't have thrift thrust down their throats, but they will gladly follow richer neigh- bours who show the way in practical economy. The burden of the war will be felt with increas- ing severity by the poor, and now is the time for middle-class people to set the pace in sim- plicity. Bacon, Butter and Christmas Fare.</p>
        <p>Breakfast bacon occupies a very subsidiary position in our household, (Can. anyone ex- plain why bacon is cheaper in Wallasey than in Warrington 1') It only appears once or twic,_ a week, and there are plenty of other suitabh. dishes available if only housekeepers will throw aside their conservative habits. I have eaten margarine at 6d. per lb. ever -since the war began, and I have done so on the strength of assurances that it is as nourishing as butter, and is made under hygienic conditions. It not so palatable as best Devonshire butter, but it does not compare unfavourably with the rather characterless Danish butter _so largely eaten in the North of .England. Christmas puddings and mince meat. are not going to be made in our household this year. I am quite'sure that an opportunity is coming for the simplification of Christmas festivities more in keeping With Christian than pagan customs. Christmas cards are also barred this year.</p>
        <p>J hope others may give us the benefit of their experiences. Meantime I remain,</p>
        <p>SIMPLE SIMON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>AFTER MISS PETTY'S VISIT.</head>
        <p>AFTER MISS PETTY'S VISIT.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of " The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,--The • Ladies' Thrift Committee which arranged the recent visit of Miss Petty has since been meeting, and I am commissioned to write to you in their name. Not only were the lectures widely appreciated, but women of all classes went home to put in practice many of Miss Petty's hints. It is still more gratifying to and that the men are saying "Encore." It is felt by the Ladies' Committee that a great deal can be done to follow up the work of " The Pudding Lady," especially in existing societies where women are accustomed to meet. Many hundreds of booklets containing econ-</p>
        <p>miical recipes have been purchased in War- rington during the. last few weeks, and it is iuggested that competitions should be held and mall prizes given for cheap dishes. The use of the blackboard is recommended in small gather- ings as well as the showing of samples of economical foods not yet in popular use. When special dishes are being advocated, it is not enough that they should be nourishing and economical. They must be .palatable if they are to become popular. ("The proof of the pudding is in the eating.") It is therefore wise for anyone who means to make recommenda- tions to test them first of all at home. After all the practitioner is the best preacher.</p>
        <p>The Ladies' Committee is so -deeply impressed with the national need for reducing the con- sumption of meat that some of them are adopt- ing the principle of having meatless dinners twice a week, without, of course, any binding pledge. They venture to think that this plan might be generally -followed not only by the poorest, but by those whose means are ample. In doing so, of course, it is • essential to find substitutes equally good for the maintenance of robust health. • •</p>
        <p>The committee will welcome any practical suggestions which may be sent to me at Win- marleigh Chambers, and they will be equally glad to respond to any inquiries at the office about suitable leaflets, other literature, &amp;c. Yours, &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>WILFRID J. ROWLAND.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>WOMEN WAR WORKERS.</head>
        <p>WOMEN WAR WORKERS.</p>
        <p>Trades Council's Fears For The Future.</p>
        <p>It was reported at the monthly meeting cf the Warrington Trades and Labour Council on Tuesday night that the Executive Committee had resolved with regard to the question of woman labour, at the present juncture, that delegates be asked to report the extent to which such labour had been introduced into the varibus trades, and, 'in cases Fherei. female labour is introduced and there is no organisa- tion catering for the women workers, that the assistance of the Council be asked, with a view to putting such workers in touch with those Trade Unions which admitted women workers as members.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>MAY BECOME PERMANENT.</head>
        <p>MAY BECOME PERMANENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Charles Dukes (the secretary to the Council) stated that the view of the Executive Was that the bulk of the female labour now being introduced as a matter of war expediency was likely, at the termination of the war, to resolve itself into a permanent phase of the national life. Mere resolutions from Trade Union branches would not be sufficient to turn the women adrift; and the men nad got to recognise them as a swelling factor in the labour market. The contention of the Trade Unions was that, to combat this, women should be organised. There was hardly an industry into which they had not been introduced, the build- ing industry being the only one that occurred to him at the moment. In all seriousness, he wanted to say that if the Trade Unions did not organise the women, the employers would. At present the women might be paid something like the same rate as the men, but the men must remember that the status of the worker was determined by the law of supply and de- mand, and not by employers' sentiments.</p>
        <p>Women Will Not Want To Go Back To The Home.</p>
        <p>If there was a lot of labour on the market, labour was going to be cheap, and women were not going to return to domestic servility simply because the men proudly requested that they should do so. lie wanted to give a solemn warning, and it was that if they did not gain the sympathies of the women by organising them, they would breed in them a spirit of hostility, the result being that they would have a. black-leg force to contend with in every industry that it would be impossible to cope with at the end .of the war. Woman labour must be cheap, because there was plenty of it, and some of the work which was at present being done at cheap rates by women might have been still done by men at fair wages, but for their own negligence in the matter of organisation. The cotton and fustian workers were cases in point, to say nothing of the chemical workers and cardboard box worker. The women worker's of the town were un- organised and, yet, almost to a man their fathers and brothers; were Trade Unionists. He wanted to know what the men were doing about it. There was no excuse for the women being outside the Unions—only the men's own in- dolence... .SPECIAL UNIONS FOR WOMEN.</p>
        <p>Mr. Berry thought women workers should be catered for, in the matter of Trade Unionism, apart from the men, and he suggested the call- ing of a special meetink. of women working in trades at the present time. They could scarcely 'expect girls to come and mix up with the men's organisations, and thought the Unions would be well advised to open out special sections for female labour, with female officers.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dukes: If you leave women to organiSe themselves, you will find that they won't! Women are anarchists. The executive's minutes were endorsed.</p>
        <p>Some- fellows seem to lose sight of the fat that it's the night before that makes the morn- ing after,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 13, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 13, 1915.</p>
        <p>t....)&lt;•+++++•+++40404.44+46+++.+40.44 0+.4" +4o.o.4•44++*ftp +o+ 44..44. 4.+5.+.440+0 4,4. 4-4:- E• 44- 3 . • - .4:: S WORLD.</p>
        <p>far reaching consequences are now being carried on in the matter of women workers. Out of a total of six hundred hands, five hundred are girls and women. Nearly all are drawn from classes that hitherto have not worked for wages, and there is a demand for educated women a, overseers of factory workers. The Normal Waist. •</p>
        <p>- --- - The waist this autumn is in its normal position,. although Empire modes never go quite out of fashion. • That the long-fronted corsage was coming into fashion was evident, long before the war, and like the full short skirt, its advent was merely delayed. The tight, or tighter waist line may be noted in a number of evening corsages. • These have seams on either side of the front, and are kept in place by bone. Wide skirts of the bouffant type demand long, tight bodices. The influence of the watteau period is distinctly visible in many of the new models, and the return of the .fichu is a. sign _of the influence. A v-shaped opening is often filled with merely a fold or two of white mousseline de sae°, quite severe in simplieity.!, A feature of this season's modes is the curious but often pleasing effect of blending unlikely materials. Thus tulle, net, and velvet or velveteen form evening gowns, and ribbon, in some instances at least three- quarters of a yard in width, is used as a trim- ming at the hem of a smart toilette of some transparent fabric. Dervelyn has been greatly improved, and with fine cloth, velvet, soft satin, gaberdine, and, of course, navy serge, takes pride of place among hosts of suitable materials.</p>
        <p>The Coming House Coat. The blouse, in a variety that may be truly described as infinite, is still as indispensable as ever. The house coat, though, may prove a rival of some importance in the near future. in crepe de chine of a heavy make this coat is a most covetable garment, when made with a kilted back to the basque and high military collar. It usually has a wide, loose belt with fancy buckle. The sleeveless mess jacket of taffetas is an accessory that will be popular during .the winter. The cross-over blouse is again in evidence; one example appears to con- sist of a wide ssarf of a delicate fabric, edged with fur, or marabout, which drapes the shoul- ders, crosses in front, forming a V-shaped open- ing, and then ties at the back apparently over a bustle. The lace vest is an indispensable accompaniment of the dressy blouse, when the latter is made of transparent material.</p>
        <p>with epper ai salt, put ill., steak (in a dish, remove the tape or skewer, stra-in the gravy over, and serve.</p>
        <p>Savoury Pie. Ingredients: Pb. of stewing steak, lilb. of potatoes, Ilb. of onions, 1 teacupful of unpolished rice, some good dripping. Method: Slice the onions .and fry them and the steak *until brown in hot dripping; then put them into a stew-jar with cold water to cover, and cook slowly for two hours. Wash the rice and boil it until tender, boil and mash the potatoeq, and cut the steak into small pieces. Grease a good sized pie dish and put in first a layer of rice, next a layer of steak, and then repeat the process until these ingredients (tie all used. Pear over sonic of the gravy, season to taste, cover with the mashed potatoes, and then bake in the oven until nicely brown.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>Women Workers For The War Office</p>
        <p>With commendable foresight the National Political League have trained three hundred women for various kinds of agricultural work, and have also given short training to women anxious to give their services in various emergency war works. It is satisfactory to know that all have proved thoroughly efficient. Some of these- women are working for the War Office, in the Forage and Remounts Departments, where they have the care of sick horses, and superintend the baling of hay, the latter by no _ means a feminine occupation, but carried out to the entire satisfaction of the War Office. Any shortage of food arising from the withdrawal of large numbers of agricul- tural labourers for the Army is a national danger, and it is for women, irrespective of class or condition, to do what they can to solve the problem. Women And Munitions.</p>
        <p>It is probable that in the near future an in- creased call will be made on the working and other classes for the making of munitions. Thousands of women are willing and anxious to do their part, and as much. of the labour in the new factories is only half-skilled, which takes but three weeks to learh, there is no reason why the authorities should not avail themselves of this undeveloped source of supply. Scores of firms can testify that women are already successfully- employed in shell-making. In one factory near Birmingham three thousand girls are working the lathes that pro- duce certain parts of shells. The lathes were constructed specially for them, and are lighter than these made for men. The greater pro- portion of these women had never before the war, worked in factories. Many were employed in shops, warehouses, in domestic Service, and many have only lately left school. A certain number consist of girls of a superior class, who are helping from patriotic motives. All earn good wages, ranging from thirty shillings to three pounds a week. In one Midland town a Munitions Committee have organised a national shell factory, and experiments that may have</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>11011 E NOTES.</head>
        <p>11011 E NOTES.</p>
        <p>Profitable Recipes And Useful Suggestions.</p>
        <p>Stewed Rabbit With Brown Sauce.</p>
        <p>Prepare a rabbit in the usual and at it into neat joints. Remove the rind fiem 3oz. el sliced bacon and cut the slices into small pieces; cook these in a stewpan inatil the fat -part is transparent, anq then remcve them to a plate. Coat the joints of the rabbit with a mixture of flour, salt and pe?pv, and fry these in the liquor left by tho bacon until they are broWn on all sides, adding a little more fat if neces- sary. Then add two onions cut into slices, and brown these also. Return the pieces of bacon to the stewpan, grate a little -nutmeg over the contents, and pour in one pint of stwk. Put the lid on the stewpan and cook vr-ry gently untA the rabbit is tender. Send to table with a dish of baked tomatoes.—" Guardian." Boiled Fowl.</p>
        <p>Truss the fowl nicely for boiling, cutting off the leg bones at the knee joint; boil it for about an hour and a half (it .cannot cook too gently), according to size and age. Serve with parslay sauce, and send it to table with boiled bacon. Macaroni Soup. •</p>
        <p>Method Boil a mutton bone in the stock with two onions, a tiny piece Of carrot and turnip, for two hours; strain, remora fat, thicken with flour and butter. Break an ounce or go of macaroni into short lengths, boil it, and throw it into the soup. -Season wi% pepper and salt, add lialf a pint of milk and two pennywottli of crelu., and serve. How to Choose a Goose.</p>
        <p>When a goose is young the bill is yellow, which turns red when the bird grows old. When fresh killed the feet are supple, and when stale they 'become stiff 'and dry.-Goese shouLl Le large and fat, and the white and grey are to be pre- ferred to those of a dark colour. To carve a goose, cut down to the breast-bone, and help out as many slices as can be obtained, taking a little stuffing out of the apron each time. Next remove the legs and wings, cutting the flesh and jerking the joint back, and then separate the neck and side-bones, and finally the back. After the breast, the fleshy part of the wing and thigh are the most favourite parts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>Rolled Steak.</head>
        <p>Rolled Steak.</p>
        <p>Take 11b. or rather more of rump steak ball- inch thick, make some veal stuffing, spread it over the steak, roll up, bind with tape cr skewer it. put into a baking-dish with a little stock, cover closely, and *bake for about two hours; take out the meat, thicken the gravy with loz. butter rolled in a tablespoonful of flour, sea-on</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <p>• • •• •••• • • • ++ • •,•</p>
        <p>Children' : 8 Corner. • •</p>
        <p>• 4 4 4 4 Conducted by UNCLE JACK. • 4 • 4+4.4.4.4.4•••••••4.4.4.4•••••00</p>
        <p>Children, Join This Corner! Enter The Competitions! Write Your Best! Always Be Glad! Uncle Jack, " Examiner," Warrington.</p>
        <p>FAVOURITE FOREIGN COUNTRIES. My DEAR. CHILDREN, You did excellently in our last competi- tion. You remember that I asked you to name your favourite foreign country and I got such a batch of essays on the subject set as used to gladden the heart of our mutual friend, -Uncle Ned, and did actually gladden mine. Of course, if I had contented myself with asking the name of your favourite country there would have been bfit one answer from all of you— BRITAIN. I knew that, and as I wanted to test, in a 'small degree, your knowledge of the other countries of the world I specially asked you to tell me something about the country you would rather live in if you didn't live in the best country in all the world.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>WHY SHE CHOSE CHINA.</head>
        <p>WHY SHE CHOSE CHINA.</p>
        <p>Some of the countries selected by some of my nephews and nieces cause me surprise, not to say merriment. For instance, one niece selects China, "because she thinks Chinese clothes so pretty." If you had to live in China, Edith, you would cheerfully wear what you are pleased to call "our ugly clothes," and live in any land almost save China, in preference, for, take it from me, the boss and girls of China, whilst they may dress in brighter colours and in what you regard as prettier clothes, don't have any- thing like the good time that the boys and girls of our own happy laud—it will be happy again when the war is over, won't it?—have.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>SOME OF THE ESSAYISTS.</head>
        <p>SOME OF THE ESSAYISTS.</p>
        <p>Niece Marian Barton, of V, Wakefield-street, favours Switzerland above all foreign countries, "because it possesses the most beautiful scenery in the whole of Europe." Niece Annie Robinson, .151, Longford-street, casts her vote for India, and her reason is a patriotic one— "because the Hindus have responded so well to the cause of the Allies." Albert Reynolds, of 110. Forster-street, also prefers India, and I rather suspect, from what he, says, that it is because he is particularly fond of tea. But let that pass. Lilian Ilimsworth, of 15, ritzherbert.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>HERE AND TIIERE.</head>
        <p>HERE AND TIIERE.</p>
        <p>A man is as old as he feels and a woman as old as she looks when nobody is looking at her.</p>
        <p>4.</p>
        <p>When a woman says humbly, "I may not be beautiful," she looks as though she thought everybody should rise up and contradict her. THEY FALL IN LOVE WITH HER.</p>
        <p>The Kind Of A Girl Who Wins Hearts—Not Always A Beauty.</p>
        <p>To be really popular means also a great deal more than merely having attention. A girl who isn't liked by other girls finds her success. is an ephenteral one. There is a popular supposition that a beautiful girl is disliked by others less favoured, but I think it is in most cases a fallacy. Girls are quite as susceptible to the beauty of another—if they like her—as are the men. In fact, most beautiful girls have a mall coterie of plainer' ones who quite adore them, -says the Delineator.</p>
        <p>But she who is the most popular is generally the one who is bright and amusing, full of good- natured news, enthusiastic and interested in things outside of her own personal success, and who has a sympathetic appreciation of the feel- ings of others. The girl who is generally popu- lar with boys is the spontaneous, affable, hail fellow sort. This does not mean vulgarity or familiarity in thought or manner—but the girl who meets half way the topics in which the boy is interc,sted; who is ready to laugh at a joke and take one on herself good naturedly. But the girl with whom the men are most apt to fall in love is she of the charming man- ners, of quick sympathy and of tact. She is not inevitably the beauty nor the belle, but has something indescribable that quietly and in- sidiously wins hearts.</p>
        <p>street, tells me that she loves mountains above everything in the way of scenery, and so, of course, she names. Switzerland as her favourite. Joseph Higgins, 37, Bewsey-street, hits on a country that is particularly popular just now, because of the gallant part it has played in the war. Of course, you will guess that I am talking of Belgium. "We have a lot to thank Belgium for," safely remarks Joseph, "for, perhaps, if it had not been for the' Belgians the Germans might have been in England long ago." They might, Joseph, but there's a very big IF ! Muriel Archer, 11, Heathside, is an- other Switzerland lover, and Louie Minshull, 29, Wakefield-street, is a staunch Belgianite; whilst Maud Higham is all for the Russians, who, she assures us, are very persevering people and "set other .people very good examples." Italy claims the affections of Niece Mona Archer, 11, Heathside, and Lucy McEvoy, 32, Wakefield-street, prefers Servia. Adelaide Griffiths, 22, Wakefield-street, is keen on Switzerland, because it " supplies us with tinned milk and chocolate."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>BRITAIN OF THE EAST.</head>
        <p>THE BRITAIN OF THE EAST.</p>
        <p>Jack Barton, 42, Wakefield-street, stands up for Japan, which he tells us has been called. "the Britain of the East," adding the highly- interesting, if not altogether accurate, piece of information, that "the Japanese houses are made of paper because ot the earthquakes, which are very common there." Only one of my nieces favours France—why, I do not know, for France has been the greatest friend of this country for years past, and she is now our staunch and brave ally, just as in the past —you have heard about' Napoleon and Water- loo, haven't you, and about Cressy and Poietiers ?—she used to be our staunchest foe. The niece who favours France is Violetta Crighton, of the Scenic Gallopers, Flag-lane. Violefta writes very nicely, and she spells even the big words correctly—and all my nieces don't do that. Nephew John Webb, 27, Catherine- street, confides.in me that he would like to be a cowboy. THE FIRST PRIZE PAPER.</p>
        <p>It was difficult for Inc to make up my mind, but, on the whole, I think, Mona Archer wins the prize this week, with the following on Italy:— "My favourite foreign country is Italy. It is a very beautiful country. I would very much like to see Mount Vesuvius and the beautiful Bay of Naples, whicli are two of the grandest sights to be seen in Italy. At the base of Mount Vesuvius there once stood two cities, named Pompeii and Herculaneum. These cities were. overwhelmed by an erup- tion of the mountiain, which is volcanic. Forming part of Italy is an island called Elba, which is noted as the place of im- prisonment of Napoleon. The Italians are a very artistic race, and some of . Italy's painters, sculptors and inusiciaiis Irere and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>Oddments.</head>
        <p>Oddments.</p>
        <p>Every period would seem to be represented this winter in one or more items of dress. One of the newest hats savours of the time of Crom- well, and a very severe cape-like collar must have been copied from the fashions 'ruling in the days of the • Protector. With the aid of velvet, the most charming ties and muffs may be made of oddments of peltry and silk, the latter for frills, and the whole finished with a hat to match. Milliners are showing a great variety of these sets, but clever woman can easily make them at home. Tippets again offer opportunities for using up old furs, and these, too, take many forms and shapes. A trimming that deserves mention in connection with velveteen, is a thick cord of gold, silver, or oxidised metal, used to outline collars and cuffs. Tarnished gold gces well with the deep shade of sapphire blue that is so popular this season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>Footwear.</head>
        <p>Footwear.</p>
        <p>Judging from the shows, that despite the war. have been held in many of the exclusive Lon- don salons, skirts are to be both full and short, which will neoessitate great attention to hosiery and to footwear. Clumsy feet were never at a greater disadvantage, but manufacturers have overcome even this defect by specially designed boots and shoes, that give grace and elegance to ungainly _ feet. Frilled stockings and hoots that lace at the back, are novelties that will scarcely survive the winter. Jewelled heels are no longer considered in good taste, but the heel of the high boot is generally of the same colour as the upper. A very neat shoe is in black suede, with the wearer's initial worked on the toe in steel beads.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>Elderberries.</head>
        <p>Elderberries.</p>
        <p>The elderberry is one of our ntan3- neglected fruits, although for its valuable qualities and pleasant refreshing flavour it deserves to be highly esteemed. If it were imported from the other side of the world and sold at a high price it would be considered a delicacy, and valued accordingly. In olden times its berries made a wine that country and townspeople alike appre- ciated and considered palatable. The birds, wiser than human beings, make the most of Nature's gifts, and if the berries were collected and marketed they might be turned to good account. A very delicious jelly can be made from the berries, which are also excellent when stewed. The golden variety of the elder is a very handsome shrub, with delicious berries, and it might, therefore, with profit, find a place in every town garden.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>HINTS ON HEALTH.</head>
        <p>HINTS ON HEALTH.</p>
        <p>Useful Information In Popular Language.</p>
        <p>Tea and the Heart.</p>
        <p>While giving up alcoholic liquors, do not make the mistake of substituting tea in their place. Huchard, one of the most prominent physicians of Paris, a specialist in diseases of the heart, has called attention to the fact that distressing palpitation of the heart is often due to toxic causes which are overlooked. To illustrate this, he gives the case of a fashion- able young woman who had constant and pain- ful palpitation of the heart, and who laboured under the impression that she had an organic disease of the heart. On examination he found no trace of apy disease of the valves or heart structures, and learned that the young lady .was in the habit of spending her afternoons in call- ing, frequently making several stops, and at each taking a cup of tea, so that in the course of the afternoon she drank many cups. Follow- ing his urgent advice, the young lady denoun- ced the use of tea, and in a short time was entirely relieved of her cardiac trouble. Huchard points out that it has long been known that the use of tea produces functional disorders of the heart of a ,very pronounced character. - He asserts that 'coffee also produces frequently the seine symptoms, with tremor of the limbs, pain in the region of the heart, nausea and profound sweating.—" Good Health."</p>
        <p>How to Live to be Strong. Jess Willard, heavy-weight champion, tells in a recent article how successful boxers must live. "Keep away from cigarettes and keep away from drink," he says. "Chew your food until, your jaws ache. If you had a nice fire going, you wouldn't throw in coal until you smothered it, would you? That's what you do to your digestion when you swallow food with- out chewing it. Fat doesn't come from eating too much. It comes from chewing too little. A hog gets fat because be doesn't use his teeth. Stay out in the open-air as much as you can. Breathe deep. The best training in the world is to fill your lungs and then hold your breath as long as you can. It doesn't take any gym- nasium to do it in, either. And brush your teeth after every 'meal! I couldn't see any</p>
        <p>sense in it when I was a boy, but my mother made me do it. Now I know that seventy-five per cent. of all sickness comes from bad teeth. Keep tobacco and whisky out of your system. chew your food, brush your teeth, breathe deep, and take moderate exercise in the open-air. An boy that does these things will be ready for any sort of hard athletic work when he gets to be eighteen or nineteen." Mr. Willard is against athletics for a boy under eighteen years of age.</p>
        <p>are amongst the greatest that ever lived. The capital of Italy is Rome, which contains many beautiful buildings, one of the -finest being the Cathedral of St. Peter. An in- teresting old building is the ruined Coliseum, where the old Romans used to fight with wild beasts. - Another interesting Italian city is Venice. I would very much like to go to Venice to see the splendid Cathedral of St. Mark, the canals and the gondolas which sail on them, the pictures and. the other works</p>
        <p>of art from which the town is famous.", "Oh, dear," Mona concludes, "what a lot have written." Not a line too much, Mona! Yours is a very nice essay. If I had a second prize to give it would go to Maud Higham (35, Gladstone-street), Adelaide Griffiths com- ing next.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>Yours loving, UNCLE JACK.</head>
        <p>Yours loving, UNCLE JACK.</p>
        <p>NEW MEMBERS.</p>
        <p>Annie Parker, of the L. and N.-W. Railway Institute, Bank Quay, is a dutiful niece, for she has persuaded her sister Ada and her brother Percy to join the Corner, and they are hereby enrolled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>WINTER.</head>
        <p>WINTER.</p>
        <p>A dear little robin once sat on .a tree (The snow lay quite thick on the track); And the sweet little pet was as bright as coultl. be: "I must just make the best of the weather," thought he, " For I know that sweet Spring will come back.</p>
        <p>"I am hungry, of course., and would like a • few crumbs; Oh, wouldn't I think them so good! But I know very well when the fair maiden - looks, Who sits at the window and works at her books, She will come and bring morsels of food."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>LOVING THEM.</head>
        <p>LOVING THEM.</p>
        <p>Here is a pretty little story of a poor child's gentle, loving nature. In front of a shop stood a perambulator, in which a baby was sleeplul; with a tiny puppy, whose nose was nestling comfortably, close to the baby's cheek. By the perambulator a little ragged girl was standing, looking terribly thin and ill-kept, but very happy. She was stroking the baby an the puppy gently and tenderly. " Are you taking care of them?" askcd passer-by, kindly. No, ma'am, I am only loving them," the little girl answered, with a beautiful smile. Only loving them! The child sets all cf us a fine example. If we all of us showed the same gentle love to olhers, there would be a great deal less quarrelling and f.e:fisbness 'among us,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>Otijitrrh fititts.</head>
        <p>Otijitrrh fititts.</p>
        <p>"BEARING BURDENS."</p>
        <p>The • Christians' Duty Towards</p>
        <p>Fallen.</p>
        <p>• The Rev. A. J. Morton, of Padgate, was the special preacher at the Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church on Sunday, and in the evening he dis- coursed to a fair-sized congregation on the sub- ject of "Bearing burdens."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>Helping Hanek</head>
        <p>The Helping Hanek</p>
        <p>The preacher found his text in the familiar Words "Bear ye one another's burdens and so fulfil the law of Christ," and in the course of his remarks he said that the stumbling brother must not be pushed to his fall and the fallen brother must not be left, as it were, by the road- side, an object of reproach and shame, for all to gaze upon, but must be helped to his feet. The old law said "Thou shalt not —!" That was the negative. The new law was a glorious positive, and it said "Thou shalt bear —!" The man who said that he loved God and bated his brother man .was a big contradiction. No matter what colour his skin, no matter what kind of sin he had committed, no matter what might he his present state; if a man was in need of succour that need was his certificate of right to their help, if they were Christians. Airs of superiority were out of place in that Kingdom, the Head of which stooped to the lowest depths of humility. When a man vaunted his holiness, he had lost his holiness; they always found the depths of humility in the truly holy man. The Christian Cannot be Indifferent.</p>
        <p>The feeling of indifference to one's fellow men and their fate was absolutely impossible to a man who had the Spirit of Christ within his soul; the feeling of contempt for the fallen would be the cause of the so-called Christian's worst overthrow. Truly had the poet said,"Man's inhumanity to man. makes countless thousands mourn." Whenever they felt ready to con- demn, let them try to put themselves in the place of him upon whom they were about to pass judgment; let them consider how they would have fared had they been beset by the same temptations and surrounded by the same set of circumstances. So often was it the case that if they knew all they would forgive, ! In his (the preacher's) classification of God's attributes he always put His mercy second to His omniscience. The preacher went on to criticise the censorious people so often to be found in the churches, and went on to appeal for the cultivation of true Christian spirit of forgiveness and brotherhood, observing that the true Christians would always have to be, as it were, crutches to some one or other—someone weak in his, or her, moral or spiritual life.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>WORD TO SLACKERS.</head>
        <p>A WORD TO SLACKERS.</p>
        <p>In. the morning the Rev. A. J. Morton preached on the subject of "Slackers." In the present crisis, he said, the word "slacker," or " shirker " had a meaning it had never had before. If a man was a slacker out and out, the world was very severe with him. At the present time any young man with no physical or home impediment, who refused to stand his corner, had well Darned the strong things which he said of him, and he was to be condemned who held back and lay in the lap of luxury while other° were fighting for their mothers, sisters and little ones. He had 210 wish to hurt any father's or mother's heart, but he was surprised when he heard .of some parents having three or more sons still at home —in one case six sons—and not a soldier among them!</p>
        <p>Throughout the day special music and anthems were rendered by the choir, Mrs. C. Winstanley being the soloist. The collections were in aid of the Society Funds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>LATCHFORD WESLEYANS</head>
        <p>LATCHFORD WESLEYANS</p>
        <p>Hold Successful Missionary</p>
        <p>Services.</p>
        <p>Special missionary services were held at the Wesleyan Church, Latchford, on Sunday. Ser- mons were preached morning and evening by the Rev. T. H. Bailey, of Antigua, who also conducted a children's service in the afternoon. Special music was rendered throughout the day by a number of children. Features of the even- ing service were violin solos played by Mr. Bailey. A public meeting was held in the church on Monday night, a large audience being present. Mr. C. E. Parker, of Penketh, was in the chair, and also on the platform were the Rev. A. J. Morton and the Rev. T. IT. Bailey. In an eloquent address on his experiences as a missionary, Mr. Bailey spoke of many interest- ing and exciting occurrences whilst in Antigua. Missionary hymns were sung by a number of school children in a very pleasing manner, their singing reflecting great credit upon their trainer, Mrs. Smith. During the evening, Mrs. Wheatley, the secretary of the local Wesleyan missionary fund, announced that the total income for the year for this district, including Sunday's and Monday's collections, was £63 10s. 2d., an increase of about £9 on last year. She also announced that the prize of the Juvenile Association had been won by Master Edward Twiss. Miss Hartley presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>"WATCH, WORK, AND WAIT."</head>
        <p>"WATCH, WORK, AND WAIT."</p>
        <p>Vicar Of St. Ann's Advent Message.</p>
        <p>In the current issue of the St. Ann's Parish Magazine, the Vicar (the Rev. Jas. Davies) refers • to, the approach of the season of Advent, and says : " The first Sunday in Advent (28th November) begins the Church's year. Through special Lessons, Collects, Epistles and Gospels, together with suitable hymns, we are reminded of the past, presence, and future of God's king- dom here on earth. From the past we muFt draw lessons for guidance, both of warning and- imitation—lessons to inspire and to invigorate. The present is;the time, of action—with pre- liminary and -continuous equipment. Without .the necessary equipment, drill, munitions, pro- visions and plans, our forces could not with- stand, much less conquer, the enemy; neither can we, subjects of Christ's Kingdom, with- stand and overcome the world, the flesh and the devil, without befitting equipment. See to it that the Advent warnings—watch, work and patiently wait—be heeded, that every means of grace are fully used, and that there be no lack of action, or of definite service, as soldiers under the Lord's banner. From the future we must also draw inspiration and hope. We must ad- vance as to our knowledge of things to be."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
        <head>ST. MARY'S TEA PARTY.</head>
        <p>ST. MARY'S TEA PARTY.</p>
        <p>The annual tea party held under the auspices of St. Mary's Roman Catholic Church took place this year on Monday evening. The venue of the gathering, which was a pronounced success, was the new Parish Hall in Dial-street, which was used for the first time for such an event, and proved to be admirably adapted to functions of the ,kind. The tea tables were beautifully deco- rated, and were well laden with good things, to which the large company did complete justice.</p>
        <p>_ Tea over, au i hour or two were given to whist, after which dancing took place, commencing at</p>
        <p>Continued in next column.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0225" n="art0225">
        <head>MAYOR'S PLEASANT TASK</head>
        <p>MAYOR'S PLEASANT TASK</p>
        <p>Opens Last Day Of Parish</p>
        <p>Church Sate Of Work.</p>
        <p>REQUIRED-AND RAISED.</p>
        <p>The proceedings on the third and last day— Saturday—of the highly successful sale of work in aid of various funds connected with the Warrington Parish Church, and the auxiliary churches of St. Clement's and St. George's, were opened by the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) who was accompanied by the Mayoress.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>£l7O IN TWO DAYS.</head>
        <p>£l7O IN TWO DAYS.</p>
        <p>The opening ceremony was presided over by the Rector (Canon Willis), who said that, on the first two days of the sale, they made .2170, which, he thought, was very good. Of that sum £lOO was realised on the first day (Thursday) and £7O on the second day. It was for the Church Council to allocate that money, but if he might make a suggestion it would be to the effect that £lOO 'vent to the Church Repair Fund, £5O to the repayment of a loan from the Fairfield Fund for the Church Institute, and £2O, which was the proportion they agreed to pay, to the day schools for their use as Sunday schools. The Treasurer of the day schools was willing to forego the £2O this year, but he (the Rector) would be very loth to with- hold the contribution, because they never knew what needs might arise during the next twelve months, and because they could not count on subscriptions for the day schools as they could in the past. In-addition to the £l7O already raised he would like very much to get another £lOO that day, and the manner in which he suggested that such sum, if raised, should be utilised would be to give £4O to the workers to enable them to get materials for working up for next year's sale, £lO for the expenses, and £5O for the two Mission Churches. A very splen- did work had been, and was being, done by the St. Clement's and St. George's Churches. They were churches Which needed and deserved every encouragement, and be hoped the Church Coun- cil would take that fact into consideration. He could not sufficiently thank the workers and the church officers for the kind interest they had taken in the sale of work; he hoped that they would feel that they were rewarded by the approval of their consciences. The Mayor, he was delighted to say, would come to the Parish Church next Sunday, and he would come in a double capacity—as Chief Magistrate of the borough and also in a semi-religious capacity; because, as they all knew, the Church and the State in this country were closely con- nected. He could understand that a Mayor who happened to be a Roman Catholic or a Nonconformist might feel some scruples about the appointment of his chaplain and about coming to the Parish Church. But, if a Mayor considered the matter carefully, he need have no scruples, because he came as the representa- tive of the State, which was connected with the Church. But not only would the Mayor attend the Parish Church as the Chief Magistrate of the borough; he would as their personal friend. (Hear, hear.) The Mayor and the Mayoress took a great interest in all the good works going on in Warrington. (Hear, bear.) In conclu- sion the Canon announced that the Mayoress had presented him with a cheque for to- wards the funds, an announcement which vas received with loud applause.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>MAYOR'S TRIBUTE TO RECTOR.</head>
        <p>MAYOR'S TRIBUTE TO RECTOR.</p>
        <p>The Mayor then declared the sale open, re- marking that he did so out of sincere gratitude for the very many services which Canon Willis had rendered to him during his Mayoralty. Whenever he had required the assistance of the Rector, in his capacity of Mayor's chaplain, that assistance had always been most readily forthcoming—most gladly given. (Hear, hear.) He valued the Rector very highly. Canon Willie could give lessons to the young men of War- rington in many ways. Considering the services he had given to his country it was marvellous how he got through such an enormous amount of work as be did, particularly since the war broke out. He had noticed the Canon's activi- ties in many directions, and if only the young People of Warrington would copy his example the :town would be a better one to live in and the country would also be the better. . (Hear, hear.) During the present terrible time the help of the Church was required more and more; the losses that some suffered, the sadness of heart which so many felt must knit them all closer together in that Christian fellowship which could be a real source of help and com- fort in time of trouble. Personally a Noncon- formist, the Mayor went on, he was asking the Rector .to honour him by acting as his chaplain for another twelve months. He (the- Mayor) had not time in his life for any narrow-minded- ness, and, moreover, he 'realised that, so long as he was entitled to wear the Mayoral chain of office, he was the representative of the State, and that the. Church that was repreentative of the State Church was the Parish Church, and consequently he proposed as usual to attend that church on Sunday next. (Applause.) He thought the Mayor, whoever he might be, ought to attend the Parish Church on Mayor's Sun- day. (Hear, hear.) THANKING HIS WORSHIP.</p>
        <p>Mr. Whittle proposed a vote of thanks to the Mayor, remarking that from his personal know- ledge of Alderman Peter Peacock the town Should be proud to have him as its Mayor. Alder- man Peacock had taken a great interest in Friendly Society work, and it would be easy to see next Sunday how that interest was appre- ciated by noting the number of Friendly Society men who would accompany him to church. Mr. Knott seconded and the proposition hav- ing been carried, the Mayor briefly expressed his thanks, after which the business of the sale of work was proceeded with, there being a goodly company of buyers. • By inadvertence the names of the ladies in charge of the refreshment department were omitted from our last week's report. The ladies were: Mesdames T. J. Hopkins and Bailey, and the Misses White, Woods, Collin, Isherwood, hording, Bottomley,*Locker, Brimelow, Colley, Brayshaw and Cooper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>PROCEEDS.</head>
        <p>THE PROCEEDS.</p>
        <p>Altogether about £240 was realised in the three days—a most gratifying total, all things con- sidered. The proceeds for the respective days were :—Thursday, £lO6 Os. 10d.; Friday, £7O 19s. 7d., and Saturday, £62 16s. 7d. To be precise, the total was just three shillings short of the figure mentioned at the beginning of this para- graph.</p>
        <p>CHURCH NEWS.—Continued.</p>
        <p>8 o'clock and being kept up until 1.30 a.m., the band of the League of the Cross supplying the music, under the conductorship of Mr. Man- Ilion.. The whist prizes were wen by Mrs. T. Dolan and Mr. J. Tracey. Those present included the Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. Peter Peacock),. who were accorded a most cordial reception, and who entered with a zest into the evening's -festivities. His Worship and the Mayoress were formally welcomed by the new Rector of St. Mary's (the Rev. T. Hind, 0.5.8.). The M.C.'s for the dancing were Messrs. 11. Houghton, Magrath and J. Eckersley, and , Messrs. 'r. and J. Dolan had charge of the ar- • rangements for the whist drive. Mr. W. Dil- worth and the ladies of the committee, had the management of the refreshment department, and the following members of the congregation assisted: Mesdames S. Massion, Caldwell and A. Whelan, and Messrs. H. Houghton, W. Chal- loner, T. Ridgway, A. Beswick, L. 'Cross. W. •Mannion, E. Nicholls, J. Duffey, W. Austin and W. Lawless. Mr. A. Kilburn acted as secretary.</p>
