      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>A Common Noun. Mention of Mr. Jones, of Wales," in one</p>
        <p>of last week's letters to the Editor reminded me of another experience. A University man, Smith by name, attended a great conference in Ireland at which representatives of all parts of the kingdom were present. He rose to take part in debate, and, in response to cries of " Name," promptly responded " Smith, of Halifax." This provoked shouts of derisive laughter at the moment, but it was destined to be of permanent service • in the owner's native lown, as elsewhere, te eminently re- spectable name in question is by no means un- common, and ever thereafter he was distin- guished as " Smith of Halifax." That was what everybody called him; indeed, although he was quite a " character " in the district, I never heard his Christian name, and do not know it to this day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
        <head>Vicars I Have Known.</head>
        <p>Vicars I Have Known.</p>
        <p>The present Vicar of Halifax addressed a foreign missionary meeting here last week. I knew three of Prebendary Burns' predeces- sors in that office. The last of the three was the late Ven. Archdeacon Brooke, who before going to Halifax was vicar of 31irfield, where he was in great favour with the colliers He was a very humorous man, and enjoyed a joke at his own expense—and they were often made about his gaiters. He lost a son—a splendid fellow—in the Boer War. Prior to that, Canon Pigou was vicar of Halifax for some time. He accepted appointment while the parish was in the throes of agitation against a most obnoxious old vicar's rate, and the scandal became so great that wealthy Churchpeopk raised a fund ensurtfig an equivalent from private sources so that it was abolished—but not until passive resisters' goods had been repeatedly seized and sold, and Nonconformist parishioners had stormed and packed the Easter vestry meeting, elected Dissenting officials, and practically brought the machinery of the church to a standstill. It was from Halifax that Canon Pigou wont to be Dean of Bristol, where he had leisure to compile entertaining reminiscences, which were well received on publication. The other Vicar of Halifax I recall was the Yen. Arch- deacon Musgrave, and I remember little about him beyond the fact that he was a prodigious snuff-taker. Glad When He Had Finished.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0232" n="art0232">
        <p>• At. the gathering in connection with the dedication festival of St. John's Presbyterian Church, the Rev. John Yonge told how years ago he preached in the old church to 25 people, and how glad be was when he had fin- ished the sermon. I could not help wonder- ing (and the reverend gentleman will not mind my habit of thinking aloud) if the feeling so candidly avowed was reciprocated—if hearers likewise rejoiced greatly when the discourse came to an end. A Sunday school teacher took for a lesson the incident in the Acts of the Apostles where Philip accepted a "lift" in the chariot of the Ethiopian eunuch and expounded to that dusky student a certain chapter in Isaiah. At the close of the lesson she proceeded: "Now, children, can any of you tell me why the eunuch rejoiced ?" And one little fellow feelingly replied, "'Cos Philip had done teachin"im !"—or, in Mr. Yonge's phrase, had "finished the sermon." As a youngster I spent some, time with an uncle on a farm on the Mar- quess of Ripon's estate, and on Sunday afternoons attended church ser- vice at Studley Royal. That was, of course, before the late Marquess became a Roman Catholic. The congregation never numbered, more than 25 all told; still, one could hardly add that either the vicar or anybody else had special reason to be "glad when he had fin- ished the sermon," as the homily never ex- ceeded seven minutes in delivery. All the same, though no doubt the fault or misfor- tune was mine, I cannot say that I ever under- stood what it was about. It was very soon over, anyhow, so that we were not bored. It is remarkable, too, that through the inter- vening half-century or so I have not forgotten a sermon I heard one Sunday evening at Ripon Cathedral, the text being "Unstable as water, thou shalt not excel."</p>
        <p>Only! At last week's meeting of the Board of Guardians the Rev. A. M. Mitchell observed what a splendid thing it would be if they could get the Government to set the example in a certain direction " instead of Preaching." For the regular occupant of a pulpit to employ just that phrase in such connection struck me as—nay, perish the thought! " No, but, pa," urged the little daughter of a minister on being told of a marvellous occurrence, "is it really true, or is it only preaching ?" Somehow, irrelevant or otherwise, the refer- ence brought this to mind. " Postage Costs Us More."</p>
        <p>Ought it not to be less in at least one par- ticular? Naturally there is much grumbling at the postal rates charged on parcels for- warded by relatives to our boys on active ser- vice. For instance, one may simply send a writing pad costing not more than 6d. (I have myself done this) and the postage actually comes to more than the contents of the parcel, and so with many other things. Perhaps it is necessary; one can understand that if the expenses of carriage were less, the parcels would have a tendency to increase in bulk, and thus might seriously embarrass an already overtaxed transport service. It is, of course, of extreme importance that the comforts sent out should reach the hands of the eagerly- expectant men for whom they are intended, therefore extreme care should be taken in packing and addressing packets. Theological Students and the War.</p>
        <p>All must have remarked with admiration upon the readiness and promptitude with which young men associated with the Sunday schools rallied to the colours on the outbreak of the war; and it would be easy to show that the same spirit animates the students in the theological colleges. Take, for example, that of the United Methodist Church at Manches- ter, where wellnigh to a man the young fellows have volunteered for service in the R.A.M.C., in which corps the Primitive Methodist and other churches already had considerable numbers, while not a few had joined the ranks. Students fulfilling occa- sional appointments have shown how im- patient they were to be released from their ordinary work in order that they might take their places by the side of our soldiers on active duty. I recently met a cousin of Tom Mann who is preparing for the U.M.C. min- istry, and he declared be would much have preferred enlistment in the infantry and did his utmost to achieve his aim, but, like all his fellow-students at Manchester College, he was accepted only for the R.A.M.C.</p>
        <p>" My Favourite Bird." What I read- of this competition in the " Children's Corner " strongly tempts me to enlarge on the " tricks and manners " of a really clever canary which was a highly-prized pet in my family for more than twelve years, and I should have pleasure in entering upon details were it not for an uneasy feeling that I have told some of the stories before. Under</p>
        <p>Continued in next column.</p>
