      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>CONSTANTINOPLE.</head>
        <p>CONSTANTINOPLE.</p>
        <p>The Religious History Of An Ancient City.</p>
        <p>THE STORY OF SAINT CHRYSOSTOM.</p>
        <p>The Rev. G. Parkin paid a visit to the Primi- tive Methodist Church, Legh-street, . Warring- ton, last week. In .the afternoon he con. ducted a service, preaching the sermon. At night he delivered an excellent lecture on the "Religious History of Constantinople." Mr, J. T. Rowland was in the chair. Previous to the address, prayers were said by the Rev. A. J. Chessum, and a solo was rendered by Miss Pettitt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <head>ON BORDER OF TWO CONTINENTS.</head>
        <p>ON BORDER OF TWO CONTINENTS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Parkin said that no doubt his audience knew that Constantinople was on the verge of two continents; Europe on one hand and Asia on the other. It was easy to see that a city so situated possessed uncommon advantages. Com- mercial men could see its advantages from their point of view, and military men could see that a city of that kind had a stragetical value. For both these reasons, different nations had long wanted it. Russia wanted it in order that she might deliver her corn harvests to the southern cities and countries of Europe. Germany wanted it to help her carry out her great scheme of conquering the world; a scheme that he, for one, hoped would never be realised. If Ger- many could not get it herself she wanted that city to be in the possession of a nation that would recognise her supremacy, and that was the reason why she wanted it to be in the possession of Bulgaria. Whilst so many were looking on the city and thinking of its- com- mercial advantages and its military advan- tages, he would ask his hearers to look at its religious history. Home Of Two Religions.</p>
        <p>Two of the universal religions had had their homes in that city. By " universal " religion he meant a religion that was not confined to one land or to one race, but which took a hold on the different countries and embraced men of all nations. These were Buddhism, Moham- medism and Christianity; he mentioned them in the order of time. Constantinople had been the home of Christianity and Mohammedism. Mr. Parkin went on to say that the founder of Constantinople was Constantine, who was born in 274 and died in 337. Constantine had a fine appearance, and was a bravo man. The Roman soldiers decided to make him Emperor, and as ;there 'were many aspirants for the Governorship, civil war resulted and many battles were fought. One of the battles took place near Rome, and it was said that on the night before the battle Constantine had a dream or vision, and saw the Sign of the Cross, and on the Cross was the inscription in Latin " In hoc signo vinces " (" In this sign you shall con- quer.") Constantine won the battle and the Sign of the Cross was adopted as a Roman standard.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>Constantine's New Capital.</head>
        <p>Constantine's New Capital.</p>
        <p>When Constantine became supreme, he de- cided to build a new capital, and with the eyt, of a soldier fixed on the site of Constantinople. A Greek pirate had made this site his hiding place, and 'already there had grown up a city by the name of Byzantium. When Constantine had built his new city the next thing that he did was to invite the leading men of Rome to come and abide there. The aristocracy of Rome was a proud aristocracy, but many of them were very pleased to go and live with Constantine in his new city. Constantine wanted the new city to be called New Rome, but the people called it Constantinople as a compliment to the Emperor. When the city was built it had - palaces and 14 churches. One of the churches was called St. Sophia. When Constantine gave it this name he was not thinking of a good woman; Sophia was the Greek word for "eternal wisdom." • -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <p>To illustrate the growth of the early Church Mr. Parkin mentioned as an instance the fact that at the Council at Nicea in 325, three hundred and eighteen bishops were present. Constantine was present at this Council. For the Church of St, Sophia, Constantine wanted the hest preachers that could be secured for it. One of those preachers was John Chrysostom— John with the "golden mouth." John was an eloquent and courageous man. When he saw wrong he condemned it. He was also a very abstemious man; a man of great self-denial. But he had enemiel, and it was alleged that he had preached against the Empress. He was sentenced to banishment and the great preacher had to leave his church. After he had gone there was great excitement in the city, and just then there came an earthquake and the beautiful church suffered. The men said : "There will be no peace until John is re- called," so within two days John received orders to return. He came back, but soon he was banished again, lle died in exile. Thirty years after, at the request of the people, his remains were brought back to Constantinople.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <head>Church That Cost A Million.</head>
        <p>Church That Cost A Million.</p>
        <p>Referring to Constantinople, under Justinian, the speaker said that the Church of St Sophia had twice been destroyed, once by fire and once by earthquake. Justinian rebuilt the church and it was said to have cost over a million of money. Mr. Parkin described the splendour of the church and stated that from that day the church had still lived on. Mr. Parkin also gave a short history of the life of Mahomet and the principles of Mohammedism. Constan- tinople, he said, came under the influence of Mohammedism in 1453; in 1452 it was attacked by Mahomet 11. When he took that city it had for centuries been one of the Royal cities of the world. Men of learning had assembled there, and with the.approach of Mahomet they fled to Europe, taking with them their manu- scripts. At that time printing was invented, and the outcome of these two facts was the Revival of Learning. That was one of the blessings that resulted from the fall of Coq- stantinople. • Mr. Parkin concluded his lecture with the words: "God is in His Heaven; all is right in the world."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
        <head>Information Wanted.</head>
        <p>Information Wanted.</p>
        <p>"Which is correct," asked the summer boarder who wished to air his knowledge, "to speak of a sitting hen or a setting hen?" " I don't know," replied the farmer's wife, "and what's more, I don't care. But there's one thing I would like to know: when a hen cackles, has she been laying, or is she lying?"</p>
        <p>WHY THE UPPER STOREYS OVERHUNG. The habit of building projecting storeys was not dictated by any striving after effect or beauty.. Th overhang of the building gave shelter from sun and rain, and was found con- venient by those who had goods to sell. Throughout Europe the town buildings were designed in this way, as may be discovered from a study of the old buildings which re- main. The, shopkeeper did not devote any space. inside his building to business---space usually being too limited for that. His wares were carried out and displayed upon a stall or in a booth. Where the streets were nar- row business people were allowed only .a cer- tain number of feet or inches for their stalls, and old bylaws throw some curious sidelights upon the business habits of our ancestors. In London the stalls are shown to have been hinged to th 6 buildings and let down when required. They were allowed at one period to be only thirty inches in width. The shop- keepers' signs often appear in old bylaws as subjects of regulation. In London a mini- mum height of nine feet was insisted upon, in order that horsemen might not be endangered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0239" n="art0239">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.— continued.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.— continued.</p>
        <p>constraint of that conviction I forbear; but I will venture to repeat one about a parrot. In a visit to the Zoological Gardens, London, nearly 40 years ago, I positively heard a bored bird in the parrot-house administer a neat snub. A man who was sauntering through halted before its perch, and this short " con- versation " ensued: " Good morning, Polly." "Goo' mornin'." "Morning, Polly." " Mornin'." "Morning, Polly." "Oh, go 'way, go 'way !" My canary could not talk, but I taught it to whistle part of a hymn tune, and it came out to meals and could find its way from below to my bedroom, and kiss and shake " hands "—but there " In the Garden."</p>
        <p>The compiler of the notes under this bead- ing recently remarked that carnations and picotees are not cultivated half as much as they should be. I have known sonic who have done well by specialising in these and other plants. I well remember a Mr. Tiles. Lord who became quite famous as a grower of car- nations and picotees, often carrying all before him at the shows. Having achieved wide and enviable distinction in this line, he gave it all up. He is still alive and well, however; met him quite lately. I know another who has had great success with orchids, his name being incorporated with that of one rare pro- duction ; and he is not the only one of my acquaintance who enjoys that distinction. I know another who many years ago set to work to show what can be dono with the cactus, and he has conic to be called the " cactus king." His place is at Sowerby, in the West Riding of Yorkshire (there are other villages of that name); the others flourished just over the border in Lancashire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 13, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 13, 1915.</p>
        <p>THE "OLD FOX."</p>
        <p>Curiosities Of Shops And Shop-keeping.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0241" n="art0241">
        <head>DATE 1110 ON THE "OLD FOX."</head>
        <p>THE DATE 1110 ON THE "OLD FOX."</p>
        <p>BY ARTHUR MOUNFIELD.</p>
        <p>Our unidentified friend of A.D. 1575 whose I figures and costs have already been quoted,' I entered a few other items which may be found instructive.</p>
        <p>Having recorded the cost of his roof he gave next the payment made to plasterers and " dobers." The plasterer probably put in the wattle work and finished the wall surfaces in- side and outside. The " dober," or dauber, was the colour washer who went over the plasterers' work and gave it a finish of lime or • coloured wash. It has been usual from very early times to cover English houses with lime, and this not entirely from ideas orelean- liness, but to render them less liable to fire. The ancient bylaws of London of the 13th cen- tury insist that all bakeries breweries, cook shops, or other buildings where fire is used, shall be limewashed within and without as a precaution, and that every dwelling-house covered with reeds or rushes be plastered wherever possible. It was usual to place moss as the roofing of country cottages, secured at the eaves by a strong board. Such a roof absorbed the rain, and needed no gutter, and as the moss would continue to sprout, the appearance of the cot- tages must have been pretty indeed. The plasterer would probably complete the floors as well as the walls, for these also were of clay or wattle. Bedroom floors consisted often of oak strips placed across joists and finished above and below with a mixture of clay, heather and cow dung. The inner walls were finished in colour. An examination of old walls shows them to have been covered with coats of blue or yellow. The yellow is yellow-ochre, a substance which has been used as a. colour wash since the days of the Romans. The .blue is usually an ugly blue, still familiar in remote corners of the land, made from the plant liverwort.</p>
        <p>order were frequently passed during the 18th century. First, they were forbidden to carry the short swords which were in common use at the period. Next- they were forbidden to have knives upon their person. Then there were laws which made it a crime to cry " Clubs " in the street, since that was the signal for a free fight among the apprentices. Then it was made illegal to grasp or lay hands on any person in the street. It was not for a long time that the outside shouter was abol- ished, but eventually shop window dressing took his place and a new era began.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0242" n="art0242">
        <head>COMING OF THE GARRET.</head>
        <p>THE COMING OF THE GARRET.</p>
        <p>The storeys of old hou,ses were rarely made more than eight feet in height. In an ordin- ary dwelling the beams allowed a man of aver- age height to walk about without danger, but placed in peril those who exceeded the aver- age. Until the end of the thirteenth century it is doubtful whether even the largest cities could have shown a house of more than, two storeys in height. But with the fourteenth century comes the mention of the garret, which in its original meaning .was a look-out. A tiny chamber placed above the bedroom, With a small window near the apex of the roof, afforded a point of vantage. The advantage of an extra chamber, however small,. was quickly recognised. It made a dry and very suitable store for grain, potatoes and other foods, and through the next three cen- turies the garret became a permanent feature of the English house. The "Old Fox " shows a first-rate example of the grain store. The openings in each gable have been shut- tered, and not glazed, and it would be ap- proached by a ladder.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0243" n="art0243">
        <head>CRAFT HAUNTS.</head>
        <p>CRAFT HAUNTS.</p>
        <p>Our own Buttermarket and Horsemarket are reminders that in the old towns industries were associated with a special area. It was so with the crafts. The ropers, salters, iron- workers, money-lenders (no one will object to our including them among the crafts men), or goldsmiths could each be found in a given street. This makes street nomenclature very interesting in some of the largest of our old cities; London has its Jewry, or Jews' quar- ter, where money-lending was the chief busi- ness. Needlers'-lane was the place of the needle makers, Paternoster-row was the haunt of the paternoster makers, and stood conveni- ently near to old St. Paul's. There is also Stockfishmongers'-row and Carriers'-row. Many old towns have a Salter-row, a Roper- gate, a Knifesmith, gate, and Chesterfield has a Glumangate. The first mentioned row was the habitation of the meat-salters, and the last indicates the abode of gleemen or min- strels. These singers were quite a fixture a few centuries ago, and were employed by the municipalities.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0244" n="art0244">
        <head>THOUGHTFUL FOR THE TRAMPS.</head>
        <p>THOUGHTFUL FOR THE TRAMPS.</p>
        <p>A few interesting facts about old world shops may be gathered from legislation which followed the great fire of London and the re- building of the city under Sir Christopher Wren. Previous to the fire the shopkeepers had pushed out their benches half-way across the footpath, but following the fire in 1666 an Act of Parliament decreed that their shelf must be only eleven inches wide and hinged to the wall. In the next year, 1667, a law was made requiring all shopkeepers to build over- hanging roofs extending at least one-half way over the footpath, for the protection of pedes- trians when it rained. .</p>
        <p>In 16/0 another law—very curious to the modern mind—gave permission to all poor per- sons to sleep on the boards of the shops under the overhanging roof. The bed was not in- viting, but at least it was dry. It may be conjectured that some experience and skill would be required to sleep successfully upon an eleven-inch board, and there were those who felt they had a grievance. An agitation was made, and after some eloquence had been lavished in Parliament, the law was altered, and boards of twenty inches were ordered. In the contented snores of the beggars, legis- lators and agitators would have their sufficient reward.</p>
        <p>TROUBLESOME FOR THE CUSTOMER. It must be recorded that the practice of the grouping of shops of the same trade did not prove an entirely satisfactory arrangement. It was convenient for the purchaser of jewellery to have all the goldsmiths' shops together; he could see all that the city afforded within the compass of a fewyards of space. ButpeoPle of a trade do not always agree. The usage developed of employing apprentice boys to stand outside and yell " What d'ye lath ?" These zealous youths, paid to push business, would pounces on the innocent onlooker and drag him into the shop by main force. Should the unwary buyer have glanced at two rival shops he placed himself in danger. The ap- prentice of each would claim him as lawful prey. Clubs and even knives would be drawn, and the rivals would bring matters to a pain- ful issue. And sometimes it- was the cus- tomer who got hurt.. Regulations to .keep the apprentices in</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0245" n="art0245">
        <head>ANSWERS TO CORRESPONDENTS.</head>
        <p>ANSWERS TO CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>A kindly correspondent who has closely fol- lowed our articles asks whether or not it is true that the old builders managcd to build walls of mud, and if so, how this unpromising material could be made staple and strong. The answer is that our forbears succeeded just as the house marten does, and by exactly the same method. The bird manages to make a very satisfactory house by the process of adding a little mud at a time to the walls and allowing that little to dry very thoroughly. The mud wall of a dwelling may be a very staple article if the builder will have patience and allow the layers to dry. Those who have traversed the byways of the Isle of Man must have noticed many examples of old mud walls, and a building material that can hold its own in that little wind-swept island is not to be despised.</p>
        <p>THE DATE A.D. 1110 on the " OLD FOX." We are reminded that " Old Fox " has a date cut on one of its timbers, which would make it much older than the date we have assigned. On the south-east corner post is carved 1110, with crossed flags above and a cross beneath. .</p>
        <p>Not many people, we think, will suppose that the building has been standing through eight centuries, and some other explanation will be sought of the figures on the post. The reminder may first be given that if a date had been inscribed in -A.D 1110 it would not have been cut in Arabic numerals, seeing that these had not come into common use at that period. The figures are of a form that was used in the 18th century, and not the 12th.</p>
        <p>A possible explanation is that the building has borne • the date 1550 carved on a lintel. The figure 5 as inscribed in the 16th century was a slightly curved and tailed off stroke. The figure t was tailed in a similar way, and each could be easily mistaken for the other. There are many cases of errors of this kind. Daresbury Church, to quote a local instance, bears a date stone on the south side of the tower with the figures 1110. During the restoration of the building a date stone of 1550 has been recut, and the workman, quite honestly, has read it 1110. Those who have had any experience of the figure 5 of Eliza- bethan days when found on weathered stone or wood will not need to be told that the error would be very easy for plain folks to make.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0246" n="art0246">
        <head>DEEDS RELATING TO THE "FOX ALEHOUSE."</head>
        <p>DEEDS RELATING TO THE "FOX ALEHOUSE."</p>
        <p>The Town Clerk, Mr. J. Lyon Whittle, has very kindly supplied some information con- cerning the " Old Fox " in the 18th century and clauses from a deed of 1793. These we append : Extraet from a " Deed of Gift," dated 19th November, 1793, .made " between Alice Skitt, widow, of the one pat, and Thomas Skitt, malster, only ,son and heir apparent of the said Alice Skitt, of the other part " : ALL that Messuage and Tenement with</p>
        <p>the Appurtenances situate standing and being in the old Buttermarket-street within Warrington aforesaid commonly called or</p>
        <p>known by tho name of the Fox Alehouse formerly the Inheritance of William Duckon-</p>
        <p>field Esquire afterwards the Inheritance of the Reverend John Sutton, Clerk since the Inheritance of John Sutton Esquire who sold and conveyed the same to George Joyn- son late Father of the said Alice Skitt who in and by his Will gave and devised the same unto his Daughter the said Alice Skitt and now the Inheritance of the said Alice Skitt and in the possession of her her assigns or undertenants.</p>
        <p>A clause follows relating to pews in the Parish and Trinity Churches. These have no special relation to the Fox, except that they belonged to the owner of it. AND ALSO all that seat or pew situate</p>
        <p>and being in a certain piece of Building here-</p>
        <p>tofore erected on the South Side of the Parish Church of Warrington aforesaid by Virtue of a certain Faculty granted unto Giles Fairclough and Thomas Patten by the then Bishotrof Chester being marked Num- ber (18) And by the said Giles Fairclough _ _</p>
        <p>and Thomas Paden sold unto Peter Platt of Warrington aforesaid Malster afterwards</p>
        <p>the property of one Francis Swinson upon</p>
        <p>whose decease the same descended unto his Widow and legal Representative Elizabeth</p>
        <p>Swinson who —sold the same to the said George Joynson who by his Will gave the same to his Daughter the said Alice Skitt AND ALSO all that other Seat or pew situ-</p>
        <p>ate and being in the Trinity Chapel within Warrington aforesaid and marked Number (55) heretofore appropriated by the Com- missioners named in a Commission bearing</p>
        <p>date The twenty fourth day of October 1761, from the Right Reverend Father in God Edmund Lord Bishop of Chester to the said George Joynson who by his Will gave the same to his Daughter the said Alice Skitt Subject to the yearly rent of 88. 6d. issuing and payable from the said seat or pew in the Trinity Chapel to the said Commis- sioners and subject as to the said messuage and tenement of the yearly Fee farm or chief rent of £2 108. payable to the Right Honble. George Earl of Warrington.</p>
        <p>By an earlier deed dated 10th June, 1767, the " Fox Alehouse " (by the above descrip- tion) was sold by John Hesketh, of Warring- ton, merchant, to George Joynson, of War- rington, mister (father of Alice Skitt), for the stun of £2OO.</p>
        <p>This is the earliest deed relating to the property which has survived. During the process of demolition two docu- ments which belong to the period of Alice fritt have been discovered. One is a doctor's bill and the other the account of a distiller.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0247" n="art0247">
        <head>SUBMARINE ACTIVITY.</head>
        <p>SUBMARINE ACTIVITY.</p>
        <p>British Transport And Italian Liner</p>
        <p>Attacked.</p>
        <p>The outward-bound British transport Mer- cian has been attacked by an enemy submarine in the Mediterranean, but not sunk. The War Office states that 23 men were killed, 30 missing, and 50 wounded.</p>
        <p>Off the Sardinian coast the Italian liner Ancona, with passengers for New York, has been sunk •by a submarine. It is feared that 300 persons, mostly women and children emi- grants, have been drowned. Accounts show the attack to have been quite ruthless. From Gibraltar it is reported that two Ger- man submarines have been sunk. The British steamers. Clan Macalister, Cali- fornian, and INI-oorina, and the Trinity House yacht Irene are also reported sunk, but by what agency is not stated.</p>
        <p>Parades, drills, marches, cleaning, office routine; so the morning passes. Rain has come. The mattress-lined corridors are thronged with men; the atmospheres thick; the floors covered with dust from shuffling feet. The building hums with undertones. Hither and thither overworked clerks hasten; Army forms and documents fluttering as they pass. Doors bang repeatedly. Bugles are blowing at continuous intervals; and at length summon us to the open- ing dinner-shift. So again We line up, and march past the row of white-capped cooks, each flour- ishing his iron ladle over a- steaming dish, and dealing into our platter a substantial contribu- tion of homely fare as we quickly pass along. A slapping of hands and an exclamation! In a moment all conversation ceases; knives, forks and spoons are dropped; the hall is lost in a hush. Our lieutenant is paying a visit. Suavely he passes up the aisle with a clear</p>
        <p>"Carry on, please; carry on, please." "Food all right?" he asks; but the eagerness with which we have resumed eating at his request is sufficient testimony of its excellence. After- wards the canteen is visited by many whose appetites appear insatiable. Some go there, too, for polish, string, writing paper, or others of a host of oddments.</p>
        <p>A brief respite for digestion, chat, a game of draughts or cards, or letter-writing; and then the crowds break up. Some are free for the afternoon, and on sight-seeing bent will run up to the Metropolis; returning shortly after tea for firepiquet or other duties. Without loss of time the clerks re-enter to their respective depots. Other men congregate around the square to watch the troops—departing for the Front yet and always. There is a cheer, and they are gone. We may not see them again. Still the bugles blow; and we know our turn, too, will come. At Night.</p>
        <p>Tea is partaken amid nightfall. Afterwards ascending the stairs and passing along the corridors we glance through the large old- fashioned windows and see great search- lights sweeping in all directions the boding skies, bathing the Whole place in daylight. The soldiers seek the town, which is busy, though very sober and still. They throng the sumptuous homes, free of access. The shops and theatres; 'too, are busy. There are long walks to be indulged in; historic places close at hand. Along the good old highways (what changes they have seen; yet still they remain un- changed!) a now effect is to be remarked as through the fog green lights shine eerily into just such a night s when Jarvis Lorry coached down to Dover and crossed into the midst of other callous conflict, whence too was born a new era of civilisation.</p>
        <p>Anon we return barrackwards, staying only on the way to enjoy a little supper in one of the busy cafes. " Home " again, across the fog, we are arrested at the entrance by the im- perative tones of the sentry:—</p>
        <p>Who goes there? Stranger, quickly tell! A friend? The word! Pass friend; good-night! All; all's well!</p>
        <p>Along the corridors over all the building mattresses are being spread for another night's rest; hurrying men go up and down adjusting the large shutters in anticipation of more furtive aerial visitors, in the dormitories for a while there is chat and banter; a tale and a song. Then out in the night bugles sound "Lights out," and for a few hours everything is still.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0248" n="art0248">
        <head>DAY IN BARRACKS.</head>
        <p>DAY IN BARRACKS.</p>
        <p>Soldier's Impressions Of English</p>
        <p>Garrison Life.</p>
        <p>A HOME TRAGEDY.</p>
        <p>The following article is by Mr. Sydney Jeffery, one of our reporters who recently joined the Army Ordnance Corps. He is the second member of our literary staff to enlist, the first one being Mr. Frank Kay, whose articles from Gallipoli have appeared in our columns: Mr. Jeffery's article on " Dickens and Peace," which was published a few weeks ago in "The Examiner," has been indexed in the official Dickensiana list; and he has, by request of the Editor, written an article on the same sub- ject for " The Dickensian."</p>
        <p>Reveille sounds at six; but we have risen be- fore that, and rapid eh culations are being induced by vigorous ablutions in almost frozen water. Crowded is the dormitory. It is long and narrow, situated on the fifth floor of largo barracks in. a famous south military centre; and not only, in crowded state, do our rough mat- tresses surround the room, but loose blankets up its centre have had to suffice as beds for recruits who, crumpled and crushed, arrived only late last night—much to the disgust of our hoarse and perspiring sergeant. As yet these newcomers have hardly shaken off their drow- siness, and look round in bewildered dismay at us " headquarters " men as we rummage in our kitbags for razors, strops, soap, towels, toothbrushes, boot and button polish, and a host of things; or sort out a garment or two for the regimental wash. Our toilet completed, we stroll down to the big barracks square in slip- pored dignity, putteeless. At the bottom of the stairs is stationed the newsboy; and in groups of two and three we stroll up and down for some half hour, chatting over the news and enjoying a morning cigarette. The bugles blow for breakfast; and hurrying down to the dining-hall we fall into line until called up in order according to the urgency of our duties or the earliness of our respective 'parades. Some have plenty of time to spare before parade; yet they growl not a little as the cor- poral dismisses them until "second shift " forty-five minutes later. Those of us lucky enough to remain, however, are very soon in the long row of .eight-seating tables, " tucking " into our bacon with a relish—eating, indeed, against time. This morning there is little con- versation at table; for during the night there has taken place a tragedy. In whispered tones one hears how- on an adjoining passage a somnambulist has climbed through a window and fallen past five storeys to be dashed to a painful death on the stones beneath. Not many miles away is his home; and one wonders how the news will be received by his wife and five little ones. Much concern also is expressed at His Majesty's mishap "over the water." In these days everything is done against time at our corps headquarters. The King has been issuing an appeal; Lord Derby, too, has a few words to say. And consequently recruits are steadily. rolling in day after day, in hundreds and tens of hundreds. Though our barracks is large, it is already crowded; and so the new- comers, after reporting at the Receiving Office, are paraded and immediately drafted to out stations scattered over all parts of these Isles. Whither they go they know not; yet they depart cheerily enough—in the Army they are all cheery faces!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0249" n="art0249">
        <head>Orderly Officer.</head>
        <p>The Orderly Officer.</p>
        <p>THE LAST CIGARVITE.</p>
        <p>!To 11.K.R.)</p>
        <p>As I sat thinking and thinking last night, Whilst smoking a " gift " cigarette, I tried to remember the things I'd to do,</p>
        <p>Yet all I could do was—forget.</p>
        <p>But as I sat smoking the "cig" which I smoked, I thought through the smoke I could trace— The hand of the maiden whose fingers had touched The last cigarette in the ease.</p>
        <p>And as I sat thinking and thinking I thought How nice it would be just to get— In touch with the touch of the hand that had touched That dainty and last cigarette.</p>
        <p>R. OSSULSTON-RICHE.</p>
        <p>Chaucer was the old word for shoemaker, and that of Leech for a medical man. Barker is the old name for a tanner, arising from the bark he used in his industry, and Jenner is the•old form of Joiner; Shearman is one who shears worsted, fustians, &amp;c., and Brigman, or Bridgeward, one who keeps a bridge. Ark- might was originally the maker- of meal chests, a wright (as in wheel-wright) who makes arks or chests for the holding of meal. A Walker is a manager of a fulling apparatus. A fulling mill in Scotland is still called a walk-mill. Nappere, from which .we get Napier, was the one who laid the cloth and stood behind his lord to serve him with the small cloth, the napkin, which we have tried to make more respectable by calling serviette. Many surnames are from civil and ecclesias- tical dignities, as King, Duke, Earle, Knight, Bishop, Abbot, &amp;c. High offices have become surnames, such as Steward, converted into Stewart, Stuart; also Chamberlain, Sheriff, Porter, Wardman, &amp;c. Sumner is from the office of summoner, whose w3rk was to cite delinquents to ecclesiastical courts.</p>
        <p>NAMES FROM PERSONAL QUALITIES. To take surnames from conspicuous physi- cal qualities was natural: We have from com- plexion and hair of individuals the surnames Black, White, Reids (red), Brown, Fairfax (fair-haired), Pigot (speckled). From stature we get the Longs and Shorts, Longmans, &amp;c. Surnames from moral and intellectual quali- ties, Wise and Wiseman, Sharpe, Good, Arm- strong, Blythe, Goodenough, and (strange to say) Toogood. Also the French Coour de Loon, the Lion Hearted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0250" n="art0250">
        <head>HOW WE GOT OUR NAMES,</head>
        <p>HOW WE GOT OUR NAMES,</p>
        <p>Fascinating Researches Into Their Origin.</p>
        <p>SURNAAIES.</p>
        <p>BY C. E. PARKER.</p>
        <p>Names help us to re-create the past. They speak to us of the condition in which our fathers lived, the abodes in which they dwelt, the animals they hunted., the occupations they followed, the means by which they got their livelihood, the battles they fought, and the weapons they made and used. We will first confine our attention to surnames, and, later, follow with an article on Christian names and on( on the names of places. The surnames we purpose bringing before our readers cover the whole period of the history of the English people, because every period has done its part in supplying these. Amongst our family names we have early British, Celtic, Saxon, Danish, Norman, French, German, Dutch, and Belgian names. We have names belonging to the Stone Age, and the iron era; to the period of water power and of steam power. I do not think we have any new names from the electrical period; perhaps it is thought we have enough to go on with. Hundreds of years elapsed between the birth of such names as Hill, Cairn, and Dykes, and those of Bow- man and Bannerman, Weaver and Mercer. Many of our surnames are derived from names of common articles.</p>
        <p>The following are mostly of early Saxon origin, and suggest the open-air life of our forefathers and the pursuits they followed Wood, Moore, Gates,. Beck (a stream), Barrow (a tumulus grave of the early Britons), Bent (a place where rushes grow), Bottom (low-lying ground, hence Longbottom, Rams- bottom). Cairn denotes a. heap of stones; Dyke; a ditch or wall; Garnet, a great granary; Grange, the farm building of a mon- astery; Knowles, little hills; Spense, a yard or enclosure; Thorpe, a village. Many of our names have their origin in military pursuits, such as Hook-man, Bill- man, Spear-man, Bow-man, and Banner-man. Several come from field sports, such as Hunter, Fowler, Fisher, Falconer, Faulkner, Bowyer, Stringer, Tod (tod being the old name for fox). We have both names and places connected with the fox: Todhunter, Todcas- ter, Todmorden, Todridge. Brock •means a badger and is used as a surname. I do not know if we have any Brockhunters, but the name Brocklehurst indicates both a badger and a wood. In localities where the badger' was common, such as in Ribblesdale, in North Lancashire, the names of many places have broc in them, such as Brockesholme, Brock- hall, Brockhole, Spotbrok.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0251" n="art0251">
        <head>NAMES FROM OCCUPATIONS.</head>
        <p>NAMES FROM OCCUPATIONS.</p>
        <p>Surnames from occupations are numerous, and suggest the kind of work in which our ancestors engaged. We have Butcher and Baker, Cooper and Carter, Saddler and Skin- ner, Taylor and Tiler, Mason and Mercer, Leech and Lorimer, Brewer, Carpenter, Shep- herd, and Weaver; and last, but not least, we have the family of smiters of iron and all their relations: Smith, Goldsmith, and Arrowsmith.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0252" n="art0252">
        <head>NAMES FROM ANIMALS.</head>
        <p>NAMES FROM ANIMALS.</p>
        <p>The animal world has been ransacked for names. We have Lions and Lambs, Foxes and Goslings, Ducks and Swans, Eagles and Hawks. In fact, all imaginable creatures have given us designations. Such were prob- ably conferred from a fanciful resemblance of the first bearer to the object referred to, and therefore would, in the first place, be given as ieknames. The name of Wedderburn, found mostly in Scotland, denotes a burn or stream, which runs the opposite way to the course of the sun, that is from west to east. Anything contrary to Nature had the term widder or wedder applied to it. In the following Scotch verse the word widdershins indicates a rowan tree growing the wrong way. The rowan tree was supposed to keep away witches, and espe- cially so if it were twisted eastward : I'll gar my Tommy gae down to the bow,</p>
        <p>And cut me a rock of widdershins grow, Of good rantry-tree, for to carry my tow, And a spindle of the same for twining o't. Dea-sol, or Deasoit, is the opposite of Wid- der, and means with the sun. Ido not think we have any English names from this, but the French have the name Dessolles, which is fairly common.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0253" n="art0253">
        <head>NAMES FROM PARENTAGE.</head>
        <p>NAMES FROM PARENTAGE.</p>
        <p>Almost every one of our common Christian names have given birth to a surname through first being given to the son; for example, John's son became Johnson, and so with 'Wil- liam, David, and all the rest. We have the same in the addition of the Norman Fitz (Ger- man Fritz), as in Fitz-Gerald, Fitz-Patrick. In the Celtic; Mac performs the same office, as in MacDonald, MacTavish; also the addition of 0 amongst the Irish Celts (some say this meant grandson), as in O'Connor, O'Brien, O'Neil. In the Hebrew son is denoted by Ben, hence we have Ben-ezra, Ben-Hadad, Ben- jamin, and a host of others. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0254" n="art0254">
        <head>NAMES FROM LOCALITY.</head>
        <p>NAMES FROM LOCALITY.</p>
        <p>In Scotland it used to be a common custom, not yet entirely died out; to call the farmers and the land-owners by the name of their farm or property, While family names were being adopted, no doubt many names of places were permanently appropriated. But in later time, .when everyone had a family, as well as a Christian name, they were often known and called in the district by the name of their farms. In lan Maclaren's " Bonny Briar Bush," we have examples of this, and the custom still holds good to a certain extent in the district of Perthshire, to which the story is related. The derivation of many Norman French names are found in the name of the estate. We have the proposition- de for part of the name, as Danet-de-Langrais. In Eng- land the families possessing such names have gradually dropped the preposition. In spite of the continual movement of population there</p>
        <p>In our Council we have both a Wilson and a Wilkinson. The former, of course, was the son of Will, but the latter suggests a certain Will who had .a small son called Will's kin, 1% ill-kin, and in course,of•time the son's son became Wilkinson-. In the same way w 0 pos- sibly have Tomkinson, Dickinson. Broadhurst and Smethurst both sugszest a wood, Inuit, meaning a wood. The Clough in Fairclough is from the Saxon, and means a cleft in a hill. The tei m is still largely used in the North of England as far as the Ribble. Farther north the word Gill is used, and still farther norh in Scotland it becomes Glen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0255" n="art0255">
        <p>Continued in Next Column.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0256" n="art0256">
        <head>DON'T NEGLECT A COLD.</head>
        <p>DON'T NEGLECT A COLD.</p>
        <p>Peps NUB Stop Bronchitis And Permanent Chest Trouble.</p>
        <p>This is a dangerous month. Coughs and colds come on with alarming suddeness. They are expected and being expected are usually treated "as a matter of course." The result is an increased death-rate .and the Reaper gathers in a few whose friends would have taken a lease on their lives. That shows there is a weak spot in most constitutions. The weak spot is in the throat and lungs. Therefore, never neglect the first sign of a cold. Always carry a box of Peps, and Willie• diately you feel a tickling in the throat and want to cough, or start shivering on getting indoors, put a Peps tablet in your mouth and let it gradually melt away. The medicinal fumes given off by the dissolving Peps will mingle with the air you breathe and reach every nook and corner of the breathing tubes and lungs where the danger lies. That's the way to check a cold.</p>
        <p>A cold always works its way to the throat and lungs because the air that carries the "cold-germ" is breathed in. The Peps medicine is breathed in also, and is so swift in action that it attacks and destroys the "cold-germ" before the latter can settle down to fatal mischief This explains the superiority of breatheable Peps over out-of-date lozenges and liquid physic which are swallowed into the stomach.</p>
        <p>There is no direct passage betwein the stomach and the bronchial tubes and lunge. Ordinary medicine only touches the lungs in directly. Peps reaches the lungs direct. When a Peps tablet is in the mouth every breath taken strengthens your defence against throat and chest trouble, or, if you are a bronchial sufferer, melts away those stubborn, thick plugs of phlegm that choke the breathing tubes.</p>
        <p>This novel Peps remedy is said to equal "A Pine Forest in Every Home," because it acts like the germ-destroying, lung-strengthening Alpine air of Switzerland. It costs pounds and many risks to visit Switzerland just now, but coughs, colds, bronchitis and throat trouble can be kept at bay quite safely at home by taking Peps, which are sold by chemists and Drug Stores at lin plus lAd. war-tax or 2/9 plus 3d. war-tax. It is a wise precaution in November to always keep Peps handy.</p>
        <p>HOW WE GOT OUR NAMES.—Continued.</p>
        <p>are localities, principally of a rural character, which preserve a wonderful continuity in the character of their names. In towns, however, we have got very much mixed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0257" n="art0257">
        <head>LOCAL NAMES.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NAMES.</p>
        <p>It might be of interest to -take a few of the familiar names in Warrington. Fletcher, or Fledger, was originally one who feathered arrows, just as Arrow;-smith was the man who made the iron points of arrows. Hay-ward was the one who looked after or protected the cattle of the village, Hay indicating a hedge or protection; French, Haie. In a . similar way Pounder, or Pinder, was the one who im- poundered stray cattle in the pinfold, .or pen- fold, as it is sometimes called. A few old people still remember the pinfold at Penketh- lane Ends</p>
        <p>The Bur-law man (Burleigh, see Kennil- worth), corrupted into Burley-man, was the one who assessed damage by trespass of cattle. Up to twenty years ago in our Penketh over- seers' meeting we elected annually a Burley- man, but I doubt if he ever had anything to do, the Penketh cattle and liens being specially well behaved and well looked after. The family Boteler, connected with our Grammar School, sprang from the occupation of making bottles, iflat'our modern glass bottles, but the old leather ones, sometimes called " Black Jacks."</p>
        <p>O ho! 0 ho! old Marjory doth know How oft the Black Jack to his lips cloth go</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0258" n="art0258">
        <head>WAR AND HOUSE RENTS.</head>
        <p>WAR AND HOUSE RENTS.</p>
        <p>Trades Council And The Landlords.</p>
        <p>"14)0 OFTEN SHYLOCKS."</p>
        <p>Local war-time rent increases were referred to at a meeting of . the Warrington Trades and Labour Council, held in the Socialist Ball, under the chairmanship of Mr. E. Plinston, on Tuesday evening. The Secretary (Mr. Charles Dukes) read a cir- cular from the Workers' National War Emer- gency,Committee, which called attention to the fact that landlords in many towns were raising rents, and women, whose husbands and sons are with the colours, are being pressed to pay more rent for the houses they live in... In muntion centres rents are rising, owing to the pressure for house room. "It is," contended the com- mittee, ". wrong that landlords should be allowed to use the national crisis for their own ends; it is wrong that workers -should be penalised because of their desire to help the nation at the present time. Working people already have sufficient burdens of their own to carry. Ten- ants will be helpless unless they join together to resist these fresh exactions. The committee, therefore, urges that a Tenants' Defence League should immediately be formed in every district where rents are rising and calls upon tenants to refuse to pay higher rents and to demand the establishment of Fair Vent Courts, and that the local authority, aided by national loans, shall build houses where there is a scarcity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0259" n="art0259">
        <head>Advance in Warrington.</head>
        <p>The Advance in Warrington.</p>
        <p>lii Warrington, said 11r. Dukes, rents had gone up from 61. to Is. a week, and not only that, but coercive insults were meted out by the rent collectors to the poor people who might not be able to meet their payments week by week, just as they fell due. " Here," said the speaker, "you .have your patriotic landlords; at least that is what they say they are! As a matter of fact, they are too often Shylocks going. to have their pound of flesh." It was, decided to circularise the poorer dis- tricts of the town on the matter, and to leave the Executive Committee to take such further steps as might be deemed necessary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0260" n="art0260">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0261" n="art0261">
        <head>IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>FEW WEEK-END TOPERS.</p>
        <p>Only Three Saturday " Drunks " Before</p>
        <p>The Bench.</p>
        <p>Monday's Borough .Bench haVto, deal with but three Saturday drunk and disorderly cases, an unusually small number. The. Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) occupied the chair, and adjudicating with him . were Aldermen James Evans, Bolton and Smethurst, and Messrs. Thos. Locker, W. Furness, W. Roberts, G. Pilling and-S. Wright. The first of the delinquents to step into the dock to explain why he got drunk and dis- orderly was fined 20s. the Chief Constable (Mr. Martin Nicholls) ,explaining that this was the man's second appearance before the Court within a fortnight.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0262" n="art0262">
        <head>Two Bottles Of Whisky On Him.</head>
        <p>Two Bottles Of Whisky On Him.</p>
        <p>It was alleged against the second "drunk and disorderly" that he went up to a man in Barbauld-street and asked him what he was looking at, using bad language. When arrested the drunken man was found to have two bottles of whisky in his pocket. He was stated to have no settled abode, and the Bench ordered him to pay 95., or go to gaol Or seven days. The man asked for time in which to pay, and was told that he must see the Chief Constable about that.</p>
        <p>'Fra O'er the Border!</p>
        <p>Barbauld-street was the scene of ailother drunken man's arrest. This individual was found lying on the footpath, and when he was aroused by a police officer he made use of bad language, and had to be locked up. He was the only one of the trio of toper-defendants to proffer any explana- tion to the Bench. "I am verra sorry, the noo," he said in broad Scots, "but this is the fir-r-rst time i'Aoh ever been in a Polis Coort. I had had a leetle dhrop of dhrink and as I had no sleep the nicht before it caught a-hold 'o me."—" It may be the first time he has ever been in a Police Court," commented the Chief Constable. "They call 'em Sheriff's Courts where he comes from."—A fine of 10s. was im- posed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0263" n="art0263">
        <head>SMASHED THE CROCKERY.</head>
        <p>SMASHED THE CROCKERY.</p>
        <p>liovcdy Scene In Common Lodging-</p>
        <p>House.</p>
        <p>The story of a rowdy scene in a common lodging-house was unfolded at the Borough Police Court on Tuesday morning, when John Allmark, Susannah Allmark, Catherine Stevens and Patrick O'Connor, were charged together with having .been drunk and disorderly the previous day, and with doing wilful damage to the amount of 3s, to crockery, belonging ..,to Michael Dilorenzo, the keeper bf the lodging- house in question, which is situate at 24, Dial- street. 'There was a further charge against the two wometn prisoners of assaulting Ada Dilorenzo, the wife of the lodging-house keeper. The _Chief Constable said that disgraceful scenes were caused by the prisoners at the lodging-house in question. The prisoners were all beastly drunk and they created no end of a disturbance, damaging property and having no regard for anybody or anything. " Popping Out To Tie Public-house."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Dilorenzo said that the two Al'marks and Stevens came to the. lodging-house on Saturday last, O'Connor arriving on Monday. They remained about the house during Mon- day evening, but kept popping out to the public-house. When eventually they came in for the night they started fighting, the whole four engaging in a free fight. They were in the kitchen at the time, and. they started throwing the mugs and cups about. They were persuaded to leave the place and went into Dial-court and continued to fight there. After some little time. they again came into the house and 'started, to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0264" n="art0264">
        <head>INDIGESTION</head>
        <p>INDIGESTION</p>
        <p>means Money Wasted cn Food.</p>
        <p>Those sufferers, who do not get nourish- ment from the food they eat because they</p>
        <p>cannot digest it, will appreciate Mr. Asquith'S statement that "waste on the part either of individuals, or of classes, is in these times nothing short of a national danger." Common- sense will tell you that money expended on food that you are not able to.digest is money wasted—waSted because stomach, liver and bowels, in their weakened and sluggish con- ' dition, cannot possibly extract from what you eat the nounshment you need. Be a wise economist. Spend--as tens of \thousands of people have profitably done—a .shilling or .two on Mother Seigel's Syrup, the famous stomach tonic and liver invigorator. See if your trifling expenditure doesn't provide a fine return in health and an enjoyable freedom from those handicaps to physical and mental • iitness—biliousne§s, constipation, pains after eating, and other digestive troubles.</p>
      </div>
