        <p>, throw the things about, breaking two basins, two plates, .teapot, &amp;c. Witness tried to separate the quartet, and was thrown on the floor for her pains and got a black eye. Some of, the other lodgers came to her assistance and the police were fetched and the four were taken into custody. The Battle of the Amazons.</p>
        <p>P.S. Armstrong spoke to being called to the lodgirig-house at 10.30 at night and to finding Allmark and the two female prisoners drunk and fighting in the kitchen. The other male prisOner ran upstairs as witness approached, but witness brought him down and questioned him, whereupon he admitted that he had been fighting and that he had broken some dishes about the house. The floor was covered with broken crockery and some of the stools were also broken. With the assistance of another police -officer witness, took the prisoners into custody. John Allmark admitted that he was drunk, but denied that he broke anything, alleging, on the contrary, that somebody else broke some of the articles over his head, cutting it in two • places.</p>
        <p>who study their , s' 'lreni c6th cians and Nur.. ....nnmend Marvel. sk you- Chernist to - -vet. See on, the •tes._ 7//f, . /</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0266" n="art0266">
        <head>Belated Regrets.</head>
        <p>Belated Regrets.</p>
        <p>The two women told the Bench that they were both in drink at the time and were sorry for what had occurred.</p>
        <p>O'Connor denied doing any damage. The Chief Constable testified to a record of previous convictions against the woman All- mark, and said that the quartet came into the town last week-end and behaved soidisgra,cefully that they had to be turned out of the lodging- house .to which they first went. Since they had been in the town they had practically run riot, and he hoped that the Bench would give them such a term of imprisonment as would deter them for ever coming to Warrington again..</p>
        <p>Pri6oners were each ordered a month's hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0267" n="art0267">
        <head>AGAIN SEPARATED.</head>
        <p>AGAIN SEPARATED.</p>
        <p>Husband Who Gave Wife 2s. M. To</p>
        <p>Keep House On.</p>
        <p>Before the Borough Bench on Trday mc.ra- ing, Mrs. Amelia Rawlinson, of 47, Watkin- street, sought a separation order, with alimony, against her husband, James Rawlinscm, who was stated to be a moulder.</p>
        <p>The previous week a separation order secured against the man was annulled on the ground that the 'parties had cohabited 'again, the husband alleging in the witness box that his wife went back to him of her own accord. Married 37 Years Ago.</p>
        <p>When the matter came before Tuesday's Bench, Mr. T. S. Steel appeared for the wife —as he had done on the previous occasion—and said that his client and the defendant were married 37 years ago, and in February, 1906, the wife obtained an order against her husband on the ground of desertion. Under that order the husband was to pay 13s. a week, but, as a matter of fact, for three years he had paid nothing at all,'and twice she had had to bring him before the Court on warrant, with the result that he was sent to prison. Eventually the defendant persuaded his wife to go and live with him again, and they cohabited for about twelve months, at the end of which he dis- appeared and was not heard of again for five months, when he returned to Warrington. He was at present earnino. £2 ss. a week as a moulder at Messrs.-Forshaw's.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0268" n="art0268">
        <head>Gave Her Ten Shillings and Took It Back.</head>
        <p>Gave Her Ten Shillings and Took It Back.</p>
        <p>On Saturday, September 25th, he came home and put down 10s.; the week before that he had given his wife a paltry 2s. 6d. When he gave her the 10s. the wife asked: "What's, the good of that? There are two weeks' rent owing." He then picked the. money up and said: "You'll have none at all." With that he got the coal hammer and said he Would make short work of the- sewing machine, but the wife dragged it out into the yard. Her husband slammed the door behind her and refused to let her come hack, telling her that she was not going to stay there any more and that she could go ad he would give her so much a week. Later when a letter from the wife was sent round, he threw it into the yard, unopened, and said that he was hot going to give her anything. On advice the wife took out a warrant under-the old order for the payment of alimony, and defendant was brought up and the money was paid and he -then applied for a discharge of the order on the ground that he and his wife had been living together again. Twice the wife had been to see the husband and each time he had told her that he was going to do nothing and she had better clear out.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0269" n="art0269">
        <head>An Oft-Refused Request.</head>
        <p>An Oft-Refused Request.</p>
        <p>The defendant asked the Bench many times to grant an adjournment, but despite their repeated refusals he persisted in making his application. Mrs. Barrow, the daughter of the parties, gave evidence as to the husband throwing the letter from his wife into the yard, and at the same time stating that he was going to allow his wife nothing in the shape of alimony. .The defendant contended that his wife left him and that he did not desert her. The Bench granted a separation order, with 10s. a week alimony; also ordering the husband to pay the costs and the advocate's fee:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0270" n="art0270">
        <head>MIA v. WOMAN.</head>
        <p>MIA v. WOMAN.</p>
        <p>Sequel To A Fight In Coekhedge-lane.</p>
        <p>'At the Borough Police Court on Thursday, John Harrison, of 1, James-street, and Mary Ann Mannion, of 40, James-street, were sum- moned for fighting in Cockhedge-lane, on OctOber 24th. A constable stated that he saw the two de- . fendants struggling on the ground. He under- stood that the bother was caused by either the man going with another woman, or by the woman going with another man. Harrison was fined 7s. 6d., and the case against the woman was dismissed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0271" n="art0271">
        <head>HOMELESS MUSICIAN.</head>
        <p>HOMELESS MUSICIAN.</p>
        <p>Says He Will Enlist And Bench Adjourn The Case.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Thursday morning, John Edward Lunny, a musician, of no settled abode, was chargeci with sleeping out, without visible means of subsistence. Evidence was given by a police-constable to the effect that at 11 o'clock on Wednesday night he was called to 108a1 Bridge-street, by the prisoner's wife, who complained that the pri- soner had been annoying her and she wanted him removed. He and the wife were living apart and he had taken up his quarters in an outhouse.</p>
        <p>Prisoner said that if he was given another chance he would enlist straightaway. The Bench adjourned the case for a week to give him an Opportunity of _carrying out his promise.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0272" n="art0272">
        <head>EX-SOLDIER kND Ills WIFE. Mayor Finds Modus Vivendi 'in Matrimonial Case.</head>
        <p>EX-SOLDIER kND Ills WIFE. Mayor Finds Modus Vivendi 'in Matrimonial Case.</p>
        <p>The intervention of the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock), resulted in an agreement being arrived at between a husband and wife who appeared on Friday before a Bench 'consisting of the Mayor (Alderman peter Peacock), 'Aldermen.. Bennett, Bolion, Smethurst and J. Evans; Councillor Wilkinson and Messrs, A. J. J9113', S. Wright, W. Furness and T. Locker, _ .</p>
        <p>The ease wa.s one in which Mr. T. 11. Swallow applied on behalf of Mrs. Mary Ann Barlow, of 58, Sharp-street, for a separation order against her husband, Samuel J. Barlow, now residing in Wellfield-street.</p>
        <p>Mr. Swallow' (instructed by Itr. Er. S. Steel), said that the parties had bbeti Married nine years, and there were five 'chil- dren. The defendant was an Army Reservist. and was called up on the outbreak of tht war. He Was woutiTled and had been disctarged from the Army. Since he had returned from France he had lived with the complainant's 'Mether. The whole trouble in this case was intemiier- ance of the defendant. On November 2nd he went hOme "in drink" and created a distur- bance. He said he would "clear out." He went' away and had not been back since. the suggestion of the Mayor, the defendant offered to pay his wife 25s.'á week until he could teld'a house and they could live together again.</p>
        <p>INTERMEDIATE LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>LAST SATURDAY'S RESULTS.</p>
        <p>The Intermediate League results last Satur- day were as follow : Bolton, 13 points, Sacred 'Heart, 6 points. St. Thomas', 13; Whitecross, 6. Heathside, 5; Bearnont, 0. Rylands's, 15; St. Mary's, 3. Parochial, 8; St. James', 0.</p>
        <p>HOW THE CLUBS STAND. The League Table now stands as follows:— Points P. W. L. D. F. A. P. Parochial Heathside Rylands's St. James' Bolton Silver-street St. Mary's St. Thomas'</p>
        <p>Beamont Sacred Heart Whitecross The above table does not include the Beamont v. St. Thomas' match.</p>
        <p>4:•••••••••:••••:•.:•••4.••••• •••• •••::-.:.• • • .:••:..•••••••••• • tt• • TI4E FOOTBALL FIELD.</p>
        <p>SECOND WAR-YEAR SEASON AMONG THE JUNIORS.</p>
        <p>c. g) C. C• ++++++++++ ++++++++++ ++++++++++ 4. + + + C. + + + + + + + + + +</p>
        <p>Johnson, Arrell (captain), T. Higgins, Blower, Bond" and Baguley. Reserves: Ifigham and Clarke. Heathside.—Griffiths; Lingard, Rutter, Mc- Evoy and Foster; Oxley and Varley; Dale, littrter,, Dolphin, -Booth, Spencer and Kay. Reserves : Bacon, Morriss - and Dowling.</p>
        <p>RYLANDS'S RECS. v. BOLTON SCHOOL. At Gorsey-lane:—</p>
        <p>Rylands'i Team.—T. Archer (captain); J. Molyneux, Butler, Swanton, Hill; Sloane, Lit- tlewood ; Sutton, Lee, Mulvey, Barker, Latimer, Savoury. Reserves: McCoy, Tatlock.</p>
        <p>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>At Wilderspool.</p>
        <p>Sacred Heart v. St. Mary's. Referee, Mr. G. Crozier. Kick-cif 2.30. Beamont v. Silver-street. Mr. G. Crozier. Kick-off 3.30.</p>
        <p>On Rylands's Ground. Rylands's' v. Bolton. Mr. S. Hayes. In Victoria Park.</p>
        <p>Heathside v. sWhitectross (Ground No. 1) Mr. R. Williams. Kick-off 3 p.m. Parochial v. St. Thomas' (Ground No. 3) Mr J. Cooper. Kick-off 3 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0273" n="art0273">
        <head>LAST SATURDAY'S GAMES.</head>
        <p>LAST SATURDAY'S GAMES.</p>
        <p>PAROCHIAL BEAT ST. JAMES'. Winners Play a Fine, Clean Game.</p>
        <p>This match, played at Wilderspool, was wit- nessed by a fairly large number of spectators. St. James'. who have been suffering of• late on</p>
        <p>account of injuries to players, &amp;c., were repre- sented by only a moderate team, but the Parochial were at full strength. The St. James' men made an early but vain effort. After some kicking bouts in midfield, Higham secured, and, putting in a judicious punt, followed up well. Brandwood, the Parochial full-back, misjudged the ball, and for a few minutes St. James' looked like scoring, but nothing resulted. The Parochial lads then took the game in hand and St. James' had a warm time. Green, the St. James' Custodian, played up finely, saving his side repeatedly: An un- converted try by Parochial was the only score before the interval. The Parochial deserved their three points lead, the Latchford lads being scarcely able to hold their determined rushes. On resuming, Parochial again took the offensive, their left-centre, Brown, being par- ticularly to the fore, getting through any amount of work, and making plenty of play for his wing. St. games' were playing a fine defensive game. Indeed, it is surprising that they held up the attack as long as they did, but it was apparent that sooner or later a score must come; as soon it did, Parochial putting themselves a further five points in advance. The last part of the game was spent on the St. James' line. but no further scoring took place. The final score• therefore read :—Parochial, 8" points; St. James', nil. The Parochial undoubtedly deserved their win. They played a fine, clean game, and were superior in almost ;every department. Brown, of the, Parochial, played a fine game for his side, and was without an .eoual on the field. The right-wing and one half-back were also good. For St. James', Green, the custodian, was of the greatest service to his side, whilst Heesom at half-back, and Trigham_ at left-centre were always stumbling blocks to the Church-street lads.</p>
        <p>RYLANDS'S RECS. DEFEAT ST. MARY'S.</p>
        <p>A Second Half Victory.</p>
        <p>These teams also met on the Warrington Football Ground on SatUrdily. Rylands's kicked off towards the pavilion, and Mooney returned. The game becume very sappy through over eagerness, until the Rylands's full-back mulled and alowed St. Mary's to score. At half-time the score was: St. Mary's, 3 points; Rylands's Recs., nil. In the second half the Rylands's forwards soon showed their form, Mulvey scoring a try. The goal-kick failed. St. Mary's gradually fell away and tries for Rylands's were scored by Dumbell, Barker, Sutton and Latimer, none, however, being converted. The result was : Rylands's Recs., 15 points; St. Mary's, 3 points. The outstanding Rylands's players were the pack, and Sloane and Littlewood at half-back. Both St. Mary's wing men And their full-back were conspicuous for their services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0274" n="art0274">
        <head>HEATHSIDE JUST TOO GOOD FOR BEAM ONT.</head>
        <p>HEATHSIDE JUST TOO GOOD FOR BEAM ONT.</p>
        <p>A good game was witnessed between Heath- side and Beamont in Victoria Park on Satur- day. The Heathside forwards gave an excel- lent account of themselves and it was mainly due to their strenuous work that victory rested with their side. The Beamont full-back was the pick of his team, and it was chiefly owing to his energy and tactics that the score sheet was blank when the cross-over was signalled. It was early, on in the second half that Day scored the only try of the match, for Heath- side; the minor points being added by Halter. Nothing further transpiring, when the whistle sounded Heathside were found victorious by 5 points to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0275" n="art0275">
        <head>JUNIOR MATCHES.</head>
        <p>JUNIOR MATCHES.</p>
        <p>HEATHSIDE TROUNCE BEAMONT.</p>
        <p>Played on No. 4 Ground, Victoria Park, on Saturday. In the first ten minutes a regret- table accident occurred, n Wycherly, the Beamont left wing, having his arm broken. This was a great drawback for Beamont, but they played on in determined style. At half- time the score stood :—Beamont, 4 points; Heathside, 2 points. In the second half the loss of a man began to tell on Beamont, for the five forwards had too much work to do in holding the Heathside six. Sinclair, the Beamont full-back, was also overworked, the result being that Heathside scored 17 points in this moiety. Hassan played a good game for Heathside Juniors. He had also a good deal to do with bringing about Beamont's first defeat. Result :—Heathside Juniors, 19 points; Beamont Juniors, 4 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0276" n="art0276">
        <head>SANKEY JUNIORS BEAT ORFORD BY TWO POINTS.</head>
        <p>SANKEY JUNIORS BEAT ORFORD BY TWO POINTS.</p>
        <p>A ding-dong game, in which both teams struggled fiercely from beginning to end, was the outcome of the meeting of Orford Juniors and Sankey Juniors on Saturday in Victoria Park. The final score of two—nil in favour of Sankey was a fair reflection of the game. Orford were cleverer at threequarter-back and pressed better, but in all other departments there was very little to choose between the re- spective sides. Walker and Coleman were both in superb form.</p>
        <p>Th&amp;w spectators were delighted in the first few minutes when J. Higgins beat the Sankey backs single-handed, but failed to score. Walker tried thrice more at goal, but was inaccurate. Sankey were granted a free-kick, through Walker knocking-on. Orford looked like snatching a victory, when Davies intercepted and broke away to the line, but Tibius man- aged to touch down.</p>
        <p>P. Higgins, Baguley and Farrel were the pick of the pack, who did excellent work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0277" n="art0277">
        <head>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>BEAMONT JUNIORS v. SANKEY.</p>
        <p>Beamont Junlors.—D. Sinclair; W. Downes, J. Anderton, W. Smith and t. Makin; .T. Coleman and A. Mason; H. Williams, A. Acourt, C. B. Healy, 11. Monks, S. Clare and A Hackett. neserve : G. Armitage.</p>
        <p>At Wilderspool. BEAMONT v. SILVER-STREET.</p>
        <p>Kick-off 3.30 :</p>
        <p>Beamont.—P. Hill; T. Littlewood, A. Makin, R. Whitfield (captain) and Wilson; W. Witton and W. Titchard ; T. Percival (sub-captain), H. Pemberton, S. Pemberton, F. jones, J. Higham and S. Lunt. ' Reserves: J. Nolan, A. Starkey and Hewitt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0278" n="art0278">
        <head>ORFORD v. HEATHSIDE.</head>
        <p>ORFORD v. HEATHSIDE.</p>
        <p>Orford.—Walker; J. Higgins, S. Smith, R. Bibby and G. Taker; W. Ellis and R. Munney;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0279" n="art0279">
        <head>FIXTURES WANTED.</head>
        <p>FIXTURES WANTED.</p>
        <p>Will secretaries .Tf clubs having junior teams please send lists of open dates to P. F. Ward, secretary Sf. James' 0.8 , 23, Cairo-street, War- rington?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0280" n="art0280">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>LEEDS SUFFER THEIR FIRST LOSS.</p>
        <p>The chief feature of last Saturday's games was the downfall of Leeds at Hull. This was the first game lost by Leeds this season. There were no striking features about the Lancashire matches. The afternoon's results were Swinton, 5_ points; Broughton R., 3 points.</p>
        <p>Leigh, 5; Oldham, 5. Rochdale H., 0; Wigan, 0. Runcorn, 0; St. Helens, 0. Batley, 13; Brighouse, 0. Huddersfield, 5; Bramley, 2. Hull, 24; Leeds, 11. Dewsbury, 11; Hunslet. 8. Hull Kingston R., 21; York, 5. Salford, 10; Halifax, 3.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0281" n="art0281">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>LO,YAL PADGATE,</p>
        <p>Many Enlistments End The Club's</p>
        <p>Season.</p>
        <p>On Saturday' the Padgate United F.C. were down to play their first game in the League Competition against Paddington United. Un- fortunately the 'Padgate Club found, it impos- sible to raise a team, as during the week six of their players had enlisted, and at their committee meeting the ,position of the club was discussed,-. It was ultimatAy decided that the secretary, write to the League Secretary resigning membership of the League owing to inability to raise a team to play the fixtures.</p>
        <p>The members of the Padgate Club last season 'Proved 'very loyal; for 'after completing their fixtures the majority of their members joined the Army, and th'ose of the members who were left set to work to . get a team to- gether for the present season. During the recent recruiting meetings the call to arms has proved too strong, for the greater portion of their players again, and the committee have adopted the only" course left to them, and can- celled their engagements for the season.</p>
        <p>In the League match between the Central and the Congregationalists in the Victoria Park, some keen play was witnessed. Both teams were seen to the best advantage when playing up the hill, and the Congregationalists were very near to making the scores equal on several occasions. The Central had greatly strengthened their team by the inclusion of several of the Longford United players, whilst the Congrega- tionalists included two reserve players, so it can, be said that the Congregationalists were not disgraced by being defeated by the odd goal in three.</p>
        <p>In the Warrington and District Junior League the present leaders, St. Elphin's, received a shock in their game with Howley United. They had been anticipating an easy victory" but the United lads rose to the occasion and made the " Saints " go all the way, and after a hard struggle they succeeded in gaining the point which enabled them to retain their position at the head of the chart and all matches won up to date.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Scouts appear to have re- turned to their form, and now occupy second position. Last week they accounted for Bewstky by three clear goals.</p>
        <p>Fairfield United Reserves are now advancing strongly, and every week they show improve- ment in their play, and are in hopes of im- proving their attack shortly. Last week they gained an easy victory over the Latchford Cadets, although the first portion of the game was well contested. The second lialf was all in favour of the Reserves.</p>
        <p>Bewsey Albions, by their victory over the Orford Juniors, made an advance of Wei positions: in the chart, and are now well igig front of the local rivals, Bewsey A.P.C. •</p>
        <p>To-day these teams meet on the Lovely-lane ground, and a battle royal is expected, as both teams declare they arc going to win.</p>
        <p>Howleyl•United are to be congratulated on their fine display against the " Elphins. They made a supreme effort to be the first club to lower the colours of the "Saints," but at the close of play they were behind by the only goal of the match, by which the "Saint" snatched the points.</p>
        <p>Mersey Juniors gained their first victory of the season at the expense of the Padgate C.L.B.</p>
        <p>To-day the. Latchford Cadets journey to Pad- gate to meet the C.L.B.' and they fully expect to bag a couple of points, but the " Lads " aro gbing to make a big effort to register their first win.</p>
        <p>Last Saturday the British Aluminium Foot- balrTeam made their debut in the Warrington and District League. They made the journey to Hollins Green, and a very -pleasant game re- sulted in a win for the Greenites by five clear goals: Whether it was the knowledge that there were two points at stOce in this game or lack .of confidence is not known, but the visitors did not display the same ability they had shown in -their previous friendly engagements. Thus the score put up against them came as a surprise to their supporters.</p>
        <p>The Walton F.C. had as visitors the R.A.M.C. (Chester) in their return match. For this occasion the home club was very strongly repre- sented. The visitor§. were greatly handicapped by the long grass and the uneven condition of the .surface of the ground, and this had much to do with their somewhat unexpected heavy defeat. After the. game the players retired to the Walton 'Arms, where a pleasant evening was spent, interspersed by songs and refreshments.</p>
        <p>Stevens, Fearnley and Brown; .Hughes,, Hands, O'Neil, Gilbert and Warburton.</p>
        <p>Elmwood Congregationalists :•• Roberts; Ph- nix and and-- Oxley; Clayton, llazeldine and Kend- rick; Littiewood, Gibson, Wells; bolt and Franklin. . Referee, Mr. J. Wright.</p>
        <p>The game in the opening stages was not vcry interesting, too much big kicking being indulged in. After ten minutes Hughes centred from the touch-line, and O'Neil gained possession and opened the score for the Central. Each defence had to clear its lines. The Central. who began to play a stronger gam?, attacked the visitors' goal, where a mistake by Hazeldinc let in Warburton, who halt not much difficulty in beating th: Visitors! goalkee,per. The Central continued to have the best of the ex- changes, and the visitors' goal had some narrow escapes from being captured. Half-time score. Central, 2 goals; Elmwood CongreAT.tionaLists. nil.</p>
        <p>On resuming the Congregationalists re- arranged their players, and this proved satis- factory. They had the greater portion of the game in this half. After fifteen minutes' play Littlewood centred to Wells, and he eluded both backs and Ashcroft, leaving his goal, Wells scored an easy goal for the Congregationalists. The Congregationalists' half-backs played a good game. Long shots by O'Neil and Fearnley were easily disposed of and at the opposite end the Central defenders were successful in saving their citadel from further downfall. Result: Central, 2 goals; Elmwood Congregationalists, 1 goal.</p>
        <p>HOLLINS GREEN v. BRITISH ALUMINIUM.</p>
        <p>On the ground of the former. The visitors were not fully represented and the home team had the best of the exchanges. Result : Hollins Green, 5 goals; British Aluminium P.C., nil.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON &amp; DISTRICT ASSO- CIATION JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0282" n="art0282">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CENTRAL v. ELMWOOD CONGREGATIONALISTS. The return fixture between the above clubs was played in Victoria Park on Saturday. Central: Ashcroft; Glover and Sankey;</p>
        <p>.. , . ''' ' ii . , . 1 • . , t _....A4 . 1. • ' ''''''..."::...' . ' 41,7 t, . ........--.•...........-........-. ‘- ' ' , ~ i ...-...---- ...-.... --. ----.-- .- „ . -.- .....--..-- - .. . -... , _.l' t ' . .._ • - —4.4..,,,k0r,^ , . .</p>
        <p>1</p>
        <p>-j</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0283" n="art0283">
        <head>AMENDED TABLE.</head>
        <p>AMENDED TABLE.</p>
        <p>Goals. P. W. L. D. F. A. P. St. Elphin's Stockton Heath Scouts 6 5 1 0 17 10 10 Fairfield United Res. 5 4 1 0 21 7b Bewsey Albions Howley United Orford Juniors Latchford Cadets C. 4 1 2 1 5 9 3 Bewsey A.F.C.4 1 3 0 6 13 2 Mersey Juniors Padgate C.L.8.6 0 6 0 6 50 0</p>
        <p>ST. ELPHIN'S v. HOWLEY UNITED.</p>
        <p>These teams met on the Arpley groundPfhere was a good crowd Of spectators when the game commenced. Both .teams played a very keen game, and special attention was paid to Boyle. of the "Saints," who, however, eluded their attentions and scored a good goal with a ehot just under the crossbar. The 'Unite.] made many attempts to equalise but failed. Half- time :—St. Elphin's, 1 goal; Howley, nil. In the second portion neither team relaxed their efforts and each goal was fiercely attacked, but without any result. Boyle broke away for the " Saints " and passed to Hough, the latter missing scoring. Towards the close of the game play was inclined to be rougm, and it was a relief to the " Saints " when the final whistle sounded with the result :—St. Elphin's. 1, giAl; Tfowley United, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0284" n="art0284">
        <head>PADGATE C.L.B. v. MERSEY JUNIORS.</head>
        <p>PADGATE C.L.B. v. MERSEY JUNIORS.</p>
        <p>These teams met on the ground of the former. and as neither club had gained a victory a keen game was expected. The Juniors proved too good for the Lads, and at the call of time the result was :—Mersey Juniors, 3 goals; Padgato C.L.8., 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0285" n="art0285">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH SCOUTS v. BEWSEY</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH SCOUTS v. BEWSEY</p>
        <p>A . F .C.</p>
        <p>On the ground of the former, at Higher Walton. Following even play in midfield, .13ewsey Made an attack on the Scouts' citadel, and were awarded a free-kick for hands, Ford saving well at the expense. of a corner-kick. This was ,well placed and Ford again saved well, Foster eventually clearing with a big kick, whie.h, enabled the 'Scouts to transfer flic! scerve of aetion and "gain a penalty-kick. This was entrusted to Farnell, but he placed wide cf the Posts. The Visitors' forwards gaining posses- sion, attacked the Scouts strongly. I hey gained several corner-kicks, but r.,.0u1d ria pierce the Scouts' defence. As the interval was drawing near the Scouts tried hard to capture the visi- tors' goal: Smith hit the crossbar with a bard drive, and from the rebound the ball was sent a few inches wide of the uprights. Half-time, no score.</p>
        <p>The . second half opened in favour a the Struts, and the visitors e)uld not break oway. After ten minutes' pla7 tlpPm.d the secre for the Scouts. After this reverse the visitors made desperate efforts to get on equal terms, and the home goal had some narrow escapes. Knowles on two occasions tested Ford with fine shots, and Foster was seen to advantage at full- back. Then the Scouts raised • the siege and transferred to the visitors' end. Kenwright, after being robbed twice, succeeded in notching the second goal for the .Scouts. Even play followed for a few minutes, and then the Scouts again became aggressive, and good play by Ken- wright and Solan ended in the latter adding the third goal. The Scouts had the best of the exchanges till the call of time. Result:—Stock- ton Heath Scouts, 3 goals; Bewsey, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0286" n="art0286">
        <head>FAIRFIELD UNITED RESERVES v. LATCHFORD C.C.</head>
        <p>FAIRFIELD UNITED RESERVES v. LATCHFORD C.C.</p>
        <p>On the Orford-road ground. The United Re- serves had their strongest team in the field. United Reserves: Lockhart; Cooper Tunstall; Bolton, Winstanley, Stockton; Kidd, England, Lygo, Boyle and Holden.</p>
        <p>Latchford C.C. : Massey; Jones, Daniels; Walker, Rainford, Morrison; Roberts, Daven- port, Smith, Howard and Cartwright.</p>
        <p>_ _ The gime opened very evenly, and keen play was witnessed in midfield. Then both goals were visited in turn, and the defences were 'found very safe. The Reserves, by the aid of several throws-in, attacked the Cadets' goal, and Lygo opened the score for the Reserves: The Cadets played up and bombarded the United goal. Lockhart was penalised for carrying the ball and Smith, taking the kick, steered the sphere safely into the net. The score at half-time was one goal each.</p>
        <p>The United soon showed their superiority and penned the Cadets to their own half, and after their defence had successfully dealt with several warm assaults, Kidd succeeded in scoring the second goal for the United. After this the visitors made several attempts to raid the home goal, but Cooper and Tunstall were quite equal attack, and the Cadets were soon again cal Ihr. defensive. Winstanley increased the lead with a long shot. The Cadets now appeared to be well beaten and acted on the defensive. Lygo scored again before the call of time. Re- suit: Fairfield United Reserves, 4 goals; Latch- ford C.C., 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0287" n="art0287">
        <head>BEWSEY ALBIONS v. ORFORD JUNIORS.</head>
        <p>BEWSEY ALBIONS v. ORFORD JUNIORS.</p>
        <p>On the Arpley ground. The game was very even and keenly contested, both sides attacking in turn. When the game was well advanced the Juniors attacked very strongly, and A. Oxley scored for them. The Albion defence had a very troublesome passage', the Juniors' forwards shooting very well, hitting the uprights and cross-bar. They failed to score again, and at the interval the score was: Juniors, 1 goal; Albions, nil. On resuming the Albions, with the wind be- hind them, were quickly in front of the Juniors' goal. Critchley put past the goalkeeper, but was ruled off-side. The free kick gave relief to the Juniors' defence, but the Albions wlre soon attacking again, when Critchley scored a legi! timate goal. Even play followed until the clos- ing stages, when the Albions _again became ag- gressive, and Miller gained the lead, which the .Albions retained until the call of time. Result: ' Bewsey Albions, 2 goals; Orford Juniors, 1 goal. L</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0288" n="art0288">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</p>
        <p>WALTON v. R.A.M.C. (CHESTER).</p>
        <p>This return fixture was played on Saturday on the ground of the former. The visitors were not able to put the best team in the field, while the home club were strongly represented. R.A.M.C. : Kay; Grimshaw, Logan ; Grice, Dal- lington, Whiteside; Wright, Wall, Bothwell, Barnes and Brown. .</p>
        <p>Walton: Atherton; Berry, Mason; Siddall Andrews, West; Lloyd, Cartwright, Gregson Marsh and Mortimer.</p>
        <p>Marsh set the ball rolling before a fair crowd of supporters. 'The opening exchanges were evenly contested in midfield. Then the visit- ors advanced on their left, Berry clearing very</p>
        <p>neatly, whilst at the opposite end Grimshaw and Logan kicked well. Both teams were showing good form, and the respective defences were keen at clearing. The home team opened their score after twenty minutes' play, Gregsen-doing the needful. , Shortly after Mortimer added ,a second goal. • The visitors strongly; attaCked, gaining a penalty kick. This was sent high over the bar. At the visitors' end Kay made a splendid save with a shot from Lloyd. From now to the interval the visitors had the better of the exchanges, and Barnes reduced the ar- rears. Half-time: Walton, 2 goals, R.A.M.C., 1 goal. The second portion opened very fast, and after a visit to the home goal, the visitors were quickly put on the defensive. From a long kick Cartwright headed nicely to Gregson, and the latter had not much difficulty in adding the point. For a while the visitors held their own, awl on one occasion they only missed by inches. Aft.,r this the home team had the better of the exchanges, the visitors only making a few visits to the home end. Further goals were added by %Mal Gregson and Mortimer. Result: Wal- ton, 6 goals; R.A.M.C., 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0289" n="art0289">
        <head>EMMANUEL INSTITUTE v. CAIRO-STREET</head>
        <p>EMMANUEL INSTITUTE v. CAIRO-STREET</p>
        <p>UNITARIANS.</p>
        <p>In Victoria Park. The Unitarians, who won the toss, were soon put on 'the defence, the Emmanuel forwards giving their opponents a warm time. Cairo-street seldom became dan- gerous, Knight rarely being tested. On the other hand the Caircstreet goalkeeper did well to keep the score down. Stubbs and Eaton put in several good shots but Hayes was equal, 011: each occasion. Neither side scored in the first half. .</p>
        <p>_ Shortly after resuming Emmanuel went ahead and after a misunderstanding between the Unitarian backs, Booth dashed in and scored with a splendid- shot. The game favoured the Institute, wlicx;e forwards were playing well on the backs. Emmanuel were not-long before they again scored, for Hardy secured and put in a shot, which struck underneath the crossbar and went through. The Unitarians could not get going, as Parr and Ayres were playing well. During this half Knight' only had a couple of shots to deal with, and these he made no mis- take with. Eaton put in a nice centre, from which Oakes added a third goal, and Emmanuel ran out easy winners by 3 goals to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0290" n="art0290">
        <head>TO-DAY'S MATCHES.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S MATCHES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. Paddington v. British Aluminium F.C. WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT JUNIOR</p>
        <p>- LEAGUE. Latchford Cadets v. Padgate C.L.B. Stockton _Heath Scouts v. Howley United. Bewsey A.F.C. v. Bewsey .Albions. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0291" n="art0291">
        <head>BEWSEY ALBIONS v. BEWSEY.</head>
        <p>BEWSEY ALBIONS v. BEWSEY.</p>
        <p>At Lovely-lane. Bewsey Albions: R. Darke; H. Gleare and W. Doughty; W. Rutter, A. Gibbons and R. White; W. Forster, A. Miller, A. Critehley, J. Kay and S. Dutton.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD CADETS v. PADGATE C.L.B. Latchford Cadets: W. Massey; S. Jones and R. Daniels; C. Waiker, W. Rainford and T. Ashton; F. Roberts, J. Davenport, 11. Smith (captain), E. lloward and S. Cartwright.</p>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>EMMANUEL INSTITUTE v. PENKETH ALBION.</p>
        <p>Played at Penketh. Kick-ofr3 o'clOck. Institute: T. 'Knight; E.' Crozier and S. Hughes (captain); F. Parr, E. Hardy and W. Ayres; A. Oakes, W. Booth; T. Hughes, F. Stubbs and Wm. Eaton. Team meet at Rylands-street by 2 o'clock prompt:</p>
        <p>NEW VOTE OF CREDIT.</p>
        <p>Proposed Common War Council For</p>
        <p>The Allies.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Wednesday Mr. Asquith made a statement as to ,the proposed Vote of Credit for £400,000,000. He also said that the General Staff was being strengthened, and that efforts were being made to bring about a more intimate inter-communication between the military and naval advisers of all the differ- ent Allied Powers. Indeed, he was not without hope that before long they might have some- thing in the nature of a common War Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0292" n="art0292">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKING MEN'S</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>There was not a 'great crowd of ttnglers out last week-end, as the present time lands us right between the season for maggot and the stone bait, and about this time no great catches are recorded in streams where these are the, favourite baits. Bread and pack in canals seem to be the only, baits to go off at present, and we cannot hold out much hope for these, as the Shropshire Union Canal, right away from Llan- gollen is in such a condition through falling leaves, that angling with any success seems out of the question at present</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0293" n="art0293">
        <head>GOWY.</head>
        <p>THE GOWY.</p>
        <p>Members who visited the Gowy last week, went with the intention of getting a good day's !sport, as the conditions seemed to be ideal, but it was found to be at least a foot lower than its normal height, and very clear. If it could ,have been fished about Wednesday or Thurs- day, no doubt better results would have been obtained, as it was plainly seen that there had been a fresh during the earlier part of the week. The best result was obtained by J. Sherratt, who was fishing the upper reaches. lie had eighteen roach and dace, the heaviest roach being 12oz. The others who fished up here all caught fish, and that was a better result than those who fished Mickle Trafford end. The Gowy seems to be the only spot where members ean..now go to put anything on the catch list. Other -places seem to be off:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0294" n="art0294">
        <head>VALUE OF ORGANISATION.</head>
        <p>VALUE OF ORGANISATION.</p>
        <p>There are rumours of another Society throw- ing in their lot with the Northern Anglers of Warrington. We have been asked what are the benefits of joining such an Association. Well. the proper thing to do would be to ask the delegate belonging to the Warrington section to attend one of their meetings and explain the benefits. There have been 200 enrolled around Liverpool district, through the way the Leeds and Liverpool Canal pollution case was dealt with. The money obtained \has been spent in stocking the water in standard sized fish. The. Freshwater Preservation League had the case in hand, and the way they managed affairs has been the cause of enrolling the 200 anglers, who were not an organised body, and did not pay towards the upkeep of the local waters, but a meeting was called and the F.F.P.L. pointed out the benefits of belonging to some powerful body, and the difficulties were overcome. This is only one case where a Society is better looked after by coming affiliated with an organised body. We could explain many cases, but surely the best thing to do would be to call a meet- ing, and as we have stated above, ask the N.A. delegate down, and make a night for discussing these matters. If that is done we have no doubt that there will-be an increase of membership in the N.A.A.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0295" n="art0295">
        <head>NOTICE.</head>
        <p>NOTICE.</p>
        <p>All committees are requested to 'attend the meeting at the Bay House on Monday at 7.30. A. S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0296" n="art0296">
        <head>HARVESTS.</head>
        <p>THE HARVESTS.</p>
        <p>The Board of Agriculture has just published its estimate of this year's harvest. It is stated '</p>
        <p>that the wheat crop is 1,180,000 quarters more than in 1914, an increase of about 13 per cent. This is entirely the result of increased acreage in wheat. The yield per acre is a little less than last year. The yield of barley is the lowest. on record, and is due to diminished acreage and poor yield. Oats have done well, both in tote/ quantity and in the yield per acre; there are 900,000 quarters more than last year. The pro- duction of hay is better than was expected from the dry weather in the growing time, but the later rains so helped the second crop as to bring the average to 1 cwt. per acre above the normal. Meadow hay is down CI cwt. per acre below average. The total production of both kinds of hay amounts to 6,600,000 tons, which with the exception of the very poor crop in 1911, is the smallest since 1901.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0297" n="art0297">
        <head>AMERICAN HARVEST.</head>
        <p>AMERICAN HARVEST.</p>
        <p>The harvest in America has been exceptionally good. Wheat is up 10 per cent., and is a record crop. Indian corn is up about 14 per cent. Barley is also a record, and is up 20 per cent. Cereals altogether are up 16 per cent. Cotton is a poor crop and down 28 per cent. Apples are down 15 per cent. We have not the Canadian figures, but we understand that the crops are exceptionally good. There ought therefore to be food enough and to spare, and if it were not for the high freights and other costs, considerably adding to the cost, bread and feeding stuffs would come down in price.</p>
      </div>
