      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0309" n="art0309">
        <head>LORD DERBY'S RECRUITING.</head>
        <p>LORD DERBY'S RECRUITING.</p>
        <p>On the eve of the beginning of the work under Lord Derby's recruiting scheme, four young men called upon Mr. E. A. Sanders and told him that they and several others would present themselves next day to give the scheme a send-off. They kept their word, and became soldiers of the King, as promised. We give details of the six patriots below. Another one, Tom Hardman, Greenbank-street, also presented himself for the third time, only to be rejected on account of , deficient chest measurement.</p>
        <p>operative Society's Office. He is a keen follower of football. He attended the Wycliffe Day School.</p>
        <p>Private Hewitt was employed at Messrs. Robertson's steel works at their premises at Appleton, near Widnes. For a considerable time he was a member of the Orford Cricket</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0310" n="art0310">
        <head>FIRST SIX.</head>
        <p>THE FIRST SIX.</p>
        <p>The first six local men to enlist under the Lord Derby's recruiting scheme were:— Private Ernest Brown, 36, Earle-street; Private Wilfred McKie, Birchall-street; Private Walter Hughes, Enville-street, Latch- ford; Private Fred Fairhurst, 1, Greenbank-street. Stockton Heath; Private David Hewitt, 68. Amelia-street; and Private Leonard Venahles Holbrook, 266, Manchester-road. •</p>
        <p>They "joined up" in the Army Ordnance Corps on Albnday, October 25th, the opening day of the recruiting office at 3, Horsemarket- • street.</p>
        <p>Privates McKie, Hughes and Hewitt , enlisted at the latter place, and Privates Fairhurst and Brown at the Police Station, and so prQmptlY were they dealt with, that they were fully equipped and in barracks at their destination by the 28th ult. It was here that the above photograph was taken. Private Holbrook almost immediately on reaching barracks, received instructions for abroad. So sudden was the call that he could not be photographed along with the other five, but started immediately, it is presumed, for France.</p>
        <p>Private Brown, who is 23 years of age, at- tended as a boy the Heathside School. Be was formerly employed by the Alliance Box Co.. Orford-lane. He was a member of St. Paul's Bible Class and Sunday School. Private McKie, who was employed as a clerk in Messrs. Walker's offices, King-street, is 25 years of age. He took an interest in cricket, having acted as scorer for Walker's team. As a boy he attended St. Ann's Day School. Private Hughes, who is a cousin to Private Hewitt, was employed as a clerk at the Electricity Works, Howley. He was educated at St. James' School. A brother-in-law is serving in the Army. Private Fairhurst. who is 25 years of age, was employed as a clerk in the Warrington Co-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0311" n="art0311">
        <p>Shops, Businesses, &amp;e.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0312" n="art0312">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0313" n="art0313">
        <head>FARMERS AND WAR FURS</head>
        <p>FARMERS AND WAR FURS</p>
        <p>Agricultural Jumble Sale For Allies</p>
        <p>And Red Cross.</p>
        <p>Great interest is being taken, both locally and in the surrounding agricultural districts, in the jumble sale which is to be held in the Brewery Field, :Wilderspool; next Thursday afternon. The objects of the sale will doubtlessly ensure its success. The first is to aid the Agricultural Relief of Allies' Fund. and the second to help the British Farmers" Red Cross Fund. These two funds are worthy of all support. Belgium and Poland have been smitten, and a great sum will be required to restore them to their normal agricultural life. Of the work of the Farmers' Rei Cros.; Fund, it is impossible to speak too highly. The farmers of the country have nobly responded to the appeals made on behalf cf this fund. and the money thus raised has beer spelt (.n wounde.i soldiers.</p>
        <p>The sale promises to be in en•irrnons ss. Nearly a thousaml lots ill be offPred far auction by Messrs. J. Mainwaring and co. and the Warrington and District Farmers' Auction Co., the joint bon'. auctioneers. I.•lnz•ashire and Cheshire have high reputations in the agricul- tural world, and the sale is commended to the attention of the farming community by an influential committee. Luncheons, teas and refreshments will be provided at reasonable charges, the catering being in the hands of the wives of the members of the committee, and friends. The whole of the refreshments to be dispensed have been given, so that the total proceeds therefrom will go to swell the funds. The band of the 2nd/4th South Lancs. will be in attendance. The entry list is still open, and those who have something to send, and have not sent it yet, are advised to hurry up.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0314" n="art0314">
        <head>SOLDIERS' HOME.</head>
        <p>SOLDIERS' HOME.</p>
        <p>Attractive Institute Opened A</p>
        <p>Winwick.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening a .very fine and inviting Soldiers' Home and Institute was opened at the Wesleyan ,Chapel, Winwick, by the courtesy of the trustees. Situated as it is within five min- utes' walk of one of the first institutions of its kind in the country, the Lord Derby War Hos- pital—with which is associated a detachment of the It.A.M.C. and Welsh Fusiliers, comprising considerably over 200 men—it is likely to prove a great boon to men so far removed from the town centre, especially during the wintry season. The institute is entirely undenoxninational and free to all soldiers and sailors, and its purpose and object is to provide for men who have en- listed to serve their country in the field,. in the hospital, or afloat, a place of rest, recreation and enjoyment, where they can associate with each other, get light refreshments, enjoy music and write home to kindred and friends who are far away. The opening night gave an encouraging augury of succcps, over 70 (of the men, in addition to many friends, forming a splendid gathering. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0315" n="art0315">
        <head>Corporal in the Chair.</head>
        <p>A Corporal in the Chair.</p>
        <p>Corporal Tattersall, who, along with his only son—who is now on his way to Serbia—enlisted in the service of his country, and who is a very popular public speaker in his own native town of Rochdale, occupied the chair, and outlined the project and scheme of working on behalf of the friends, and expressed hearty thanks for the generous hospitality extended. He has taken a great interest in the scheme, and been very successful in securing gifts of magazines and over a hundred volumes of books—making a splendid library—and a billiard table, for the use of the soldiers, as well as substantial gifts in the way of money, which has been given as an act of gratitude for the self-sacrificing spirit of the lads in khaki. Other offers of assistance have also been received.</p>
        <p>The gathering was also addressed by the Rev. Percy meson and Mr. Frank Hickman, as repre- sentatives of the Bold-street &amp;Mien' Home, Warrington, both of whom spoke of the noble work being done by kindred institutions, and wished the scheme every success and prosperity. Inaugural "Free an ;Easy."</p>
        <p>A sing-song followed, a most interesting pro- gramme having been arranged by Private F. Richmond, after which the evening was spent in a "free and easy" manner. Refreshments were served free to the soldiers by lady members of the congregation. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The evening closed with votes of thanks to the trustees and all friends .who had in any way contributed to the success of the gathering. The room has been throv-a open nightly.since to the men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0316" n="art0316">
        <head>CINEMA OR WIN WICK PiTIENTS.</head>
        <p>A CINEMA OR WIN WICK PiTIENTS.</p>
        <p>It was proposert to hold a " Tommies' " tea in the Parr Hall- on December 9th for the pur- pose of providing a permanent cinematograph for the ur 3' of the Lord Derby War Hospital, and comthrts for the inmates. A meeting of those interested will be held at the Town Hall next Thursday evening at 8 o'clock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0317" n="art0317">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0318" n="art0318">
        <head>TOP ROW (left to right).—Private E. Brown, Private W. McKie, Private W. Hughes. BOTTOM ROW.,---Private F. Fairhurst, Private P. Hewitt.</head>
        <p>TOP ROW (left to right).—Private E. Brown, Private W. McKie, Private W. Hughes. BOTTOM ROW.,---Private F. Fairhurst, Private P. Hewitt.</p>
        <p>MILITARY WEDDING.</p>
        <p>Gallant " Tommy " Makes Good Use Of Short Leave.</p>
        <p>The marriage of Private Percy Fletcher, of the South Wales Borderers, and Miss Agnes Free- man, daughter of the late Mr. John Freeman, of Walker-stivet, Warrington, was solemnised at the golborne-street Baptist Church, Warring- ton, on Saturday afternoon, by the Rev. H. Ogden (pastor), assisted by the Rev. D. Curtis, of Hill Cliffe Baptist Church. The bride, who was given away by her brother, Mr. George</p>
        <p>man. The service was choral, the hymns "0 Love Divine" and "The Voice that Breathed o'er Eden" being sung. Mr. Rickers, the organ- ist at the Golborne-street Baptist Church, pre- sided at the organ. There was a large congre- gation.</p>
        <p>The bridegroom, whose private address is St. Barnabas'-place, ;ovely-lane, Warrington, is well known in the district. Previous to enlist- ment he was employed by the Warrington Co-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0319" n="art0319">
        <p>ENTERTAINING "TERRIERS"</p>
        <p>WIVES.</p>
        <p>Who Will Give The Plucky Women Another Pleasant Evening? At the Drill Hall on Tuesday evening, a very enjoyable concert was Provided by Mrs. Ralp:n Johnson, asaiFted by the pupils of Miis Part- ingtou's School; Stockton Heath, aud Messrs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0320" n="art0320">
        <p>PRIVATE FLETCHER AND lIIS BRIDE. Photograph by Fred Arthur.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0321" n="art0321">
        <p>Dawson, Sankey-street, for the wives,. of the Territorials who are busily eibga:zetl iii making comforts for the men at the Front. ton a pre- vious occasion a similar entertainment was arranged by Mrs. Garnett, Mrs. Budd and Miss Longdin. The committee would be glad of further help in the way of providing occasional pleasant evenings for the brave little women who are "keeping up their end" at home whilst their gallant husbands are winning glory and renown for the old town on the shell- torn battlefields of Europe.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0322" n="art0322">
        <head>LOCAL AMUSEMENTS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>TOPICAL MUSICAL COMEDY AT THE ROYAL COURT.</p>
        <p>The lovers of musictil comedy are very num- erous in Warrington, if one might judge from the attendance at the popular Royal Court Theatre, for during the week the delightful and appropriate "Balkan Princess" has been added to the long list of successes associated with this place of amusement. One might say that the production ,this w*ek borders very closely on light opera, for unlike most musical comedies it has a plot, whin?' can be followed with attention throughout the whole of the piece. It is well constructed and pleasing, to which may he added the remark that in "The Balkan Princess" Paul Reubens, the versatile composer of its enchanting and melodious music, .is found at his best and brightest. The premier part has been awarded to Miss May Stevenson, an artiste Well known to lovers of musical comedy in our town, for does one not remember her splendid portrayal of "Princess Caprice" and Kitty Kent in "The Marriage' Market" of some few months ago? Her singing in the present production is as *fine as ever, and the hope is expressed that in the future we again may have the pleasure of listening to further of her successes. Mr. Noel Leyland's study of Duke Sergius is excellent and reminds one forcibly of that noted ladies' musical comedy favourite, Bertram Wallis, so' long associated. with "The Balkan Princess." The comedy element is in the hands of Mr. Edmund Russell and Mr. Willie Ifartell - re- spectively, the former as the quaint and droll Henri, the waiter in the Bohemian restaurant, being quite the success of the even- ing. We predict a brilliant future for this successful comedian, for the applause at the close of the performance amply testified to the good humour in which he had put the audi- ence. Mr. Willie Hartell, as Blatz, the con- federate of the bogus Prince Boris, is exceed- ingly amusing, his burglarious exploits evoking whole-hearted applause. A pleasing feature of the performance is the dancing of Miss Berenice Melford and Mr. George F. Burdett, and although one expects to witness a speciality ' dance in musical comedy, the efforts of these two classical dancers are much above the ordinary. One cannot close without a word of praise for the.admirable chorus associated with this fresh and crisp production, which, together with the augmented orchestra, go to make up one of the most successful. musical plays we have as yet witnessed.</p>
        <p>" PEG 0' MY HEART." Great London Success Due Next Week.</p>
        <p>"Peg o' My Heart," the play that has cheered and delighted all London for a year past, will be presented at the Royal Court Theatre next week by Messrs. Macdonald and Young's com- pany. Here, indeed, is a very welcome play.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0323" n="art0323">
        <p>As Lady Chichester, in "Pea o' My</p>
        <p>Heart."</p>
        <p>It is the very thing for war-time. It is a true defence of . theatre-going, for it is bright and wholesome, and it takes one's mind away from care. "Peg" is Irish—with a touch of New York about her—a devout Roman Catholic, who does not mind saying so, and who is not at all shy of pitting certain modern loose ideas, much to their disadvantage, against fundamen- tal Christian morals. She is a character so freshly drawn as to be almost a shock to the theatre-hardened among us. Mr. Manners, the author, has a swift incisive method, an ease in handling his situations, a fine sense of hurriour, and a glorious appreciation of moral truth. The Chichesters set out to make a lady of "Peg." In return she makes a woman of the daughter, who is about to ruin herself at the behest of a thorough-paced married voluptuary. The Irish-American girl sweeps aside all the foolish cobwebs of so-called "love and affinity:" She goes direct to the point, and such is the cunning of the play that we feel the castiga- tion which it administers to modern ideas, without losing for a moment the sense of fresh- ness and delight.</p>
        <p>The play is the work of an author manifestly experienced in handling and arranging, con- trasts of lights and, shades. A ripple of laugh- ter is suddenly held up by a flurry of tears, which in its turn is again arrested by an exquisite touch of humour.</p>
