        <p>MISS ENA DOUGLAS,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0324" n="art0324">
        <head>SKETCH-A(:110B VI'S AT THE HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>SKETCH-A(:110B VI'S AT THE HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>Premier place in a "bill" which is as full of good things as a sausage should be of meat is this week occupied by the Craggs, a troupe of comedy acrobats in a sketch entitled "Billy in the Ring." The Billy referred to is a yokel of the most verdant bucolic order, and he aspires to become an acrobat. He accordingly attempts to join a travelling circus which pays his village a visit, and the sketch is concerned with the fun which is extracted from him when the acrobats put him through his paces. The sketch is both funny and clever from the gymnastic point of view. George Newburn is truly billed as "the star mimic of star artistes," for seldom have we seen more faith- ful impersonations of great performers than those which he gives of .Tom Foy, George Lashwood, George Formby and Harry Tate— famous comedians whose methods are as far asunder as the Poles. Campbell and Brady get in Some real smart juggling work, their hand- ling of the Indian clubs being particularly slick. The lady member of the duo ,is also a clever dancer. James Mooney and Ida Holbein Provide a turn which, judging from the bumper reception accorded it when we were present, is entirely to tire taste of Warrington vaudeville lovers. The lady sings smart songs at the piano, the gentleman dances eccentrically and otherwise, and the pair of them unite in a tongue twister about "Sister Sophie Sewing Socks for Sailore that, in the language of the showman, "positively knocks the glittering public silly." The Maro Duo put in some ex- cellent ,triple-bar work, and Conway George tickles the risible faculties vastly with a really bright and original burlesque on grand opera. He also mimics a child singer "as to life." "Snowflakes, Gently Falling," is the title of a taking Iceland song-and-danoe scena introduced by the Sisters Gaye. The " Gaumont Graphic," with its up-to-date news-pictures goes to com- plete a sterling programme. Next Week's Big Draws.</p>
        <p>The entertainment at Macnaghten's Vaude- ville House next week promises to be a fine one. for the programme includes the famous Fred Conquest in the farcical absurdity "What a Dog," this being the star item. The Sie Tahar Troupe, in Franco-Algerian acrobatic novelties, is also a worthy addition to the show. Many other turns of note are due to appear, and will go to complete one of the best prOgrammes booked for many weeks past.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0325" n="art0325">
        <head>"STAR" FILMS AT THE 111 KINEMA.</head>
        <p>"STAR" FILMS AT THE 111 KINEMA.</p>
        <p>Two films of outstanding excellence were in- cluded in the programme at the Star Kinema in the fore part of this week. Though of a totally different character, eaCh was capable of gripping the interest of the most hardened</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0326" n="art0326">
        <p>N</p>
        <p>RAILWAY FARE ALLOWED.</p>
        <p>picture palace habitue. "A Garret in Bohemia" was a novel film, depicting the struggles of an artist and musician to keep the "wolf from the door." "The Insurrection" was a drama produced by the famous Lubin Company. The scenes depicting the bombard- ment of a town by the American Navy were magnificently conceived, and made one marvel as to how it was possible to produce such a film., "Pathe's Gazette" is again topical, several ex- cellent pictures of the King's recent visit to France being shown. The programme also con- tained two comedies—" Merry Moving Men" and "The New Butler." In the last is filmed</p>
        <p>Billy Reeves, a coming cinema comedian. • The chilf attraction for the latter part of the week is "The Dawn of a To-morrow," a subject eininently worth seeing by all true film-lovers:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0327" n="art0327">
        <head>Next Week's Premier Attractions.</head>
        <p>Next Week's Premier Attractions.</p>
        <p>Next week's big films at the popular Church- street house will be "The Exploits of Elaine" and "'As Ye Sow." Both these films are pro- duced by well-known cinematograph artistes. and picture-goers are assured of good entertain- mPnts. for independent of the star items there will he many worthy pictures of note.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0328" n="art0328">
        <head>GRE.I l' PRODUCTION.</head>
        <p>GRE.I l' PRODUCTION.</p>
        <p>" Cabiria " At The Graml Next '%Veek.</p>
        <p>The picture lovers of Warrington are to have</p>
        <p>an opportunity of witnessing one of the greatest films that has ever been preduced, " Cabiria," the latest and most stupendous picture in the film world, will be shown at the Grand Electric Theatre three times every day next week, as well as at a special matinee OD Wednesday and at two matinees on Saturday. The matinee on Wednesday has been arranged with the intention of giving the wounded soldiers an opportunity of seeing this 'great film, and they will be admitted free of char. The Saturday matinees are timed to commence at one and three o'clock respectively. " Cabiria " is a film that presents to the eye a great event of the Punic Wars. Thousands of actors take part in it, and the scenery is on a most elaborate scale. The cost of production ran into thousands of pounds, but the universal success of the film has more than justified the initial expense. It is safe to assume that every time the film is shown next week it will. brP witnessed by full "houses."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-13_WidnesExaminer_art0329" n="art0329">
        <head>THELWALL HEYS HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>THELWALL HEYS HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>Thanks To Promoters Of Successful Whist Drive.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. Shenton, secretary of the Working Cornmittee.at the Thelwall Heys Military Hos- pital. has written to thank Mr. Helsby, 19. Hazel-street, for the most generous support which the latter, and a band of workers, so willingly gave towards making a whist drive, recently held at the hospital, such a great success. " I ask you," ran the letter, "to con- vey to one and all our sincere thanks and ap- preciation of your good work in assisting to make comfortable those who have ventured their. lives for us."</p>
        <p>Printed and published by John Walker and Co. (Warrington), Limited, at the ExAmisys General Printing Works, Bewser-street, War- rington; also published by them at the EXAMINER Offices, 2, Victoria-road, Widnes; 32, George-street, St. Helens; 3, Market-street. Earlestown, all in the County of Lancaster; and at Church-street, Runcorn, in the ConniX of Chester.</p>
      </div>
