        <p>FRENCH HONOUR For A Warrington Royal Garrison Artilleryman. BOMBARDIER CHADWICK'S REWARD FOR VALOUR. Warringtonians will ba proud to learn that the Medaille Militaire, the highest military honour that the French bestow upon their heroes below commissioned rank has fallen to the lot of Bombardier Charles Henry Chad- wick, of the Royal Garrison Artillery, for con- spicuous service in France. Bombardier Chad- wick is the son of the late Mr. Istiac Richard Chadwick, 160, Liverpool-road, whose death we recently recorded, as a great loss to Sankey Bridges Wesleyanism. FAME FOR A SANKEY, BRIDGES WESLEYAN. Bombardier Chadwick, who is a fine young fellow of over six feet, was originally a plasterer, but finding his occupation somewhat monotonous for one so given to activity, he, six years agd4 joined the Artillery, and the out- break of war found him taking care of that important part of our Empire, Gibraltar. He was always interested in sport. When at home he was a keen yachtsman. and was one of the crew of the Seagull, owned by his brother, Mr.</p>
