        <p>He has a brother-in-law training with the 10th South Lancashires at Whitchurch. PRIVATE G. ROUGHLEY. Letter From Captain T. Ridgway. Captain T. Ridgway, writing to Mrs. Rough- ley, 33, Golborne-street, regarding her son, Private G. Roughley, . of the Territorials, reported last week as missing, says "On the 25th September he was not with his own company, but with the bombers as a dis- tinct -unit. I have made exhaustive inquiries and I believe your son was wounded, and if not killed on that day, he is probably a prisoner for, as you have been informed, the Germans re-occupied the trench(' that had been taken. . . . 1 knew your son well, and had a great regard for him. He was always cheery and bright, and in request at anything in the nature of a con- cert."</p>
        <p>KILLED. PRIVATE FREDK; DEAN; "Always Cheerful And Willing And Very Popular.'" The day before she received tidings that he had been killed,. Mrs. Dean, of 245, Winwick- road, Warrington, forwarded to her husband Private Fredk. Dean, a-parcel of good things., together with a melodeon and a 'mouth organ; two musical instruments .for which he had specially asked, in order that he might be able to liven up his comrades with some music dur- ing their spells of relief behind the firing; line. Next morning came a letter from a com- rade, Private Daniel Cronin, setting forth the fact that the writer had the painful duty of in- forming the young wife that her husband „was killed on October 17th. . "He was." said the writer, "next to me at the time. We had been mates all along; we both served together before we went on the Reserve." A later letter from</p>
