        <p>DIED OF WOUNDS. PRIVATE HARRY KENT. Eldest Of Four Soldier Sons Dies Of Wounds. Mr. and Mrs. H. Kent, of 42. Airyle-street, Warrington, a fine patriotic family, on Novem- ber 6th received official notification from the War Office that their eldest son, Private Harry Kent, of the Gordon Highlanders, had died of wounds received on October 3rd. Private Kent came safely out of the engagements of September ~25th and 26th respectively, but was wounded the following Sunday in the abdomen and hip. from which he contracted blood poisoning, which caused his death. "Until the Day Breaks." The Army Chaplain, writing on November 6th to his parents, states "Just a line with enclosed postcard. I</p>
        <p>thought you might like to have it, as it was the last message from the homeland he was able to any extent to grasp. We laid your dear son to rest yesterday in the British Cemetery. Both British and French soldiers took part. Two hundred of them were laid beside each other until the day breaks and the shadows flee away.' What a happy re-union that will be. I am trusting that you are keeping up for the sake of your other dear boys.. Just look up and let the great Burden Bearer strengthen your heart and bring you closer to Him-. Remember Psalm cxvi. 15." Further letters of condolence have been re- ceived from the Matron of the hospital where Private Kent passed away. A I. . and N.-W. Employe. The subject of our photograph was 27 years of age,. and before enlisting in September, 1914, was employed in the London and North-Western Railway Company's warehouse at Bank Quay. He was drafted to the Front in July. after being home on furlough in June. He attended Wycliffe</p>
